      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>LADIES' LETTER.</head>
        <p>LADIES' LETTER.</p>
        <p>MY DEAR NELLIE, With the near approach of Christmas our thoughts naturally turn to that occasion of fes- tivitieti ever welcomed by the juvenile portion of the community. But, alas! over too many British homes hovers a cloud caused by the war in the Transvaal. Many a family will be bemoaning the loss of some brave father, son or brother in that conflict, and the prospect of spending a happy Christmas is greatly discounted by the thoughts of some loved one lying dead or wounded or doing his duty to Queen and country at this season of the year. There is no doubt that there will be some parties at which fancy dresses will be in vogue. So, remembering my promise of last week, I will now give you a few suggestions for garments of this character. Little 80-Peep is always a favourite. Her dress is so very much of a muchness that it would be needless to describe it in detail. There are, however, a lot of improve- ments which might be carried out. Of course, it goes without saying that there must be a large picture-hat and, whatever you do, don't forget the staff and yellow ribbons.</p>
        <p>The representation of one's favourite land may easily be carried out. Hero is a suggestion for Erin. White dress of some rather soft material, cut in a clinging style. Large green silk sham- rock appliqu6s round the hem. Green soft silk sash draped across bodice, and caught on the right shoulder and left side of the waist with miniature harps. As a head-dress, an aigrette of shamrocks rising from a tiny silver harp. Other patriotic emblems might be represented as follows :—The British Flag may be shown b., while silk or veiling dress, the skirt kilted with the Union Jack as a draped tunic ; a smaller flag draped across the bodice and caught on the left shoulder with a gilt laurel-leaf clasp, or a wreath of artificial laurel leaves can edge the top of the low, or half-low, bodice and form epaulettes. Two tiny Union Jacks are worn in the hair.—The British Navy is repre- sented by a dress similar to that just described, but the white ensign takes the place of the Union Jack, and the under-dress should be blue. Large gilt anchors may be used Lo catch up the draperies. Another thing that might be tried is the sug- gested alliance with our kinsmen across the "Herring pond." Thus the sk:rt could be adorned with stars and stripes, whilst the Union Jack might be loosely laid above it. Bat try and work the white-headed eagle in scraehow—osy in the hair.</p>
        <p>There are any number of other daaigns that might be chosen. Britannia wears a breastplate and helmet of silver or steel, with a kilted white skirt and tunique with the Union Jack mounted as a shield, and a trident in the other hand. France has a white gown, tha skirt rather short, with a fishwife's tunic of striped red, white and blue muslin fichu knotted in front, and pointed cap of the three colours. The national Welsh costume is an effective turn-out, with the white frilled cap underneath the tall beaver hat, short blue stuff gown, with large check apron and bright red frieze cloak or fringed shawl. There are also several pretty designs for a Poppy and a Geisha girl. The Poppy's skirt is of pink silk, with green leaves and trails of poppies round, whilst one immense bloom forms the headgear. The Geisha's dress is the ordinary Japanese kimono. This may be of yellow, red and black. The um- brella av d tin fan are most assuredly required.</p>
        <p>Children's party dresses are exceptionally pretty this year. A charming frock for a girl from 11 to 14 years of age is made of ivory veiling, and has a full bodice with a falling collar of the material, vandykad and edged with three ruchings of very narrow ivory satin ribbon. This collar forms revers in front, the right side crossing over the left and disappearing under the sash of ivory satin. The skirt is bordered with a frill matching the bodice trimming, and this is carried up the front to simulate a robing, and seems to continue, as it were the revers that passes beneath the belt. The top of the bodice is filled in with a yoke of tucked ivory mousseline-de-soie, and ruffles of the same silky material are sown beneath the frills of ribbon- edged veiling that finish off the close-fitting elbow- This frock might be copied in any pale colour, and ruches of lace, or rows of baby-ribbon, could replace the satin trimmings.</p>
