      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>CHAPTER XIII.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER XIII.</p>
        <p>Bob plunged into the woods. As he was taking a cut known only to himself he heard a rustling and stopped short to listen. His nerves were all tension for he was full of suspicions without being able to tell which way to direct them. To his sur- prise he saw his father skulking along. Bob did not wish to meet his parent. "He ain't up to no good," thought the son to himself. "I must go and see mother, though, and see what she says of him." That danger passed, Bob ran on again and never stopped till he got to the post-office which Alec had visited the evening before. He told his errand.</p>
        <p>"Here are some telegrams, Bob, for Mr. Felton. And how are you, Bob? Good for nothing, your father says. But I'm glad to see that you've turned over a new leaf. I tell him work with the squire will teach you a lot." "Father ain't no good; don't you believe him," said Bob, not at all disconcerted. "Give me the telegrams." . _</p>
        <p>"-And here is one for the other gentleman that came last cvaning—Mr. Beauworth. He's a rela- tion of the squire, of course ? " Bob took the envelope without saying a word. Then he ran off to meet Mr. James, but he put the telegram for Mr. Alec into his pocket. The dog-cart was waiting at the station. The port- manteau was not there. Bob looked for it and then concluded that Mr. James had sent it off.</p>
        <p>James Felton soon came hurrying out of the station. "All right, Bob; where are the telegrams? The luggage is gone. Now jump in." Bob obeyed. His mind was resolving several things, but he was cautious. He was trying to think of a plan which would give him Alec's address. But was he gone? "Mr. Beauworth has left his umbrella, sir," said Boirpresentiv. "Shall I take it to the station to be sent off? "Has he? Well, I'll write and ask if we are to send it on to Mr. Beauworth. He didn't happen to tell you, Bob, where he was going?" "He didn't say he was going anywhere," said Bob, falling into the trap. "He went all in a hurry. He didn't even remember you, did he, Bob?" "I don't know, sir." "Not generous to slink off like that. Well, look here, I'll give you five bob when I go." "Don't want for nothing," said Bob. "Oh, don't you? You won't be wanted here after we go; Rudd will do all that is necessary." Bob did not answer, not knowing what this might mean, and James Felton presently remarked: "Did you hear from Nancy that Miss Ruby and I were going to be married, Bob?" "No," said Bob, looking stupid. "Nancy didn't tell none of us. Don't do for us to talk of the squire's affairs." "Quite right, too. But this event will make you sit up. Miss Ruby is a favourite with the people, eh? But they mustn't expect much, for the wedding will be in town. We have to sail directly after."</p>
        <p>"The squire won't like Miss Ruby's going, he thinks a lot of her," said Bob, thinking things were getting clearer to him now. Mr. Beauworth was sweet on Miss Ruby, and of course the two young men had been fighting over it. . But that his Mr. Alec had gone away without saying good-bye to him, no, he didn't believe that!</p>
        <p>When they reached home James flung Bob the reins and disappeared. The horse being cared for, Bob ran bacit to the house. He must find out all that Rr dd knew. The old man was not too much pleased at the turn of events.</p>
        <p>"Lor a mercy They say that Miss Ruby is going to marry that young gentleman all in a hurry, and Nancy declares that miss is crying her 13,e, out over it.'</p>
        <p>iss Ruby won't marry that man," said Bob. "It': t'other one, Mr. Rudd, that's she's sweet on. When did no go? There's his umbrella left in thk halt."</p>
        <p>"He wcnt last night, all in a hurry. They had words, that's what I think."</p>
        <p>"I saw him last evening and he didn't say any- thitg about it," Bob answered.</p>
        <p>"His luggage went to the station anyway. Mr. James took it."</p>
        <p>"Everythi,ng is upside down since these visitors came, and the squire's that glum as if the world was coming to an end." Bob came to a resolution. He would see Miss Ruby and ask her. It was not very easy to do this for he had no special duty to perform in her part of the house, and it was not easy to invent one. But happily Rudd was also anxious to hear a little gossip, and Nancy was as likely-as anyone to know what was going to happen. "Nancy will be wanting some more coals," said Rudd. 'Here, Bob, take some up with you and 'ask Nancy to come to the pantry as I want to see her particular on business." Bob needed no second bidding. He was off, and Iv as soon knocking at the door which cut off the passage. Nancy came in answer to his knocking. "Who is it ? " "Bob, as is bringing coals from Mr. Rudd." Nancy opened the door. "I've got orders to let no one come in or go out. That only means my poor lamb's friends." "That there Mr. James is just the old gentleman himself, Nancy," said Bob. "Don't you let him come near Miss Ruby! Where's Mr. Alec ? " "He's gone, and Miss Ruby's crying her eyes out over that."</p>
        <p>"I know all about it," said Bob, looking wise. "Just let me ask Miss Ruby what she knows." "I daren't, Bob. Whatever can the likes of you know ? "</p>
        <p>"Mr. Alec was my friend," said Bob, nodding his head.</p>
        <p>"Don't you say nothing if I let you see her. I have to get all my poor lamb's dresses ready for the wedding. Did you ever hear of a wedding 1 being done like that? It's more like making hasty pudding than anything else that I know." Ruby was sitting in the window-ledge where she was looking out of the window, despair written on her face. She started when she saw Bob's honest face.</p>
        <p>"0 Bob, lam glad to see you. Tell me where Mr. Alec has gone." "That's what I want to know, Miss Ruby. He's gone. but didn't he tell you ? " "Not a word, and he promised to help me. Oh, I know something has happened to him or he would have managed to let me know. He promised to save me-"</p>
        <p>"Don't you want to marry Mr. James, Miss Ruby? "</p>
        <p>"Want to go with him? Oh, no, Bob. You are kind, and Mr. Alec liked you. Do go and find him, and tell him I must see him. They say I must be ready to-morrow morning to go to London and that I must marry Mr. James, but I won't. "</p>
        <p>"Rush! hush! dearie," put in Nancy, who was hard at work ironing some collars against Ruby's departure. "Mr. Alec didn't tell you he was going away on the sudden, Miss Ruby; that I told him Vother day that this wasn't a place for him. Perhaps he's found it out."</p>
        <p>"He was here last night, and he must have gone very, late or very early," said Nancy. "But any- wan he's. gone," _</p>
        <p>Bob could find no clue. "Mr. Alec has enemies, Miss Ruby, and they don't want him to see you." "I am worse than a prisoner, but a Beauworth was never made to do anything that they would not do. Mamma told me so when I was a little girl." . -</p>
        <p>- "Mr. Alec won't let you do nothing you don't like," said Bob, quite understanding the situation, but the point was: Where was the man who alone could save her?</p>
        <p>"I have no one to help me. Father says that he has promised and that I must go." "Don't you take on, Miss Ruby; perhaps mother could help you." Bi" Oh, no, Bob, she couldn't help me, Father would be angry with her." "Thep I will, Miss Ruby." Bob at this moment was not a comrhon boy; :he was a knight errant, determined to save the lady who appealed for help. Nancy, who had no faith. in Bob's powers, hardly listened to the two. Now and again she repeated: "It won't be so bad, dearie, when you are his wife, and he's a well- krown young man, and that's 002Knethinz.4 ,</p>
        <p>"I'll go and thins about it, miss Ruby. When is it they say you have to go?" "Papa told Nancy that I was to be re morrow morning at nine o'clock, and that we weft all going to London. Oh, I won't got" "But you must, dearie. When you are married, why, there's ways and means of making the men better than they are by nature and you so pretty, too. Why, he'll be as proud of you as anything." Ruby was not to be consoled by these words. She was only angry with Nancy. "Be quiet, Nancy, Alec. said he would save me, and he's gone. Oh I it was only last evening that he said that. Bob, if he's gone, it means that he could not do anything, and that he went away rather than tell me so." "No, Miss Ruby, he ain't dor that. He's gone because he couldn't help self, but I shall find out wire he is, you take on. We have got to-day to get. rta,t "Bob, will you really SN Ruby, putting her hand on "1 will, Miss Ruby." Ruby wondered, but she as too much in need of help to have Little to doubt. In her heart she did not think that Bob could really help her more than by finding out where Alec was, but she was grateful to him for his evident desire to do all he could. "Thank you a thousand times, 'Bob." "Trust me, Miss Ruby, no One shan't hurt you. Nancy, give me the key of the stairs for I sleep just below." "I dursn't, Bob. No, the squire would nigh kill me." "How am I to get to you then, Miss Ruby?" He looked out of the window. There were only creepers, old ivy and the thick stem of an old wisteria. Bob took a piece of string out of his pocket, and let it down. "I'll whistle, Miss Ruby, and you can let me down the key. It won't be like Nancy giving it to me." "Thank you, very, very much, and you'll find out why Mr. Alec went away?" "I will, don't you keep on, Miss Ruby, I'll find out. Nancy, you go and talk to Mr. Rudd." Bob escaped unperceived, and ran downstairs to think out a plan. The first thing was to find out where Mr. Alec could be. "Mr. Rudd, Nancy's coming, and there's that harness that has to be mended. The squire did say I was to take it to the saddler's." "Any day'll do for that." "Best go to-day." Nancy appeared at this minute, and Rudd, want- ing a good gossip with her about this startling news, gave leave. Bob hurried to the stables, and was soon on his way to the station, after leaving the harness at its destination. The one sleepy porter who was there was a friend of his. The trains at Huckdale were few and far between, and time not precious. "Hulloa, Kemp, did the gentleman who came this morning send off any luggage? Mr. Beauworth, our visitor, left some behind him, and it was to be sent off." "There weren't no luggage sent off this morn- ing," said the porter. "The gentleman sent some telegrams, that was all." "And no one went last night by the last train?" "No, you seem mighty curious this morning, Bob Rabbitts. How's you and your father getting On?" "Awful bad," said Bob, grinning. "I'm think- ing of running away, I am, but there's mother." "Well, it ain't difficult if so be you have some money. 7 "Ain't got none," said Bob, grinning, "but I'm in service with the squire, so I must be off." As Bob walked along, he said: "That's it, Mr. Alec ain't gone away, but he's done for somehow, and if they've done for him then they'll swing for it, if my name's Bob." Bob walked home in very low spirits. As he was crossing the wood he noticed that close to a small path which waa at right angles with the road, the gras was very much trodden down. He stopped seddenly. "What's that? " he thought. He left the path and plunged into the wood. A few yards away Bob's quick eye noticed something which made him stop. "Ods Daggs I" he muttered, "someone las been burying,gomething here." Bob stooped down and saw the freshly-covered earth. He plunged a bit of stick into the ground and came upon something hard. Then kneeling down, he removed as much earth as he could. At last he reached the obstruction. Again he shovelled out the earth with his hands. "Ods Daggs 1" he exclaimed again, with a low whistle. "It's Mr. Alec's luggage." Ruby 'was anxiously watching for the return of Bob, but the morning wore away, and no one came near her till it was time for the midday meal. Everything seemed to be disorganised in the house- hold, and when the great bell sounded Ruby started up. She .heard her father's footsteps in the passage. The door opened. "Papa," she exclaimed, as she ran up to him, "have you come to tell me it is all a bad dream? Say it is not true. I cannot believe it; you do not mean that I am to leave you and go with—" "Come downstairs, Ruby. We shall be alone to-day. This is the last day I shall have you to myself." "To-day and every day, papa. I won't go with that man!" "Ruby, you must not say that. You will make it much more difficult for me. You must go." "There can be no must to a Beauworth," said Ruby, slowly. "0 papa, you do ,remember that when you were young you were told to do some- thing which you thought dishonourable, and you would not do it You said that a Beauworth would rather die than go against their conscience." "Where did you hear that?" the squire said, looking at Ruby. Then he added, quickly: "That was long ago when I was young." "I read it in those letters that I found. I don't want to be less brave than you were. I know I ought not to be James Felton's wife, because-0 papa, I hate him." "You are wrong to set your face against your duty," said the squire, not looking at Ruby. "I will do what I can for you Ruby, but you must not spoil it all by your self-will. I thought once that you would obey me blindly. I counted on that. Be reasonable. You cannot understand, but believe me when I tell you that my word of honour is given, and that I cannot alter. I would not tell you a minute sooner than I could help, because I thought that something might turn up, but now it is useless waiting longer. Say no more and let me do all I can for you, dear child." They were silent a little, and then went down- stairs together. Ruby could not eat, she shook her head and refused to try. The squire dismissed Rudd, and then be said, gently: "1 shall go with you to-morrow. You will be good for my sake, won't you, Ruby ? " Ruby could not answer. She was making up her mind that come what might, she would not become James's wife. She was ignorant, but her very ignorance was in her favour. She did not know all the dangers of the world, but she had a true instinct, and she resolved not to go against it. Alec had said that love was necessary, and she believed him. The squire took her silence for consent., and was emboldened to say more. "James and his father are now seeing about the cabin -which is to be as comfortable as possible, and you will not mind the voyage?" "You cannot live here alone without me, papa." "I must, dear, but I shall soon come and see you. I shall let the old place." "You would miss me and you would know I was wanting you, papa. Oh, you have never been unkind to me before. Do think again about it, and send the Feltons away. I will do anything else you tell me, but not that. "Hush, you hurt me, child. Eat something, you will want all your strength." Ruby tried to obey, for suddenly she thought, I shall want all my strength to fly from him. Then looking up suddenly, she asked: "Papa, why did Cousin Alec go away in such a hurry? He did not say good-bye to me." The squire was gazing beyond Ruby as if he did . not hear her words. "Papa," she repeated, "where is Cousin Alec?" g "He is gone away. You know, Ruby, that he wished to interfere with my wishes. Indeed, it was not honourable of him. He tried to make you disobey me.2' "But why did he go away so soon ' "I do not know where he is." "Did he go back to London ? " "Most likely." "Then I will go to London too," thought Ruby. "He will take care of me. If I only knew his address, or where to find him!" "Has Nancy been getting all your things ready? James will not care about fine dresses. You can get all you want out there. It will not seem strange to you, Ruby, to return to Australia. Don't you remember my coming for you ? " • "Yes, I remember your coming when mamma was deg. But now I don't want to lroyo Yelyclotic,"</p>
        <p>whispered</p>
        <p>S arm.</p>
        <p>**Though you ctict not want to come with ma. Do you remember that ? " "Yes. I cried, I remember that. If only you were coming with mat ' "You rinist be brave, Ruby. You are a Bean worth. " "And so is Cousin Alec." "You must not see him again. He did not behave honourably." Ruby said nothing. She was learning to believe only her own feelings now. The squire thought that she was giving in, He began to be more hopeful. "Go back to Nancy now, dear, and I will come and see you this evening. To-morrow we shall aui start early." Ruby flung het arms round her father's neck. "Papa, is there na way out of it at all?" "None, or don't you think, Ruby, that I would have tried all means before I would have parted with you ?" "Very well, papa. I have asked you as much as I know how to ask."</p>
        <p>.phe went sadly up the stairs, and the squire fancied that she was at least resigned. He little knew the determination which Alec had called forth.</p>
        <p>When Ruby reached her room, she remembered that she must now think out some plan of her own. Everything had failed—even Bob had not come back.. Where was he? Nancy was still busy at work, but not top busy to .go back and lock the passage door. "Is there no way out of it, dearie ? " said the old woman.</p>
        <p>"None; papa says that I must go to-morrow." "Well well, it won't be so bad. You must do all he 1(114 you, dearie ; and then marriage is no worse than you might expect." "Get my things ready, Nancy, and don't say anything more about it. I mean to be brave. I am trying to remember all that Alec told me." Ruby did not sit any more at the window now; she began gathering together all her little treasures and putting them into a box. Nancy told her that the squire had said it was no good taking too much, as Mr. James did not want to travel with</p>
        <p>tuuch luggage." It was getting dusk when there was a little stone thrown up at the window. Ruby ran to it and looked down. There were no tears on her face now, only her lips were closed as if she were making a firm resolve. She flung open the window and looked down.</p>
        <p>"It's only me, Miss Ruby. Can you get down the stairs and let me in ? " Ruby ran back to Nancy. "Look here, Nancy, it's the last day I shall be here. Give me the key of the door below." "Oh, lovey, I dursn 't. Ask me anything else." "It is only to let Bob come up this way. He can't come down the passage, or he will be seen. Give it to me." Nancy entreated, but in vain.</p>
        <p>"Giv; it to me, Nancy; you must. I shall go away to-morrow, and then you can think of your- itself. You can say I made you do it." Nancy gave in at last; Ruby was no longer the ignorant child, but a woman with a strong will. She ran down herself and opened the door. Bob put his finger on his lip.</p>
        <p>"They are there, Miss Ruby," he saidi in a whisper, pointing down the passage. They crept up the stairs, and then Ruby turned towards him. "Tell me, Bob, make haste—do you know where Mr. Alec is?"</p>
        <p>"No, Miss Ruby, but he never took his luggage, so he did not mean to go." " "Then he will come back this evening to save me?"</p>
        <p>Bob shook his head. "I don't know; I don't think he will." "But why not? He would never go and leave me without saying anything." "He's gone, but I can't tell where; and I rather think that Mr. James knows."</p>
        <p>Ruby thought a minute. "Why do you think so ? " she asked. "Because he never told you nor me, and because he did not take his things, and because he was expecting a telegram. Will you open it and read it, Miss'Ruby ? " Bob held out the telegram. Ruby shook her head.</p>
        <p>"Oh, he might not like me to." "If you don't read it, I must give it to Mr. James or the squire, and they are not partial to him."</p>
        <p>Ruby's scruples were a little shaken. "It may tell us where he is." She tore open the enevlope. It ran thus: "I will receive the young lady, bring her here. Scott, 10, Anchor-street, Kensington." A. smile spread over Ruby's face. "I understand it, Bob, and don't tell anyone about it.</p>
        <p>"I haven't, Miss :Ruby; I'm not such a fool!" "I am so glad. Now, Bob, I'll tell you every- thing. Will you help me ? " "Of course I will, Miss Ruby; the squire won't think I could help you." "But if he finds you out, he will be angry." Bob smiled. "I don't care about that. "They are going to take me to London by the nine o'clock train to-morrow. Is there one earlier, or must Igo this evening? I shall go to London, and go to a friend of Mr. Alec's. He said I was to; and if I can once get to London, no one will know this address but you—and, 0 Bob, you must not tell."</p>
        <p>"Not if they was to burn me up I " said Bob, grinning at the very idea. "I want you to come with me to the station; but Nancy must know nothing, for papa will be very angry with her, and you will be questioned, but----'</p>
        <p>"Trust me; I shan't tell nothing." "I have just enough money for my journey; but I should like you to walk with me to Dent Station. They would know me at Iluckdale." "It's a long way for you," said Bob; "but I'll go with you." "Do you know what is the earliest train, then?" "There's one at seven, Miss Ruby, but you would have to get up mighty early." , "I don't mind that. Nancy will be busy, and won't see me; and I will meet you at the little door."</p>
        <p>"Mr. James may be about, and i.e may see you." "Then I should say that I wanted to say good- bye to the woods. Oh, it is true. Bob, I could cry my eyes out, but I must not do that." "Tears won't mend nothing. be here, but I must get Rudd to send me with the carriage, and that will be better for you than walking." "Yes, there will be the big luggage to go early— I never thought of that." "I will get out a little before we get there, and no one will know who I went with, and Bob's face was full of delight. "Then you must go out alone and meet me at the turn of the park." They agreed on the programme, but Bob was not satisfied yet that Mr. Alec would be in London to receive Miss Ruby. 3"Suppose he's never been to London ? " le But I will beg or I will go out as a servant, or anything, to avoid—" Bob nodded. He did not think that Miss Ruby looked much like a servant; but if she went to a friend of Mr. Alec's, why, it would be all right.</p>
        <p>He went off to see about the luggage, but the difficulty was that the carriage was to go to the same station from which the party wits to start. "Where there's a will, there's a way," and Bob soon arranged a plan. He would go first with Miss Ruby, and then to Huckdale Station. In that way, no one would know anything about it, if luck favoured them.</p>
        <p>As for Ruby, she seemed now to be able to think for herself. She realised that for a long time she had been kept in ignorance of many things, so as to make her give in to this marriage. She had loved her father very dearly, but now she felt that it was hard to forgive him; but she made excuses for him. He was compelled, she said, over and over to herself; if he could have helped it, he "that not have done this thing. She told Nancy that she would pack her own handbag, and in this she put all that she thought would be absolutely necessary. She was going to a friend of Alec's— she trusted him entirely. Why had he gone away without telling her. She could not guess—but it must have been James's fault in some way or other. When she found him all would be right. Would her father be sorry, or only angry with her? It could not be helped. She had done all she oculd, but now she must act for her.self. The evening drew in, Nancy had done all the packing, and now sat in a low chair moaning over the parting from her darling. "I shall never see you again, dearie, never; and when you are gone, the squire won't care about us. He is hard on us. Well, I have tried to do my duty by you." "Come, Isiancy, don't cry. If it is possible, you shall come and live with me. Anyhow, I'll ask Mr. Alec to take care of you." Suddenly there was a sound of footsteps heard, and the squire walked in. "Ruby, darling," he said, softly. . Ruby wu bj his_eide. He .expep4cl her to su</p>
        <p>something. ne naa even put orr coming to see her- till it was late, as he could not bear to hear her complaints. "Ruby, are you ready for to-morrow?" "Yes, papa, I have nothing left to do." "And you will not rebel ? " Ruby was silent. _ "Look here, darling, if you knew all, yo 6 would' not blame me—you would be sorry. You would try to make it less hard for me. "Then- tell me all, papa. I want to know—l want to understand."</p>
        <p>The squire held Ruby at arm's length. He saw before him the girl he had taken care of ever since she had been a tiny child. He had watched her growing up by his side; he had seen all her sweet ways, her love of all living things. He had felt her arms round his neck every morning and even- ing; She had twined herself round his heart and he loved her. Now he was going to part from her. How could he do so? He would never have felt it pang at sacrificing anyone but her. He had been a hard master. No one loved him but Ruby, and he was going to sacrifice her. A dim wish came over him to bear everything but this, but the wish was too vague to act upon; at least, the other side of the picture was too impossible to realise or to face. He could but try and soften the blow. Even now he almost thought that he must insist that he should accompany her. Would dames be kind to her? Certainly he would not ill-treat her, but as to caring for her he had not even made a pretence of that, thinking her too innocent to be taken into account.</p>
        <p>"Ruby," he said, looking into her eyes, "will you forgive me?" "Papa, you can't help it, you said so. I love you too much to say I won't forgive you, but—" "If I could—but it is too late. If I had known long ago, then it would have been different." "If you had• known what, papa?" "That I should love you as I do now." "Didn't you always love me ? " "Come, Ruby, we must not talk, I want to tell you a few things which you must remember. After my death—</p>
        <p>."Papa, don't tell me anything. I don't want to know anything." "You must, child. You know that Alec Beau. worth is the heir to this property?" "Yes, I know, and he was good to me. 0 papa, why couldn't it be as he wished?" "Because it was not possible. When he is dead you will be the owner of the place." "I don't want anything if he dies." "You will have this property, and your husband will have to take care of it."</p>
        <p>"Papa, where is Cousin Alec?" "I do not know. I toltl you so before, Ruby." "I shall write to him and tell him that I do not wish him to give me this property." 'You will do nothing of the kind; besides, it would make no difference. Now you must go to bed and sleep, and to-morrow I shall go with you. We are to travel alone. James and his father will go by an earlier train. • "An earlier train ? " said Ruby, feeling her colour forsaking her cheeks. "Yes, I thought you would rather have me with you alone for the last time."</p>
        <p>Ruby felt that the ground was being cut away from under her.</p>
        <p>"If Igo with you why must we go so early? I shall not be ready." . "It will make very little difference. I will see." "Good-night, papa, and good-bye." "Not good-bye yet.", "Yes, I shall never be your Ruby again. How can you let me go ? " The squire turned away. "Nancy," he called out, and the old woman followed him out -of the room.</p>
        <p>"Listen, Nancy, I won't have Miss Ruby seen by anyone this evening. Let. her go to bed at once, and remember I am trusting you." "The poor child! She don't take a fancy to the young man, sir." "If you speak like that, I shall turn your daughter out of her cottage." Nancy said no more, for the squire's face was hard and cruel.</p>
        <p>Ruby did not go to bed or to sleep. She dare not risk missing Bob in the early morning, and then she would have to tell him of this new danger. She saw Nancy go to her room, then she locked the door upon her, so that even if she woke she should not be able to get out, and it would seem as if it were not her fault that Ruby had escaped. Daylight came slowly, then at last, when there was only a faint glimmer, Ruby heard the pebbles against her window. She opened it softly and looked out. The faithful Bob was below. "Miss Ruby, arc you ready? it's no use if yon can't Come now at once."</p>
        <p>Ruby had secured the key. In another minute she was in the passage below. Then she listened! All was quiet. She opened the door and found Bob.</p>
        <p>"Mr. Felton is to go by the early train!" "But at the other station, not at Dent, so if you come at once we can catch the Dent train. If you go into the third-class carriage he won't see you." It was a bold game, but it was the only one possible. She gave herself no time to think, no time to do anything but hurry. They got to the station at last, but Bob could not see her off. Ruby was only anxious to be let alone. She had put on a thick veil, and she was not known by the porter. The train came, and she hurried into the last carriage. Perhaps James would not really come by this train. She crouched in a corner when the train stopped at the next station. She saw no one on the platform. It did not wait long, the whistle sounded; but there was a shout, and the engine- driver slowed again. Two men were running into the station. The train pulled up, and the door of a first-class carriage was flung open. Ruby looked out for one moment. It was James with his father.</p>
        <p>(To be confinued.</p>
      </div>
