      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <p>(Copyright.)</p>
        <p>A CHRISTMAS ADVENTURE</p>
        <p>By GRANT ALLEN,</p>
        <p>Author of "The Woman Who Did," "This Mortal Coil," "The Tents of Sham," "Miss Cayley's Adventures," &amp;b.</p>
        <p>"You will come to spend Christmas with us, 'Lancelot ? " Mrs. Pendleton said in her fussy way, in the drawing-room of the Somervile Club. "Of •course you'll spend Christmas with us. Why, Roma wouldn't think it as Christmas if you weren't of the party. Now, mind," with uplifted forefinger, "we shall expect you."</p>
        <p>"Lancelot doesn't need such a lot of persuasion, mamma," Roma put in with a quiet smile, and a glance of those arch eyes. "I think you may leave it to his own inclination with perfect safety." "Now that's kind of you, Roma," Lancelot answered. "You supply me with, ready-made pretty things, which I'm so bad at saying. Yes, Mrs. Pendleton, to tell you the truth I had no idea of spending Christmas anywhere else than at the Shieling. If you hadn't asked me I think I should have been bold enough to propose myself." "That's right," Mrs. Pendleton said, smoothing her dress as she spoke. Mrs. Pendieton's dress had never the ghost of a crease in it. "Of course you ought to come. It's quite right that you should come. In Roma's position and yours, when young people are engaged--" "My dear mother, if there's one thing on earth I will not endure," Roma cried, hastily, for other people were listening with amused faces, "it is to be called a young person." Mrs. Pendleton stared at her in blank astonish- ment. "I'm sure I don't know why," she inter- posed, in her bland, unimpressive voice. Mrs. Pendleton was a bore of the first water; one of those people whose tongues run on for hours at a time in a dreamy monotone, and who never, by any chance, tell you anything. "You are young, and you are a person ; by Act of Parliament you are a person. I know that's so, because Mr. Armitage told me that women voters are included in the words any person or persons' ; and he ought to know, because he's, a solicitor. And if being young and being a person—legally a person—doesn't make you a young person, I should like to know what's the good of speaking the English language with propriety and correctness, as they say in the grammar books? However, that's neither here nor there. What I was going to say when you inter- rupted me with your objection to being called a young person was thin: When young people, or middle-aged people, or old people, or people in general, irrespective of age, are engaged to one another, it seems to me only right and fitting that they should make a point of spending Christmas together, unless, of course, serious family affairs prevent them. In this case Lancelot's father and mother being now in India, M3erious family affairs 'do not prevent him. Therefore, I say, Lancelot ought to come to us." And having delivered herself of this profound piece of sus- tained reasoning without a single break, Mrs. Pendleton looked about her, beaming, with the sense of having accomplished a social duty. "Lancelot is only too glad to come, mother," Roma put in in a lower voice, and with a touch of discomfort at her mother's diffuseness. "Ha is quite at home with us." "Oh, we shall make no stranger of you, Lancelot," Mrs. Pendleton continued, leaning back in her easy-chair and folding her hands in front of her.</p>
        <p>"Yes, we shall," Roma interposed, cutting short another harangue before it could get well under weigh. "For my own part, mother, I hate to be told I'm to be made no stranger of. I know it means they will put me in the worst attic bed- room, and give me cold mutton for dinner, with a nursery pudding. I like to be made a stranger of —treated as if I were Queen Elizabeth on a pro- gress round the kingdom. And as far as I'm concerned that's how I mean to•treat you, Lancey." The young tnan bowed. "It will be quite good enough, he answered, smiling. "I ask nothing more than a royal reception." "Then we may expect you on Friday? Friday's Christmas Eve," Mrs. Pendleton went on, musing. "You'll come on Friday." "Why not Thursday, mamma? " Roma said quickly. "Lancelot is coming down from Oxford on Thursday, and he might as well come direct to us."</p>
        <p>"My dear child, how forgetful you are. Don't you remember that Thursday is the day for turning out the spare bedroom—the blue spare bedroom— which Lancelot must sleep in; and as Lancelot will get to us early, I hope, that would be very inconvenient. No, it must be Friday, Lancelot," so good-bye till then. We must be getting to Waterloo now, and you will want to be running back soon to Oxford. Friday then, Friday! Roma, dear, where's my satchel? What, come with us to Waterloo? " Oh, if you like, certainly. When young people are engaged it's very natural they should wish— This is the way down, is it? I really don't know my way about the club yet. You see, it's Roma's club, not mine. In my day they had none of these men-and-women's clubs, they would not have been thought quite nice; and I really don't know whether, even now, I approve of them or not, though Roma drags me to them. When I was young we should never have dreamt of going anywhere in London without a chaperon. But that's all altered now. Four-wheeler— Waterloo! Mind the step, Roma. So kind of you to see us off, Lancelot. I like to find this politeness in a young man. Young men nowadays for the most part—" The noise of Piccadilly drowned the remainder of Mrs. Pendleton's facile threnody on the degeneracy of the young man in the present generation. Lancelot bade them good-bye at Waterloo, and waved his hand to Roma, whose bright face, smiling at him as the train steamed out of the station, was the last thing he saw of it. Then he beckoned a hansom, jumped in, and drove off to Paddington, repeating to himself many times as he went that of all the sweetest, dearest, cleverest, prettiest, and most lovable girls that were efer born, Roma Pendleton-- I spare you the rest; we have all been there.</p>
        <p>Lancelot ran down on Thursday from Oxford to Marbury, the nearest station on the Great Western line to the Pendletons' home, end decided to sleep the night there, since he might not go on at once to the Shialing. Next morning he would start early, he said, sending his luggage round by rail, and would bicycle himself across the twenty miles of upland moor-like country that lay between Marbury and the Pendletons' district. That was pleasanter and quicker than taking the intricate cross-country railway journey, and it would bring him sooner to the house if he started at once after an early breakfast. So he pencilled a hasty note to Roma, telling her when to expect him. Then he went off to bed at the little inn, and was soon dreaming that he had taken a First in Greats, and had been called to the Bar with unusual distinction. Next morning was a lovely, frosty December one —the very morning for cycling. The roads were hard as iron and in splendid condition. Hoar- frost covered the ground and sparkled in long sprays of diamond-work on the larches by the roadside; but the sun shone clear in a cloudless blue sky, and the birds were vocal in the holly bushes and hawthorns. Lancelot rode along joyously with youth and love in his blood. He had a new machine—the latest design, with all the modern improvements; and his wheels ran round iip-hill, with the bearings immersed in the constant oil- bath, as if the slope were a level. A north-east wind blew behind him and helped him on his way merrily. Oh, the joy of that level upland expanse! It was one of those days when one thanks Heaven one is alive, and when Schopenhatier doesn't seem to have a leg to stand upon. Who could be pessimistic with a sky like that, and on such a road—above all, with Roma Pendleton at its end to welcome him?</p>
        <p>We are all youns, once, and the time for pessimism is not the day before Christmas. Oftener, indeed, it seems to be the day after. There is nothing like cycling through open Country on a bright winter's morning to give a man a sense of .utter exhilaration. When your thoughts are pleasant ones too, I am not quite sure that the exhilaration is not all the greater if one finds oneself alone. The sense of speed, of the changing landscape, of the distance traversed, of the mastery over space is more concentrated, I fancy, when one rides by one's-self, with only the birds and the son for companions. Anyhow; Lancelot Saville rode up the hill that leads out of Marhury in an ecstasy of health and vigour, singing aloud as he pedalled, and as. glad as the larks in the air above him. The highway over the downs from Marbury to South Barton is one of the loneliest and wildeei in southern England. After mounting for a mil or two through fields and farnas it reaches at,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <p>open moorland expanse on the top, black with ling in winter, and continues for fifteen miles at one break between unenclosed stretches of gorse and heather. Only a single inn disturbs the glorious solitude of the endless heath—a little way- side inn, the Jolly Postboys, frequented by carriers and by the few tourists whom the hilliness of the upper road does not deter from this the most picturesque way of reaching South Barton. Most people prefer the longer route in the valley by Pilsbury and Great Stanton. So Lancelot rode gaily on, scarcely meeting a soul after he reached the upland. The frost had rendered the surface as hard as stone, and he made good time over the level, almost without being aware that he was working.</p>
        <p>Aboui. four miles fom South Barton, a tempting footpath diverged to the left across the blackened moor—a grassy footpath through high furze and heather, which looked as if it must cut off a long &amp;Misr of the high road. Lancelot did not know this short-cut before, for he had been little in the neighbourhood, and when he was there, his time was more occupied in walking about the grounds of the Shieling with Roma than in distant excur- sions. However, he had an ordnance map in his • pocket, and dismounting from his machine, he opened it and consulted it for a moment by the roadside. The path must lead direct into the valley—no doubt by a steep hill, where one would have to wheel one's bicycle; but it certainly cut off a corner, and every minute was precious—when one was going to Roma. So he decided to try it. Mounting gaily once - more, he turned off along the grassy path, a mere track through the heather, so overgrown in parts that the prickles of the gorse tore his legs through his stockings and knicker- bockers as he pedalled. In softer weather, it would have been an impossible place, indeed, for cycling, but frost equalises most things. He rode on, little heeding, and with his brain full of schemes for Roma and the future.</p>
        <p>Suddenly, as he rode, he was aware all at once of a slight jerk, then a sense of danger. Next instant, he found himself sprawling on the frozen ground, with bleeding face and hands—a very miserable picture. It always takes a little time to realise the full gravity of an accident. Lancelot's first thought was merely that this was a pretty plight in which to present himself to Roma. He took out his handkerchief and wiped his face; he knew then that he had cut his nose badly, and that his forehead was streaming. He looked around and began to wonder how it had all happened. How had he managed to stumble? His machine lay beside him, somewhat entangled in his own, leg, and buckled and battered out of recognition. A nice new machine, too, utterly spoilt and ruined! That was his first thought. Then he raised him- self on his arm and inspected the ground, still half-dazed and stunned. He saw now what was the matter. A treacherous little pool had lain in his way—stagnant water in a hollow worn by feet in the path; and this pool had frozen lightly over, with a skim of ice, He must have half crossed it without observing it, when the hind wheel went through; then the tyre gave a side-slip on the soft mud of the bottom. It was easy enough to see how the accident had happened; the next thing was to consider how he could extricate himself from it.</p>
        <p>"I shall have to walk the rest of the way," he said to himself, as he surveyed his bent and broken spokes, "wheeling my machine ignominiously. If there's anything I hate, it's entering a town with a smashed bicycle, and affording innocent amuse- ment to all the children of the place as an acknow- ledged duffer! " As yet he had not even attempted to rise. But after a minute or two of lying still and nursing his knees with his torn hands, Lancelot recovered sufficiently from the first effects of his fall to pick himself up and begin to think about moving for- ward. To his great surprise, as he tried to get up, he realised for the first time how serious was his position. A dart of pain shot through 114 s leg when he endeavoured to stand. He sank back and waited; then made another effort. Ha! what pain! .__</p>
        <p>A hideous wrench! The whole truth dawned upon him. This was.no slight accident. He had broken his right leg! A compound fracture! Up to now he had been rejoicing in the sense of solitude. The realisation of his misfortune made him wish he had had a companion. Though, to be sure, he had reached that point of trouble when a judicious frieiad would naturally have deemed it prudent to desert him. He raised himself on his arm once more, and shouted for help. But he might as well have shouted for water in the desert. 'He was a mile and a half away from the high road in either direction. Even the upper road itself was little frequented in summer, and almost abandoned during the winter months; as for the footpath he had followed, a day or two might elapse before anybody crossed it. In a flash, the real danger of the situation came home to him. He might die of cold and hunger before a soul discovered him.</p>
        <p>After a while, he listened, and to his intense delight, heard a curious thumping sound in the valley below him. It went thud, thud, thud, quite regularly like a steam-engine. A ray of hope broke over him. Where there is an engine there must be men to work, it. He put his hand to his mouth,. trumpet-wise, and gave a loud halloa in the direc- tion of the sound. His voice rebounded from the opposite hill beyond the valley, but nobody answered. He shouted till he was hoarse. He could not make the men at the engine-house hear him.</p>
        <p>"The noise of the machinery must drown my voice," he thought to himself. "There's nothing else for it but I must crawl down to them Comehow."</p>
        <p>He began to crawl down the hill, dragging his maimed leg after him. The pain was intense every (1.0117 and then, as he gave it a sudden wrench; but there was no help for it. He must drag himself there. at all hazards. He trailed himself along on his hands and one knee through the high-grown heather. Fortunately, the slope was nowhere very steep, and he could hear the thud, thud, thud of the engine growing nearer and nearer as he descended into the valley. It had the unmistak- able regularity and periodicity of human machinery. He heard it now, up, down; up, down; up, down; up, down again! Only one thing disquieted him— there was no smoke or steam visible. But perhaps the chimney was hidden by bushes! He crawled on in agonising pain, each step costinc, him hard, but bringing him nearer and nearer to that unvary- ing, imperturbable engine. • Through a break in the copse that clad the •Atom of the valley, he could see the engine- house now. A cold chill came over him. It was alarmingly small, a low domed structure, more :ike the little Mohammedan tombs he had seen the wear he wintered in Algeria, than an English factory. And what could an engine be doing in this wild valley, untraversed even by a road? A saw-mill, perhaps! Yet even a saw-mill implies a cart-track to carry away the planks by. Still, the regular thud, thud, thud continued plain and unmistakable. It was a machine, without a doubt, and surely wherever a machine is working a man or men must be there to Work it.</p>
        <p>Half-dead with pain and exhaustion, he dragged himself over a boggy level that stretched between himself and the little engine-house, and lay in front of it, face to face with it. His breath came and went. The machine inside ran thud, thud, thud with heart-sickening regularity, but there was no one to tend it</p>
        <p>It was a hydraulic ram, set up to supply'water to a great house on the hill-top, half a mile distant.</p>
        <p>Lancelot had never seen a hydraulic ram before, and knew nothing about it. But he took in at a glance the main facts of the situation. The machine is worked by a small jet of falling water from a spring, and once started, it goes on of itself for months and months, unless anything chances to put the mechanism out of order. Raising himself on his hand, he unfastened the rus :. hasp, opened the door, and crawled in. Then he understood instantly. The little rise and fell of the valve, the pipe that carried the water to the higher level, both told their own tale. He recognised now that he was worse off than before. His hearing had misled him. He had gone off a beaten track into a sequestered glen, where no man paised. There was very little chance, as things stood, that anybody would discover him! Faint with pain, he squatted down in the bare damp but and leant his back against the wall. The place swam before him. In a minute he half- swooned, half fell asleep from exhaustion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>111.</head>
        <p>111.</p>
        <p>About one o'clock Roma began to get anxious. Lunch-time came, and still no Lancelot. Mrs. Pendleton, with her knitting on her lap, was fum- ing. "Young men nowadays have really no sense of politeness! They get on these new-fanged machines of theirs, and ride across country, with something they call 'punctures' delaying them mysteriously, and if they happen to reach you in time for lunch, well and good; if they don't, they burst in upon you with an easy smile, and bo sorry; a puncture:- In my young days, their elders would have taken care to teach them polite- ness. And that nice cream of yours spoilt and . cheese fondu reads to go downt I call it</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
