      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>EXAMINER FRIDAY DECEMBER 22, 1899.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER FRIDAY DECEMBER 22, 1899.</p>
        <p>unpardonable! However, I suppose we must begin by ourselves. It's nearly two, Roma! If Lancelot comes late, he had nobody to blame but himself and his punctures. Punctures, indeed! punctures! I'd puncture them if I had the chance. I'd get rid of them altogether, nasty, noiseless things, creeping upon one unawares at the rate of sixteen miles an hour, and frightening one out of one's seven senses with their bells and their lamps. Parliament ought to interfere. That's my idea about it. "</p>
        <p>.Roma did not attempt to combat the idea in question, nor to point out that sixteen miles an hour was rapid creeping; she was growing too alarmed. She sat down to lunch with a very troubled face and not a little dissatisfaction. That lovely cream that she had taken such pains with! And thOse delicious new lobstery things she had learnt at the School of cookery! It was a triumph of a lunch; just to think that Lancey should not have turned up to eat it! After lunch she ran out herself, and sent a telegram to the one inn at Marbury to ask whether the young gentleman from Oxford who slept there last night had set out for South Barton on a bicycle this morning; and, if so, whether he had taken the upper or the lower road. ."And I will pay the answer," she said to the toys zly-haired young woman who took it in at the office. The towzly-haired young woman looked amused, after her kind, and affected an air of immense superiority to such mere human weaknesses. In less than an hour the answer came back: "Gentleman started about nine by the upper road. Should have arrived long ago." Roma ordered out the pony-carriage immediately, and set out with Mrs. Pendleton to search for him in a fever of apprehension. They drove for some miles, and then met a boy herding sheep among the low grass that intersperses the heather. Interro- gated as to bicyclists, the boy scratched his head and answered dubiously, after many questions, that he'd seen one of them there things pass by this morning, it might be about eleven, and that it turned off the main road by the Long Common footpath. Roma, now wild with alarm, let her mother drive home the pony, and followed the track herself with the boy and their servant. A mile and a half from the road they came suddenly across a broken and battered bicycle. Roma looked at it in horror. "It's his! " she oried. "It's his! I can see his initials on it, L. C. S. All broken and bent! And nobody near it!"</p>
        <p>The mystery was profound. But they never thought of going down the hill to look. Nobody ever went down the hill, indeed. It was a wild valley on Mr. Burbury's estate;, and Mr. liurbury was well known as the most cantankerous landlord in all the district, and the most set against tres- passers. They continued on by the footpath instead, thinking that Lancelot must have been severely hurt—so severely that he did not even care to wheel away his mangled machine; but that he had, limped off towards the town, and might even by this time have reached the Shieling. As they neared the town, they inquired of every- body on the slope where the roads united, but no one had seen a disabled bicyclist. Roma returned home in terror, but found no tidings. Then' she hurried out once more on a vague hunt on the hillside, accompanied by one or two volunteer searchers from South Barton.</p>
        <p>inner surrace or the loose side covered with the same material as the table-cover, in which case it will, when dropped out, lie or-nly on the latter without attracting any observation. Another plan, and nct a bad one, is to have the reverse of the flap covered with blotting-paper, bearing a few ink marks, and to bring in with the slate a few sheets of paper, of similar size and appearance, which are used ostensibly to dry the slate after cleaning, and are afterwards left on the table, the false flap falling upon them.</p>
        <p>A third plan is to use two slates, placing the one upon the other when you desire to obtain a spirit message. The lower slate has no prepara- tion. The upper, which has tho false side, is laid upon the other, and the false side drops within the frame of this latter, where it attracts no notice, while the message appears upon the under side of the upper slate. Of course, such a slate as above described cannot be handed round for examination beforehand, as the false side would fall out, and "give away" the trick. To obviate this drawback, I designed for my own use a special slate, which could be freely handled with the false side in position. One side of the frame (represented, in section, at the right hand of Fig. 1) is made with a deep square groove, a, throughout its length. Along this groove lies easily a loose tongue or fillet of. wood, b, in thickness about three times the thick- ness of the silicated cardboard. This slip of wood is pressed by a couple of small springs behind it, lying in the space a (but not shewn in the figure), towards the opposite end of the slate. Its opposite edge has a narrower groove down the centre to receive one end of the permanent writing slab, or slate proper, c, which is securely attached to it, and moves with it, the space behind the tongue allow- ing a play of about 1-16 in. The opposite end of the frame is as shewn at the left hand of the figure, but in this case c is not a fixture in its groove, but moves easily within it. A false flap, d, is now inserted on the under side of the slate. Whefl in position, it is clipped by the pressure of the springs between the tongue above-mentioned and the opposite side of the frame, and so held securely. To release it, the performer holds the slate by the "tongue " end, with the flap to be released undermost, as in Fig. 2. By pinching both the true and false surface between the finger and thumb, and drawing them towards him, the lower extremity of the false side is drawn clear of the frame, and being thus freed, it falls out.</p>
        <p>of producing spirit-writing, DM in tnis case a little wore dexterity is necessary, and it will therefore appeal more especially to those of my readers who have outgrown the reproach of being no conjurer." The writing may be an answer to either of the questions above suggested, but. in this case it is made to appear not on a slate, but on a card, enclosed in an envelope in conjunction with a minute fragment of lead pencil. The requirements for the feat are a pack of playing-cards (ordinary or "forcing," according to the skill of the operator), a pack of blank cards (playing-card size), a packet of ordinary envelopes, and a couple of pencils sharpened to an extra long point, so that a fragment may be broken off when necessary. Unknown to the audience, the top card has, written in pencil on its under side, the "spirits' " reply to the intended question, say, "King of Clubs,' supposing that the performer intends to "force " that card. Advanc- ing to the spectators and casually shewing the pack of blank cards, he invites some person to draw one. This he takes back in his right hand, and, having shewn unmistakably that it has no mark on either side, "changes " it, by the sleight familiar to every conjurer, for the top card, the one marked "King of Clubs."</p>
        <p>Here, again, he may use the sleight already de- scribed in connection with the slate, and she* both sides guiltless of writing. In the case of the card, however, a minute variation is necessary. The card should be held upright, between the first joints of the first and second fingers, with the thumb in, front, as in Fig. 7. This latter, when the hand is dropped, gives the card the impulsion which causes it to make a complete revolution, the card being left after the movement between the first finger and thumb. The card, thus ptoved free from writing, he places, with a fragment of the lead pencil, in an envelope chosen by a second spectator, and fastens down the flap.</p>
        <p>• 4:P r</p>
        <p>_,. . err , 111 . , ~,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>-w-- IV.</head>
        <p>-w-- IV.</p>
        <p>It was about four o'clock before Lancelot really recovered consciousness. He lay still in the hut by the hydraulic ram, just aware that his leg was very stiff and painful. Gradually he came to himself. He understood now the serious nature of the situation. Unless he could in some way call attention to his whereabouts, he might starve of cold and hunger where he lay. It had been hard enough to crawl down the hill with his broken leg; to crawl up it again would, be simply impossible. He must set his wits to work at once for some means of communicating his peril to the rest of the world elsewhere.</p>
        <p>Suddenly a flash of inspiration came over him. The ram! The ram! It must supply water to a house somewhere. If he stopped the supply he might induce the owners to come down and look after the working of the machine. Could he stop it, that was the question? He examined it care- fully, with his numb, cold fingers, and saw that by the aid of a knife and a pocket-handkerchief it might be possible to check the little bob of the valve from its ceaseless movement. Happily, he had both in his..pocket. The struggle for dear life turned him automatically into a mechanician. He examined the thing closely. In two minutes he knew more about hydraulic rams than if he had made a dozen of them.</p>
        <p>He jammed the valve with his handkerchief. For a minute or two it made abortive attempts to go on; then it stopped entirely. Lancelot watched it for some seconds. At the end of that time he was satisfied he had succeeded. Would the people at the house or works which it supplied with water notice the interruption—that was now the point at issue. If it was a factory, he thought to himself, the chance was small, or a factory would have stopped work by now, and would not recommence till after Boxing Day. If it were a house, the betting was better, but even so it was far from a certainty:</p>
        <p>Hour after hour he waited. He lit his few remaining matches sparingly at intervals to look at his watch. Towards seven he began to despair. Then suddenly, a noise! Voices and footsteps approaching the little building! "Can't have frozen," one voice said—a rough masterful voice. . "The, stream's running free. Something must have jammed it. Those plagues of boys again! I must get a new lock. They will play with the thing whenever they find the door ope.n ". _ _</p>
        <p>n" Most likely, sir," a second voice answered respectfully, "They do play tricks. Good thing I happened to notice it so ,soon, or there'd have been no baths for the ladies and gentlemen to-morrow morning:" _ _</p>
        <p>The -door opened, and the two men entered. The master, carrying a lantern, looked down at the floor in surprise. Why, what's this ?" he cried in a gruff tone, turning Lancelot half over with his foot. "A tramp, Ido declare! What do you mean, fellow, by trying to sleep in my outhouse ? " Lancelot gave a quick scream. "Take care," he cried, wincing. "My leg is broken. You hurt me horribly. I have had an accident." Mr. Burbury regarded him with calm satisfac- tion. "Oh, broken your leg, have you ?" he said. "That's well! That's all right. A very good thing too, if you all broke your legs. Poaching my pheasants—that's what you've been after, and jolly glad I am you've got your deserts at last for it. Why, Greenfield, look at this! The fellow has actually gone and stopped my ram from working. Stopped its ram! What do you mean by this, fellow? Do you know that you've interfered with a gentleman's water supply?" "I did it to call your attention," Lancelot answered, taken aback by the man's brutality. "I tell you, my leg's broken. I had an accident on the path above—fell off my bicycle badly, and I crawled down here, thinking it was an engine, from the sound, and then I saw my only way was to rouse your house. I must be carried up carefully." Mr. Burbury was busying himself removing the obstruction. "A pretty thing!" he said, in the intervals of unjamming the valve, "if tramps like you are to go sticking handkerchiefs in a gentle- man's hydraulic ram! A pretty thing, certainly! Greenfield, remove all this stuff, and throw it where the man can't get at it again. Then come back with me at once. This is disgraceful, disgraceful! " "You don't mean to say," Lancelot cried, thoroughly alarmed and horrified, "you're going to leave me here to die of hunger!" "You're a trespasser," Mr. Burhury replied in a curt voice, regarding him as if he were an octopus. • "It's your business to get out the same way you came here. No affair of mine. But I &amp;ball inform the police, and if they choose to rtanove you, seeing that your leg's broken, of ccurse that's emir matter. I can't have tramps stopping up my water." "But I'm not a tramp," Lancelot cried, playing his last card. "My name is Lancelot Saville. I'm ar Oxford man, engaged to Miss Pendleton. And I was going there to-day to spend my Christmas." This announcement produced a certain visible effect: the landlord was mollified. "Oh, very well, then, Greenfield," he went on, after perpend- jug a minute. "If this person's a gentleman, that alters the case slightly—though he's trespass- ing—he's trespassing! I must go back now, or I alien be late for dinner. I can't be late for dinner. But you can stay here with this person, if you choose, and I'll send George for the police. You're a trespasser, Mr. Saville ; you have no rights, remember; but Mrs. Pendleton's a neigh- bour a mine, and I don't want to be unneigh- baurly. I'll do what I can. I'll have an Ambulance cot sent for you. it. wao a stretch of humanity unheard of in Mr. Bit rbu ry. RI, after all, Lancelot Saville did spend his Christmas broken leg and all with Roma Pladlatons _ _ _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>(Copyright. ,</head>
        <p>(Copyright. ,</p>
        <p>CONJURING FOR CHRISTMAS.</p>
        <p>BY PROFESSOR HOFFMANN, Author of "Modern Magic," &amp;c.</p>
        <p>GLENDOWER : "I can call spirits from the Tasty deep." HOTSPUR : "Why, so can I, or so can any man. But will they come when you do call them?"</p>
        <p>-SIIAICSPERE, "HENRY IV."</p>
        <p>One of the most striking developments of the magic art is certfinly spirit-writing. It cannot be called a novelty, for it is as old as Babylon the Great; witness the hand that wrote on the wall on the day that Belshazzar was slain. Even at the close of the nineteenth century, however, writing is the most Up-to-date mode of spirit communica- tion. Spelling by taps and tilts has been found too slow a method, and nowadays, if anyone wishes to communicate with his grandmother's spook, he does not, as of old, consult the kitchen table, but provides a slate and pencil, and, ' given the assistance of a competent medium, the old lady will write him a more or less intelligible message. If the inquirer is curious to know how she does it, we may refer him to a little book: "Slate-writing and Kindred Phenomena" (written by a clever American conjurer, W. E. Robinson, and pub- lished in England by Messrs. Sampson Low, Marston and Co.), where he will find a score of ways fully described. In the present paper I have no intention of poaching on Mr. Robinson's preserves, or of expos- ing the frauds of Mr. Sludge. I propose merely to explain one or two methods whereby the reader may himself produce "spirit messages" for the amusement of his friends, and that not in "dark- ness visible," or under the convenient cover of a table, but in the open, and in full gaslight.</p>
        <p>For such a feat to produce the maximum of effect it should combine two elements—first, the appearance of writing where there was no writing a moment before; and, second, the revelation by means of such writing of some fact of which the performer can have no knowledge, as, for instance, the total of numbers secretly written down by different members of the audience.</p>
        <p>Aft effective mist-en-scene for the illusion is as follows: The performer hands out three blank cards, the size of an ordinary visiting card, and invites three persons each to write on one of -them a number of three figures. This dolne, each card is doubled in half, and to prevent the performer baying even a chance of peeping at the figures the three are collected in a small square envelope, such as •is used by tradesmen for wrapping "change." This is handed to a fourth person, who is invited to add up the four numbers, and to note their total on the back of the envelope, which he retains and puts in his pocket. For greater secrecy as to the numbers written, the three cards are then set on fire, and allowed to burn to ashes on a plate. Meanwhile, the per- former exhibits a slate, which he cleans with a damp sponge, showing both sides free from marks of any kind. Laying this flat on the table, he invites the person who added up the numbers to come and join hands with him on the slate, the spirits being invited to do the same sum on their own account, and give their version of the total on the under side.</p>
        <p>z r i .. . -.. , . f zi),,,, ~. . • : . ex% 4r17 / , e .1 • -r: r a a 1 ....., FIG. 1. .</p>
        <p>_//</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>FIG. 2.</head>
        <p>FIG. 2.</p>
        <p>After a few moments the sound of writing is heard. Presently it ceases; the slate is turned over, and on its under side is found a number, say, 1494. On comparing this with the number on the envelope it is found that the "spirits " are correct in their arithmetic. The two numbers are the same.</p>
        <p>But the feat is not yet concluded. The performer, offering the slate to some person who has not yet assisted, asks him to rub out two figures of the total just obtained, the first two, second two, or last two, at his pleasure. Again the slate is laid upon the table, the side with the remaining figures uppermost, and hands are again joined upon it. The performer -invitee the spirits to re-write, on the under aide, the two figures which have been expunged from the upper side. Again the sound of writing is heard, and when the slate is again turned up the two expunged figures are found re- written on its lower surface.</p>
        <p>First, how does the conjurer know what numbers will bc written down? As a matter of fact, he does not know them, nor does he need to do so, seeing that those numbers are never added up at all. He has three cards with numbers of his own, say, 632, 711, and 151 (all different as to hand- writing, size of figures, and blackness of pencil) folded and placed in just such another envelope as the one in which the genuine numbers are after- wards collected. When this has been done, and the envelope closed, he holds out his right hand to receive it, the duplicate, meanwhile, being con- misled in his left hand. "Who will add up these three numbers for me? "he asks. As he says this he, with a perfectly natural gesture, transfers the envelope (apparently) from the right hand to the left, in so doing "changing" it for the envelope containing his own figures, the total of which he knows to be 1494. The subsequent burning of the three (substitute) cards not only makes the feat appear more magical, but prevents any inconvenient question arising (though this is very unlikely) as to the numbers written.</p>
        <p>---- A ) ON II ' ) ' ,O</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>FIG. 3.</head>
        <p>FIG. 3.</p>
        <p>Bat how is the number, 1494, made to appear on the slate? The simplest method of effecting this is to use a trick slate—i.e., a slate with a false sido lying over one of the true sides, but capable of being dropped out at pleasure, when the true side is revealed, with anything that may have been pre- viously written thereon. The writing surface is usually not of actual slate, but of cardboard covered with black silicate paint, such as is com- monly used for "book" or memorandum slates. The false side is of the same material, and in the ordinary make of trick slate simply lies within the frame, which it fits easily, dropping out of its own accord when the slate is turned with that side undermost.</p>
        <p>A conjurer giving a regular show would get rid of the false side, when neadful, by dropping it on the servante behind his table, but this plan is not always available, and in such case some expedient becomes, necessary for enabling the performer to drop out the false side on the surface of the table without attracting notice. One plan is to haTe the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>FIG. 4.</head>
        <p>FIG. 4.</p>
        <p>For the benefit of the amateur wizard, I have handed a model of the slate in question to Messrs. Hamley, 229, High Holborn, from whom it will henceforth be obtainable. Whichever kind of slate be used, the required total is written on the inner side, concealed by the false flap. The sound of writing is simulated by scratching the slate with the little-finger nail of the hand that is undermost.</p>
        <p>Of course the false side is not released until the slate has been duly cleaned, and shewn free from writing. It is, however, quite possible, with a little address, to chew (apparently) both sides of the slate clean, even after the false side has been removed, and the writing consequently exposed. To do this, the performer should in the first place chew the slate held upright in the left hand, as in Fig. 3, the clean side towards the audience. Then, grasping it with the right hand, fingers behind and thumb in front, as in Fig. 4, he should rapidly lower it L..° the position shewn in Fig. 5, apparently sliewing the opposite side. In reality, however, as he lowers it he also gives it a vertical half-turn with the fingers and thumb. The effect of the combined movement is that the slate describes a complete revolution, and the side now shewn is therefore the same which the spectators saw at first. The illusion is perfect, all being persuaded that they have seen both sides.</p>
        <p>Experts will recognise the principle of this sleight as being "as old as the hills," but its application to the slate trick is, I believe, wholly my own. We have still to explain how the expunged figures re-appear on the opposite side of the slate. To produce these a little additional piece of apparatus is necessary, taking the form, in my own working, of a boxwood ring, as shewn in Fig. 6, fin. wide, just large enough in diameter to fit comfortably over the tip of the thumb, and bear- ing, by way of "stone," a small piece of French chalk, standing up about tin. French chalk, it may be mentioned, writes on slate (or any like surface) exactly as a slate pencil would do, but makes no sound. This is placed in the right vest pocket of the performer. While shewing the total just produced to the front row of spectators, the slate being held upright between the hands—fingers in front, thumb behind—it is an easy matter to thrust the right thumb into the pocket, slip its tip into the ring, and with the projecting point of chalk write on the surface the number 14. Someone is now asked, as we have said, to rub out any two consecutive figures. If he rubs out the first two, 14, the spirits are requested to re- write these figures on the opposite side. But he may rub out the second two, 49, or the last two, 94. What is the performer to do then? In such case he has only to vary the form of his request. Instead of asking the spirits to re-write the expunged figures, he invites them to re-write the figures which have been, left standing. The effect on the audience is the same, for the one feat is as surprising as the other.</p>
        <p>FIG. 5.</p>
        <p>~. .....) , vow. `,.: . 4C)-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>FIG. 6.</head>
        <p>FIG. 6.</p>
        <p>The reader should here note, by the way, that to make his own figures meet either the second or the third alternative the second and fourth digits. of the original total must be alike—in this case both 4.</p>
        <p>It will be obvious that the plan last above described, for writing a number or name under the very eyes of the audience, has no necessary connection with the use of a trick slate, but is available with any slate, and may be used as an independent trick. Neither is the primary effect limited to the addition of numbers. There are plenty of ways in which the "spirits " may get credit for supernatural knowledge. •For instance, if the performer has any knowledge of card conjuring it will be an easy matter for him to force a card, and get the spirits to write its name for him. Or, if he lacks the skill to force a card, he may get the same effect, to the uninitiated, by the use of a "forcing" pack, in which all the cards are alike; in which case the name of the card drawn is, of course, a foregone conclusion. Or, again, he may have a card freely drawn, but ask the drawer (professedly to prevent the possibility of thought-reading) to put it without looking at it in a "changing card-box" (procurable at any conjuring depot), previously "loaded" with the card whose name he proposes to reveal. Or, again, be may ask someone to arrange a oat of dominoes in proper domino fashion, when the spirits will reveal the numbers at the two ends of the line. To do this, all that is needful is that he should secretly withdraw one domino (not a double) which will give him the dellired answer. If, for instance, he al:stracts the 6-3, 6 and 3 will be the final numbers of the completed line.</p>
        <p>I will conclude with another method (original)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>FIG. 7.</head>
        <p>FIG. 7.</p>
        <p>The trick Is now done. The corresponding card is forced, and the writing on the supposed blank card is exhibited at leisure. The performer may, however, greatly heighten the effect of illusion by having a second blank card chosen and placed with another fragment of pencil in a second envelope. This envelope we will call No. 2, the first, containing the written card, being No. 1. No. 1 he takes in his left hand, No. 2 in his right. Both are held horizontally, face downwards, but with a difference—No. 1 being held between the first finger and thumb, and No. 2 between the first and second fingers. "Here," he remarks, "we have two blank cards, each carefully enclosed in an envelope. On which of them would you like the spirits reply to appear ? " It is an even chance that No. 1 is chosen, in which case he has only, after a due interval, to open the envelope, and shew the mystic writing. Should the choice fall on No. 2, he says, turning to some person on his left: "Then this envelope (handing him, appar- ently, No. 1) will not be needed. Open it, please, and see that there is ' no deception '—that the cards are, as I have said, free from mark or lare- paration." But as he turns to offer the envelope his hands have momentarily come together and again parted (the first half of the movement being made by the right hand, and the second by the left). At the moment of meeting No. 1 has been nipped between the first finger and thumb of the right hand, and No. 2 between the first finger and thumb of the left hand. By the time the turn of the body is complete, the envelopes have changed hands, and it is No. 2 which is handed for examination, No. 1 remaining in the right hand for tho purpose of the trick.</p>
        <p>The above change, neatly executed, is quite invisible, it is practically the same as the wizard has already employed for changing the drawn card for the one on top of the pack, but its use for changing two!cards apart from the pack, or, as in this case, two envelopes, is, so far as I am aware, an entire novelty. It will be found to enhance in an extraordinary degree the magic of the trick, which, skilfully performed, should suffice to estab- lish for the revler a high reputation as a drawigs- room conjurer,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>FILE-SMITHS' STRIKE.</head>
        <p>THE FILE-SMITHS' STRIKE.</p>
        <p>A writer on labour questions in the " Sunday Chronicle" makes the rollowing clmment upon the file-cutters' strike :—Some three months ago I made a few remarks on the strike of the Warraigton file-smiths and file-cutters fcr an advance of wages. From some circulars which have been sent to mo, and a visit which has been paid me by come of the officials, I regret to find that the dispute is still urs?ttled, and that it has been going on now for nearly six m-inths. The society is a very old one, and thirty or forty ye,rs ago was one of the best organized in the kingdom. 1 irieht possibly be considered as such now if there IC been no progress made in the science of r niattion since that time. What was good organizitio !hen ia almost disorganized labour now, but unfortui. 'y to use a common expression, the file-smiths and file-t utters have not progressed with the times. It is, however, no use flinging this at the men when they are in a hole, aud whatever may be thought about their tactics their pluck must be admired when it is remembered that they had been fighting for nearly six months, larrly on what they could pick up. There are a good share of 2,000 members rf the trade in Sheffield, and it is not to their credit that they have only subscribed some £5O to the relief of their brethren in Warrington. Now that the file-cutters at Warrington have made such a splendid stand in order to secure weekly earnings of about half the amount that a good many workmen would cill living wage I should like so hear that the Lancashire trades, at any rate, are doing something towards help- ing them.</p>
        <p>Presentation at Howley Mills.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday evening, Mr. John .Na.lor, rit Beeston Tower, on behalf of the joiners at Howley Mills,pre• sented Mr. W.H. Pearce (foreman of the joinery de- partment) with a marble timepiece and ornaments to match, on the occasion ot his marriage. The timepiece bore a silver plate, on which was in- scribed :—" Presented to W. H. Pearce by the joiners of Howley Mills, Warrington, on the occa- sion of his marriage, December, 1899." Mr. Naylor expressed the hope that Mr. Peal ce would be loog spared to spend a happy and prosperous nigrrieu life. Mr. Pearce responded, and thanked Mr.Nhylor for his kind words, and also the employs for the beautiful presents. He said he should always look upon them with pleasure, and they would remind him of their great kindnesr.</p>
      </div>
