        <p>PATRIOTIC CONCERT,.</p>
        <p>Enthusiastic. Audience at the Conservative Club.</p>
        <p>The concert which had been arranged by Mr. Percy Randall, in aid of the Mayor's tund for the relief of the widows and orphans of the soldiers and sailors who may lo3e their lives in tho war, and which was held in the Assembly Room at the Conservative Club, on Monday evening. as a patriotic one in every sense of the word. The audience was an enthusiastic one, and from the commere.ement of the prpgramme to the finish, there , was an air of patriotism running through the whole pro- ceedings. Every item was calculated to stir up the patriotism of the audience, and perhaps never before in Warrington have such a largo number of patrio:ic songs teen sung at a single concert. The hall presented a gay appearance, being decorated with national flags, the Union Jack and Stars and Stripes predominating. On the platform a number of plants were arranged, which ' had been lent for the occasion by the Corparation. The concert was under the patronage of the Mayer and Mayoress, Colonel Spratt and the officers of the 8:13 and 40% Regimental Districts, and Colonel J. C. Ridgway and the officers of the Volunteer Battalion. The pro- ceedings commenced with the singing of "Rule Britannia "by Mr L Auckland, the chorus being taken up by the large audience. A selection by the Band of the Bth and 40th Regimental District; should have fol- lowed, but Mr. Randall announced that the band cou:d not attend as half of it had been called up to the front. Instead Mr. Randall gave a selee ion of patriotic airs on the pianoforte in cap.tal style, including "The absent minded beggar," "Private Tommy Atkins" "Hearts of Oak," "Seldiers of the Queen," and "The Girl I left be- hind me," for which he aces loudly applauded. Miss Dorothy Trustlove is a promising young vocalist, and for her excellent interpretation of " The Minstrel Boy" she was vigerotisly encored, sing- ing in response " How I got my Uncle John." Later in the evening she feelingly tendered the song '• Daddy" f3r which she was also recalled, singing " Rothesay Bay " in response. Mr. L. Auckland sang " Thy sen- tinel am I "with great success, but was heard to best advantage in "The boys of the old Brigade "in the chorus of which the audience heartily joined. There was an outburst of applause when Mill 3 Kate Beesley re- cited Kipling's " Absent minded beggar." Miss Beesley is an accomplished elccuticniet and the poem appeared much etroneer and more thematic as she recited it, than when seen in print. Her test effort, however, was un- doubtedly her thrilling recital of lennysan's " Charge of the Light Brigade "the charge being blown by four bu- glers. In reply to an encore she gave Hamilton's "Or- dered to the front." Daring the evening, a collection in tambourines was made on behalf of tee object of the concert and the appeal to WPay, pay, pay "realised the handsome sum of £4 Os 7d. Mr. E. Pearce very credit- ably sang Leslie Stuart's popular song " Soldiers of the Queen" the audience joining in the chorus with much spirit. In response to a recall be repeated the last verse and later in the evening sang "The sons of -freedom ' for which be was alto cneered singing in response " Jack's the boy." " Let me like a soldier fall" and " The King's Own" were sung with excellent effect by Mr: J. Parkinson. who gained the hearty plaudits of the audience, as did Miss A. Bate, who sang with much taste" The Battle Prayer" and "The dear Homeland." Bugler Ince played the concertina solos " The Moun- taineer and" Cock o' th North." ihe inspiring tune of the latter "caught ore" with tbe audence, and Bugler Ince was compelle Ito repeat the solo. Special I praise is due to the Orpheus quartette who sang " Com- ' rades in Arms " and "Tom the Piper's son " in admir- able style, being recalled for en!). Mr. Randall accent- panied throughout with his usual skill. Mr. RANDALL, in the course of a few brief remarks expressed his gratification at seeing suce a good ieiponss to his appeal on behalf of tbe widows and orphans of the soldiers and sailors. it seemed to him that the audience thought as he did, that the cause was a deserving one. He thanked them for their preseree and support, and also thanked the Mayor and Mayor( se Colonel Spratt and officers of the vs% and 40-h Regimental District, and Colonel Ridgway and officers of the Volunteer Corps for their kind patronage, and the led es and gentlemen who had kindly come for- ward and helped him with the prograrome.—(Applause) —especially Miss Bosley, who ter )ugia her recitation of the " eeekent-minded beggar " had been the means of adding to th- fund, the handiorne sum of £4 Os 7d. (Applause.) He must also thatk the committee of the club and Mr Fleeb-ook for placing the use of the room and the piano at his disposal free of charge.—(Applause4 —and Mr. Ralph Johnson for dreerating the platform, the Town C'erk for the,lo in of the plants, and the prin- ttrs and the Piles who had helptd him half way in the expenses of the concert. The proceedings were brought to a close with the singing of the National Anthem, Mr. J. Parkinson, take. log the solo.</p>
      </div>
