      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <p>CHRISTMAS finds us in a Christmas Greet- ings. condition which does not promise well for the usual festivities. Hearts are heavy, many families are in mourning, and our thoughts an not attuned to the sentiments which usually prevail. But the season retains all its custo- mary features, and it will De our duty to put as cheerful a face upon everything as circumstances permit. Households unaffected by the war will be re-united, and once more enjoy that sense of family unity which neither tile sneers of rationalists nor the so-called disintegrating influences of the time, have seriously effected, We have been told that the influence of the family is declining. Christmas is a yearly refutation of the assertion, and liberal holidays and compre- hensive railway facilities Make family gatherings and influence more marked and subtle instead of being less prominent and general. Trade scatters the members of a family, but Christmas brings them together again. There may be vacant chairs and sorrowful memories, but they are a part of our common lot, and they have their influence in softening charade'. and preserving our most noble emotions from the erosions of trade and money-making. At the present moment it is more necessary than ever that our family life should be cultivated. A nation is only a collection of families, and where family feeling is strong so will national feeling be. It is also important to escape for a time from the desolating influence of bitter controversies and war-alarms. Christmas will enable us to do so, and to enjoy the more simple scenes and domestic sentiments. It will</p>
        <p>11.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <p>recall to us the old message of "Peace on earth goodwill towards men," of which we more than ever stand in need. If we were not occasionally reminded that this is the highest aspiration and the most noble ideal, we might forget that the world hangs together, and that commerce, travel, social intercourse, and literary cultivation flourish best when the ziations are at peace, and men are not shedding each other's blood on the battlefield. It was the late Lord DERBY who said that "the greatest of British interests was the interest of peace," and all men of the Liberal creed ani of Liberal sentiments aim at establishing good- will, between masters and men, family and family and the nations of the earth. Escape from the ideal we associate with Christmas is irapossible.We have to come back to it as a necessity of the time. There is especial need of this goodwill just now, because we are in danger of forgetting the poor and the needy, the suffering and the helpless, in a burst of patriotic feeling for direct sufferers by war. Around us, wherever we may live, are deserving objects of our tender egard. Let us remember them, and make Christ- mas a time of ease and comfort for them. A merry Christmas is not simply one of eating and drinking. It is one of brotherly and sisterly help, of emotions finding expression in practical deeds. "The ancestor of every action is a thought," it has been said. If we think wisely we shall act well. Our readers have our best wishes for a merry Christmas in the sense which is suited to them best. We hope they may enjoy the season, and find it profitable as well as pleasant, a relief from business, a time of reunion, an opportunity for exercising the sweet deeds of love and charity May they all enjoy, in some way or other, "A Merry Christmas and a Happy New Year."</p>
        <p>THE military situation in The Military Situation. South Africa is a very anxious one, and the authorities at home, as well as the generals in the field, do not conceal their apprehensions respecting it. From the first we have been compelled to follow and re- sist the Boer plan of eampaign, instead of develop- ing a plan of our own. Sir W. BUTLER is said to have sent home before his withdrawal a plan of operations which has been the governing idea of our actions in the field, modified by events over which we had only a partial control. He recom- mended that we should abandon as incapable of effective defence the whole of Natal north of the Tugela river. We did not carry out this scheme because we desired to utilise the time at our disposal to obtain as much coal as could be got from the Dundee coal fields, not only for the large towns in N atal, but for the use of the railways and the navy. Lady- smith was stored with war materials and pro- visions, and it held an important rather than a strong position on the line of rail leading into the Orange Free State by Harrismith. The advance 1 by Bloemfontein was to have been the main attack, but there were not men available to make it in the first instance, and the invasion came as a surprise. The advance by the Boers towards IVlaf eking and into Bechuanaland was another disturbance to the original scheme, and subsequent events have shown how cleverly it protected the Orange Free State, and what peril Kimberley was placed in by the free movement which our non-invasion of the Orange territory gave to the united forces. It is true that neither Ladysmith nor Alafeking and Kimberley have fallen, but they are in distinct peril, and our efforts to relieve the first and the third have com- pelled us to carry on the war at very great disad- vantage. No doubt Sir G. WHITE was instructed to remain at Ladysmith, or he might have retired on the Tugela river in safety soon after General YULE'S retreat from Dundee enabled him to do so. Stores of many kinds would have had to be sacri- ficed, but the military advantages of a retreat to the Tugela and the blowing up of the railway bridge at Colenso and the Buiwer bridge over the high road would have been considerable. Looking at events as they have since shaped themselves, and in spite of a very brilliant defence by Sir G. WHITE, we can see that this course would have been best. But we were under the impression that we could withstand the Boors,that they would not have been able to bring such long-range artillery into the field, and that we could readily hold Ladysmith and relieve it when we were ready. Ladysmith can hold out for another six weeks. It is doubtful however, whether General Buller can relieve it until he has been reinforced by men and guns, though General WHITE may be able, by concerted movements, to retreat instead of capitulating, should the worst come to the worst. No such hope can be held about Kimberley, where the invested force is small though the relieving army is about the same dis- tance away as BULLER'S force is from Ladysmith. Lord METHUEN is strengthening his position on the Modder, and there are no serious indications that his railway communications are in peril, if immunity cannot be long hoped for in this respect. South of the Orange State, the situation is likely to improve as more troops arrive. Sir CHAELgS WARREN'S division may go to Lord METHITEN's aid, the Indian troops may go to General BULLER'S. General KELLY-KENNY'S division is likely to operate as soon as it arrives in the north of Cape Colony. For three or four weeks longer Mafeking may hold out, and Colonel PLUMER may relieve it from the north. Suspense and anxiety must therefore, prevail for another month, and by that time, if the Boers do not become menacing, a new complexion may be put on affairs.</p>
        <p>It is with no little thankfulness we read in a contemporary the true explanation of Sir William Butler's recall from the Cape. We have never been able to sympathise with the attacks upon this distinguished soldier, first, because recrimina- tion and malice never repaired a blunder yet; secondly, because, in our opinion, the attacks were not in the least degree well founded. Sir William Butler disagreed with the Milner policy, and as soon as he knew what that policy was he offered his resignation, so that all possiblitv of friction might be avoidedrancl then loyally placed on record what he thought the plan of the campaign should be if the Milner policy ended in war. He suggested that Natal should be abandoned as far as the Tagela,where a defensive force of 30,000 men waste be posted. The main army, of not less than 100,000 men, was then t 3 advance through the fiat and accessible Orange Free State, whore our men could meet the Boers on equal terms. Events have borne out Sir William Butler's view with marvellous accuracy. Our reverses have compelled Buller to act from the Tugela, and have compelled the Government to concentrate a vast force in Cape Colony, whence it will probably be led by Lord Roberts and Kitchener into the Transvaal by way of the Orange Free State. In Sir William Butler we have a soldier whose knowledge of Boer i tactics in unrivalled. He s described as "eating out kis heart, scorned, abuseil and slandered be- cause he foresaw more clearly than his fellows the horrors of the present," and did his best in the short time left him to repair our military de- ficiences in that quarter. Assuredly England must kave a better use for such a man as this.</p>
        <p>71,410 MW.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <p>The country will watch with great interest the ' effect of this war upon the Volunteer movement. So far it has been pleasantly stimulative. The employment of picked Volunteers at the front, moreover, should bring a fresh access of enthusi- asm for a movement which only a few years ago seemed destined to fall into a stagnant and unsat- isfactory condition. The point at issue is whether the forces of the country can be so largely added to by volunteer effort that a reconsideration of the military problem for the present would be unne- cessary, or whether the Government will be com- pelled to reorganise the army on a modified con- scription basis. The question must be faced be- fore long. We have so huge an empire now that we cannot hope to defend it with our present army, assisted by the fleet. The only remedies are disintegration, which is unthinkable, or a big increase in the military forces at the disposal of the War Office. Several highly placed authorities are of opinion that conscription in some form is unavoidable, and that Parliament will enact it in the first half of next century. Others believe that the task confronting us could be overcome by a great extension of the Volunteer system, under which the men would be better trained, batter armed, longer in service and compelled to give up far more of their time to military duties than now. This may be aforlorn hope, but forlorn hopes sometimes come to fruition. It is, however, too early to prophesy the outcome. Our entire mili- tary system is in the melting-pot. What shape it will take when it emerges it is impossible to say.</p>
        <p>We are very sorry that the well-mean endeavours of the Runcorn Board of Guardians to procure assistance from the Patriotic Fund for a Crimean veteran in their Workhouse, have failed. Many people will remember that a large sum was subscribed at the time of the Crimean War—osten- sibly in aid of the widows of the soldiers killed— but the number of widows has been nothing like sufficient to exhaust the funds or even employ them adequately. The money is really " hcked- up," and it benefits nobody except perhaps the officials who are paid for taking charge of it. The Runcorn Guardians have received a courteous reply from the Secretary to the Fund regretting that "it is only for the relief of widows and cannot under any circumstances be applied to soldiers," and the veterans who fought at Bala- clava, Alma, and Inkermann have nothing to hope from this quarter and experience has shown that they cannot expect much from other quarters. As Mr. A. R. Norman remarked, the Fund places a premium on the death of a soldier. He cannot benefit from the generosity of the nation unless he gets killed !—and his widow then reaps the re- ward of his valour. It is nothing short of a scandal that these brave fellows who went through the terrible campaign must "fend for themselves" when they are quite incapable of doing so,or retire within the seclusion and disc Trace of the Work- house. The injustice of their case is the more ap- parent and shameful when we know that the money which would relieve their wants,is lying idle and useless. Public opinion is stronger than red- tape, and we counsel the Runcorn Guardians to persevere in their efforts to get this fund distri- buted among those who not only need help but fully deserve it.</p>
