      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>Warrington Field Club.—Lecture by Mr.</head>
        <p>Warrington Field Club.—Lecture by Mr.</p>
        <p>Ruddock, F.I.C. At the sixth meeting of the session, held in the Museum Lecture Room, Mr. F. G. Ruddock, F.1.C., delivered a lecture on coal. He commenced by describing and illustrating; by simple but most effec- tive experiments the nature of combustion,and the dissociation which at certain temperatures befalls the elements of compound substances. He then briefly advertel to some of the consequences of this law of dissociation which have to be remembered *en considering the state of our planet during the ages preceding and immediately following the con- solidation of its primitive crust. With admirable lucidity he described the relatively high proportion of carbon present in the atmosphere during whole geological epochs,and the proportionally favourable conditions for the growth of cryptogamic plants when that stage of biological progress bad been reached. The enormous development of vegetation during the carboniferous epoch ; and its effects in the fixation of carbon during the millennia of that epoch, with the gradually resulting alteration of the atmosphere, diminishing the chances of aure7o- bic forms, but fitting the earth's surface for the ex- istence of air-breathing animals were stated; and the lecturer then proceeded to discuss some of the questions arising out of the modern economical utilisation of the coal whose chemical constitution and geological history he had so clearly set before his audience. He explained the difficulties which had until recenty prevented the general use of water gas as a heat and power producer ; but,as he showed, steady patient work by men of science and different nationalities has recently borne fruit in tt:e discovery of methods which have eliminated those difficulties and are initiating changes in more than one branch of industry which will have far- reaching if not absolutely revolutionary effects. A hearty vote of thanks was accorded to Mr Ruldock, on the proposition of Dr White, seconded by Coun- cillor L Greening, F.L.S.</p>
