        <p>IMPORTANT TO WIVES AND FAMILIES OF THE RESERVISTS, RUNCORN. The wives, families, or relatives of reservists who have been called up for active service are requested to furnish their names either to Superintendent Years,n, at the Runcorn Police Station, or to S2rgeant-Instructor Cal- laghan, at the Drill Hall. SUDDEN DEATH AT WIDNES. The death is reported et Catherine Finn, 38, Moss-street, Widnes, who died suddenly on Mcm- day ab ,ut 7.30 a.m. The woman had enjoyed good health all her life, and before retiring on Sunday night she ate a hearty supper of potato° pie. About 4.40 the next morning she complained of violent pains in her side. She was taken thwnstairs, and hot salt applied to the affected parts, and also given brandy end port wine, but without relief. She expired at the t:me stated. • RUNCORN PARISH CHURCH DIVIDING CLUB. The 19:h annual report of the above, just issued, shows an income for the year of £264 14: 71, of which £230 lie represented subscriptions ; £8 194 entrance fees ; £22 2i 6d levies : and £1 33 lid fines. The expen- diture included £99 103 sick pay ; £5 on account of deaths; and £lO iLL 9 or management expenses. The amo-mt available for dividend among 177 members was £149 6s 10d leaving 23 11!-Ii in haud. Tree accounts were duly audited by Messrs H Clarke and W E Dudley. RUNCORN FREE LIBRARY. .</p>
        <p>The issues fo-t7;;---mon-th of November, when the library was opPn 22 days, were as follows ;—Theology, 21; philosophy 6 ; science 56 ; hist3ry 86 ; poetry 9; fic- 'ion 1,421 ; mis,•ellannous 134 ; refnence works 63 ; French literature 2 ; music 16; total 1,819, showing a decrelsa in the lending department of 191, and an in- crease in the reference department of 36, as compared with the corresponding perio -1 of last year.</p>
