      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>EVILS AND DANGER OF DRINK.</head>
        <p>THE EVILS AND DANGER OF DRINK.</p>
        <p>Mary Ann BAen, 26, Tayior's-row, Runcorn, was charged on TueFday inornin-,, before Mr. F. J. Norman, with cruelty to her children, on the 18th of December, and remanded to Fautsford for eight days, so that the state of her mind might be enquired into.—lt appears that for sometime past the woman has been taking drink heavily, to obtain which she was constantly pawning almost everything in the house. On Monday night sh- became possessed of a raz )r with which she threatened to cut the throats of her five children, the youngest of whom is but three months old. She did the chillren nr, harm however and contented herself with sin:ply flour- ishing the closed weapon over them. Subs',quently she gave herself up to the police and threatened to injure herself if she was not locked-up. SALE OF WORK AND CHRISTMAS TREE AT BRUNSWICK</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>Scoot.</head>
        <p>Scoot.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday afternoon a Sale of Work and a Christ- mas Tree were opened in the Brunswick Schoolroom, with the object of aiding the funds of the Runcorn Wesleyan Circuit. Amongst those present were :—Revs J A B Malvern, and H Morrison, Mr A R Norman, Mrs F J Norman, Miss Norman, Mr S Taylor, Miss Taylor, Mrs j W Frith, Mr and Mrs R H Posnett, Messrs. E Marshall, George Bellamy, T Cooke, T Simister, and R Morris. The opening ceremony was performed by Mr. A R Norman, who congratulated the ladies sewing meet- inc upon the excellent result of their labours as evi- deeced by the display of goods upon the stalls. In the Past the Runcorn Circuit had been favoured by having in their midst a number of wealthy gentlemen who had always been ready to make up any deficiency in the funds, but owing to removals they had lost a good deal of that kind of support, and had now to depend to a certain extent upon efforts like the present. He had much pleasure in declaring the sale open and wished the enterprise every success. (Ap- plause.)—The services of Mr Norman were suitably ac- knowledged by Mr G Bellamy ; seeonded by Mr R H Posnett ; and supported by Mr T Simieter.—Te stalls and stallholders were : Christmas Tree : Mesdames Bellamy and A R Normal&amp; ; sewing meeting stall, Mes- dames Christien, Morrison, Mellor, Rawlinson,Lightfoot, Ellison, Marshall and E Taelor; refreshments. Mesdames Massey and G Millington; tableau, Mrs F J Norman,and Miss Norman; children's entertainments, Misses Simkin and Taylor. The committee responsible for the arrange- ments included the above mentioned together with Messrs George Millington,A Riley, F Ellison, J Birkett,G A Hayes, T Saunders,J Norman, C F Kniveton,W Jones, T Simister, E Taylor, J Private, G Bowyer; with !Are Rawlinson as treasurer ; Mr F J Norman, assistant treasurer ; and Mr W Millington, secretary. During the evening solots were given by MiFS3S A Cooke,Withington, Bisbrown, Carter and Leathwool. The decoration of the stalls was undertaken by Messrs George Hayes and T Leach.—The sale of work was continued on Thursday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT</head>
