      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>STEAM ROLLER DEFERRED.</head>
        <p>THE STEAM ROLLER DEFERRED.</p>
        <p>Mr. T. Wright in accordance witla his notice moved "that the Council purchase a steam roller for use on the highways within the Council's district," and said the question was one that had been before the Council pre- viously. when it was stated that a ten ton roller—which was all they would require—would cost something like £476. Thot wss rather a big order and at the last meeting of the Council Mr. J. Mclntyre suggested re- ferring the question to the Highways' Committee. As that committee consisted of 21 members repreFentative of the Council, he offered no ottction to the manr be- ing relegated to the committee and would withdraw his motion on that understanding. Mr. Wright was given permission to withdraw his motion, and Mr. J. Mclntyre propoaed that the matter be referred to the Highways' Committee, observiog the subject was one that required to be gone into in a thorough manner, as it involved a very large outlay, not only in initial cost, but also in cast of maintenance and supervision. Mr. Linaker seconded, and the motion was carried.</p>
        <p>THE LIGHT HAILWAY4.-A PLEA FOR WESTON POINT. Mr. J. Mclntyre reported that the Parliamentary Commit tee had resolved "That it he a recommendation to the District Connell that they offer no opposition to the proposed light railway from Warrington to Weston provided that the traffic and the rights of the public were satisfactorily secured, and that the roads be widi ned where necessary to the satisfaction of the Council," Unfortunately the the representative of the District most interested in the Echeme was not present at the meeting of the Committee owing to an omission in the sending out of notices, and he believed he had a aueeeition to offer. The maps were examined very care- fully, and the Committee found that from Stockton Heath to Warrington the proposed railway did not come upon the Ceuncil's roads at all, but between Runcern and Weston they did come upon their roads, and th it was the point upon which they directed their attention. He had no doubt they would have further opportunities of vine into the questioe again,a,nd therefore he moved the acieVien of the Committee's report. Mr. C E Liaaker seconded, addine that Mr. Mclntyre had forgotten to say that the coretesitt,e passed another resolutien asking " that the promoter's repre;entative should meet them in Warrington within the next fort- night to censult with regard to the line from Warring- ton to North% ich which was to be an electric tram- way." _ . .</p>
        <p>Mr. J H Chalwiek. as the repreientativP of Won Point said the first scheme would be of more benefit to</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>This meeting twAr place at the Workhouse, Dutton, laA Tuesday, when there were present :—Messre j Moseley-Williams (chairman), A R Norman, J Mead-iw- croft, T Reynolds, the Rural District Councillors, with George F Ashtun (clerk) W E Hough (deputy clerk),and James Cunnah (master).</p>
        <p>THE CRIMEAN VETERAN.-NO RELIEF. Mr. J. S. French, secretary to the Royal Commission of tie Patriotic Fund, wrote a 3 follows :—" In reply to your letter of the llth inst., I very much regret to have to inform you that Mr. John Percival is not eligible for relief from the Patriotic Fund, which is only for the relief of widows, and cannot under any circumstanc.s be applied to soldiers. Toe Light Brigade Relief Fund administered by this Commission is only for the relief of those who took part in the Charge of the Light Brigade at the battle of B daclava,,and as Mr. Percival apparently did not take part in that charge, his name not b2ing on the list of survivors, it is regreted that he is not eligible for aid from that funcl."—Replyiuz to a further enquiry from Mr- Ashton, asking for a copy of last year's balance sheet. Mr. French stated they did riot issue balance sheets, but their annual report could be pur- chmed at any Parliamentary stationers as a Parliamen- tary paper. The Clerk also read a letter out of the public Press from the Chairman of the Patriotic Fund to the Lord Mayor of London, which set out the particulars and methods of the distribution of the fund and which was written to dispel erroneous ideas regarding the disposal of the fund. _ _</p>
        <p>The Chairman was personally of the opinion there was something wrong with the funds when they were com- pelled to beep a man who had fought their battles for them, as a pauper. Mr. A. It Norman also considered the fund came very short in that it did not provide for such cases. It seemed t) him that it was nothing but a premium upon the death of the soldier. If a man went through the war and had a wife and family dependent upon him, and was not killed there was nothing for him or them ; but if he was killed the wife and family were provided for. He thought that was a fact that should be taken into account in future, and he thought that men who had gone through the war should be better provided far in future.</p>
        <p>In reply to Admiral Parry the Chairman said the man wa,s not a pensioner,lnd they could do nothing for him at present. The matter dropped. CHRISTMAS CHEER FOR THE INMATES.</p>
        <p>Miss B. Dutton, on behalf of the Lidi visiting, Com- mittee wrote asking for permission to follow their recent annual custom of giving an annual tea and ektartain- ment to the inmates of the workhouse early in January. They would be glad to see the Cnairman and any other Guardians who could attend.</p>
        <p>The offer of the Ladies' Committee was accepte with thanks.</p>
        <p>A CHRISTMAq BOX FOR THE INMATES.</p>
        <p>The Master reportel that Mr. J imea Taylor, of Heisy, had sent a cheque for £5 for the purpose of pro- viding a Christmas treat for the inmate. The gift was gratefully acknowlechzed, on the motion of Mr. Mclntyre. secondel by Mr. Woodward.</p>
        <p>ADMIRAL .1.7,NE3 PARRY ONT oFFICIALISM.—THE ADMIRAL VEXED.</p>
        <p>Admiral Jones Parry referred to the visit of Mr. Daiasey, the Local Government Inspector, a month ago, and commented on the fact that the remarks he made to the Board were not made in the House Commit- tee. The Inspector said one of the officials was too good for his work. That was an extraordinary thing to say. Mr. Dansev quite forgot that this officer was the only assistant that the master had in the house, and the only person he could rely upon in case of fire or mutiny or in any other emergency. Yet the Inspector came there and said he was too good for his work. The Inspector also made 30me remarks about whiskey bottles being found in the lockers, and recommended that a closer supervision should be kept on the lockers. All that the inspector found was s3me little phial which had got into one of the lockers. Be characterised these remarks as a snub to the master and matron publicly made in that room. The Inspector might have made these state- ments quietly at the meeting of the House Committee. The next complaint Mr. Dansey made was about the beds in the hospital. He (Admiral Parry) thought the comfort of the pe•sms in that house was very largely under the care of the Ladies' Committee, and notwith- standing what was said to the contrary some years ago, he thought that Committee was the most useful Committee they bad. (Hear, hear.) The ladies were of opinion that the present beds were far better than flock beds. He thought Mr. Dansey's re- marks on this subject were a snub to the doctor, the La ies' Committee, and the master and matron. The Inspector further suggested that the boys' teeth should ise examined by a dentist. That question had been be- fore the Board previously, and the House Surgeon who he believed took a great interest in the boys was quite prepared to pull their teeth out, or anything else. He protested against the remarks of the Inspector as being alteeether uncalled for. He would have replied to the Inspector at the time, but he understood from the Chair- man that the meeting was closed. He had fully intended to refer to the matter at the last meeting but was un- able to attend. He asked Mr. Danes, in the Hemet Committee if he had any observations to make, and he said he had none. He thought his remarks might have been made quietly in Committee instead of before the rutevtiog of the board. , The Chairman quite agreed with Admiral Parry that Mr. Da.nsey's remarks would have been much better made b!fore the Committee.</p>
        <p>ASSESSMENT COMMITTEES AN EVIL.</p>
        <p>Mr. Meadowcroft, referring to a resolution passed at the last meeting of the board in favour of the creation of a new clue, for the hearing of rating appeals, and the abolition of ths jurisdiction of Quarter Sessions in assessment appeals, said he would like to suggest an amendment to that resolution. He thought it did not go quite far enough. It oug'zt, in his opinion, to provide for the abolition of assessment Committees ; they were the sourca of the evil. He wished to knaw if it was necassary that he ahould give notice of at. amendment to that resolution'.</p>
        <p>The Chairman ruled that it would b 3 necessary to give notice. Mr. Woodward pointed out that to 13, quite in order they should proceed in accordance with Standing Order No 8.</p>
        <p>The matter then dropped. THE COMPLIMENTS OF THE SEASON.</p>
        <p>The Chairman wished all the members the compliments of the season, and expressed the hope that they would all coma back to their duties in the New Year with renewed vigour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>RUNCORN NOTES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN NOTES.</p>
        <p>[BY A CORRESPONDENT.] The festive season of Christmas will be observed in Runcorn in accordance with time-honoured cus- tom. Special services will be held in several places of worship, and there will be the usual joyous carolling. Yet, over all, hangs the dark-edged cloud of the war in the Transvaal, for Runcorn, like other towns, weeps and mourns for its dead—its truly noble dead—the soldiers whose lives were laid down in their country's cause.</p>
        <p>In connection with the calling out of the remain- ing portion of the Army Reserve it is pleasing to record the kindly conduct of the Highfield Tan- nery Manager. who hearing that one of his men had to go by the 27th inst., promptly gave him,s, holiday —not without pay—so that he might visit all his friends and prepare himself; and the Reservist was also assured that his wife and family will be looked after whilst he is away.</p>
        <p>The suggestion that the proposed tramways from Warrington to Weston should also branch off to Weston Point is a very good one, and one that would greatly benefit that rising district. For years the want of a good road communication between Runcorn and Weston Point has been keenly felt, and as year succeeds year the requirement be- comes more marked. Should the company include such a branch into their scheme they will go a long way towards securing the sympathies of local governing bodies.</p>
        <p>On Thursday night the usual Christmas Show was held in Runcorn. Tradesmen vied with each other in displaying the repleteness of thAir several establishments, and adopted many novelties in the way of " window shows" to attract the attention of the buyer. At night time 3arge crowds paraded the principal streets for the purpose of seeing the sights, and the town presented quite a busy appear- ance, which gives the impression that prosperity is on the increase.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>ST. HELENS R.F.C.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS R.F.C.</p>
        <p>History of the Club.</p>
        <p>Warrington's fixture with St. Helens is Saturday, is an important one for both clubs. S. Helens are now third in the League, and tiie following biitory of the Club will be read with int_Test</p>
        <p>" Nothing venture, nothing win." Soule few years ago wban the Northern Rugby Union came into exist- ence, the St. Helens Club was playin4 second fiddle in the town. A lo)ld step was decided on, and the club joined the new union, a move which met with much opposition and excited no little adverse comment, but which has been amply justified by t:oe success with which it has been attended.</p>
        <p>Before Northern Union days St. Helens. as we have stated, was not the premier team in the town. The position was then held by the St. Helens Recreation, a club which was carried on for the particular benefit of the emplcues in the vast glass works and collieries belonging to Messrs Pilkington Bras. The Rec. did not join the Northern Union at fire, and later on they ,stirted an Association team, which in its first year killed their Rugger section. Thus it happens that at oresent favour is pretty evenly divi- ded between the " Saints " (Rugby) and the Recreation (Asiociation) clubs. In the town there is a population of be:ween 80.000 and 90,000, and, the great proportion being workine men, unbounded enshusiasm is displayed in football. St. Helens has "gates "of 4,000 and 5,000 at:ordinary matches, and on special occasions the spec- tators numeer some thousands more. Despite the increasing vogue of the Association code, St. Helens is still a stronghold of the Rugby gime, and the support accorded the club anent which this history is being written is nearly, if not quite, as great now as formerly. The St. Helens Rueby Club origin- ated in the St. Helens Ringers F C., who played Queen's Park ; now the gnund is at Knowsley-road. In the 1883-4 season the old Rangers changed the naine,an.i the club was called" St. Helens." At the time two of the present Northern Union referees were members, Mr. A. Borthwick being captain of the first team and Mr. J. Li wards holding a similar appointment in the" A" team. For a few years things went on without any- tiling brilliant la,ing done. Then the Kendal contingent, Cross first and then Graham and Allen, appeared on the scene, and from their advent can be dated the present prominence of the club. It has gone through na4.ny vicissitudes, but has gradually worked its way into the front rank. A VERY SUCCESSFUL SEASON.</p>
        <p>Viewing its achievements, we notice that the most suceeq4ful season the club has yet experienced was that of 1893-4. The chief result of the eight months' play was that St. Helens displaced Broughton for the first division of the Lancashire Rugby League. The team made themselves champions of the second division, and an idea of their prowess may he gathered from the analysis for the season :—Playel 43 matches, won 31, lost 7, drawn 2. The total number of points scored was 466 against 112, a mAjority of 354. In Leage encounters the majority in points was 165, and in twenty-'three matches die opponents or St. Helena failed to score at all.</p>
        <p>Perhaps the club's greatest feat was that of running into the final for the Northern Union Cup in April, 1897. They met Batley at Headingly, and had every prospect of success. In the first round the team had beaten Lees by fifty-eight pomts to nil ; t.han tney vanquished Castleford by sevente-m points to three ; next their neighbours, Wigan, succumbel, and in the fourth round Tyldesley had to own defeat. The semi-final brought St. Helens into the field against S arinton, " The Lions," for whom Valentine was playing. Thus the final issue lay between Batley and the Saints—worthy representatives of the White and Red Roses. But St. Helens entirely failed to play up to their real form or their reputation, and the team suffered defeat by ten points to three. It was somewhat curious that in the following season the St. Helens club was drawn against Batley in the first round of the same competition. Batley were at hems, and the men from the chemical town once more wept under, The St. Flelens Club has twice gone touring. During the winter of 1891-2 the men went North, where they beat Percy Park and were defeated by Rockcliffe. next tour was also in the North, Reckcliffe being drawn with and West Hartlepool and 33uth Shields beaten.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>TELE PLA.YER WITE TaE FIIGUE3I' HONOURS'</head>
        <p>TELE PLA.YER WITE TaE FIIGUE3I' HONOURS'</p>
        <p>The player who has attained the highest honours is William Cross, afov-raentioned. When Lancashire was the chanapion county Cross played half back with the representatives of the Red Rose. He ale) played for the Nlrth of England, and was chosen for his c iuntry against Wales, but %vita unable to play through sickness. Cro,s commenced football when he was sixteen years old, appearing for Kendal Hornets. Hs playing career kad been one of anomalies. After comin to St Helens, he played for the North of England even before he was picked for the county (Lancashire). Besides this ha assisted Westmoreland, which claimed him under the birth qualification,against his adopted county at Wigan, where Lancashire was unespectedly beaten. Subsequent to his Lancashire County matches he was again included in the ranks of the Westmoreland men. Turoughout; he has been an amateur.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>WONDERFUL PLACE KICKER</head>
        <p>A WONDERFUL PLACE KICKER</p>
        <p>&amp;allow waa another of the grate plavors in St. Helens. He was not only the speediest man in the team, but at the end of more than one season held the record for the number of tries sclred by any single player. Sudlow was famous for his great success in place kicking. It was a piculiar feature of his play that he did not take a short run, as most others do, for his kick. Instead, he stood over the pall, swung his leg back, and the leather went through the posts with un- erring precision. He very seldom missed his aim. Several of the men who played at 13itley are still members. Doherty, who stands sft. 4in., has been in the ranks for some ten years, and is one of the finest de- fensive players in the nountr,___</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>CLEVER TRICKSTER.</head>
        <p>A CLEVER TRICKSTER.</p>
        <p>Last se tsoa D tyid Traynor was ackriowleiggcl to be the best cantre in Lancashire, and he his shown this winter that he has lost none of his skill and dash. The last match in which he playel before this article was written was against Tyldes!ey, at St. Helens, and David gave a characteristic display. His deceptive feints so puzzled his opponents that he wriggled his way through them all, and after gaining a brillian try he completed Lis achievement by kicking a goal from the place. Foulkes, a player of a dozen years' standing, is still in capital form. For some years he has been recognized as one of the best fall backs in the North. He played for his county as full hack in the seasons 1893-5. appearing in all the county matches, except one. in 1894-5.</p>
        <p>Briers, too, is'a county man, an I i notti strong and speedy. . _</p>
        <p>At the present time St. Helens is ring we'l. The players, nearly all local men are in sterling, consistent form, and are enhancing the reputation they have gaiued of being strong forward. While the club has been in the Northern Union it has always come out of the tonrney at the end of the season with a fair share of success, and now it stands in the very first flight in the competition, and great h-pee are entertained that the cur) may yet be br.uf,,,ht to St. Helens.—Frorn " The Golden Penny."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>CHRISTMAS GREETING.</head>
        <p>A CHRISTMAS GREETING.</p>
        <p>Whoever roads this Yuletide Souvenir May joy be the,rs on many a Christmas Day ; May Heavenly blessings thrnuf.h the glad New Year Cheer all their life and brighten all their way. Full many a one in City's buly street, In hamlet quaint, in village gray and old, Will get the message tat is ever sweet— The joyful message that the angels told. And from a heart that fain would be sincere; Accopt the greeting, time worn, yet still 2:314, ; A Merry Xmas and a Happy Year, And all the bliss true love can bring to you, Peace and Goodwill for aye triumphant reign, With all the gifts that follow in their train ! CFIARLES F. FORSHA.W, LL.D. Hanover Square, Bradford.</p>
        <p>Fatal Fall Down-stairs.—lnquest.</p>
        <p>Mr. S. Brighouse. county coroner, conducted an inquiry at the Warrington Police Station on Wednesday evening, respecting the death of Elizabeth Dumican,a vvidow,aged 62 years,who died in the Infirmary at five o'olock on Monday after- noon.—The Coroner as the opening of the inquest, said he had received information from Dr. Battle that deceased was admitted to the Infirmary on the sth of November, suffering from a com- pound fracture of the leg, caused by falling downstairs, complicated with heart disease.— John Dumican, son of the deceased, said that he and his mother, who was 62 years of age,resided at 9, Gandy' s Walk. They were the only occupants of the house. At about seven o'clock on Sunday morning, the sth of November, when the accident occurred, he was upstairs when he heard his mother fall downstairs and shout. He ran out of th 3 room and found her lying at the foot of the stairs. She was taken to the Infirmary. In answer to a juror, witness said his mother was in the habit of coming downstairs in her nightdress to light the fire and then going upstairs again. A verdict of 'Accidental Death" was returned.</p>
