      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <p>HOMOCEA. FOR THE BATTLE-FIELD.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>NOTICE.</head>
        <p>NOTICE.</p>
        <p>TROOPS' FOR THE TRANSVAAL should WITHOUT FAIL, be provided with Homocea, as there is nothing more soothing or healing known. FOR WOUNDS, Cats, Bruises, Broken Skin, Sore Places, generally, use Homocea often, and plenty of it. INFANTRY need Homocea for anointing the feet on the march, and for scores caused by chafing a.xsutrements.</p>
        <p>CAVALRY need Homocea for troubles caused by rough riding, damp saddles, etc., and especially for piles.</p>
        <p>SEE THAT NO FRIEND goes to the front without Homocea. What else can they rely on to touch the spot "and soothe the achiag part" ?</p>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETIGS. --- Rugby Football .[By "Ovalist."' Though a very sudden :haw set in on Friday evening and continued all the following day, there was very little Rugby football in Lancashire, and none whatever in Warrington. There was just a question as to the fitness of the grc•und, which was soft in most places, but the committee decided to be on the safe side and postpone the match with Wigan. If they had allowed it to pro- ceed, only a poor "gate" would have assembled on such a raw day, and there was always the additional risk that the referee might decree the ground to be unfit. Smith failed to get his permit after being off work, so that the public were spared the disippoiatment of see- ing the team turn out without</p>
        <p>The match with Wigan will probably have to be played on some mid-week day. Excellent fare is pro- vided for to-day however, providing that the frost keeps off. St. Helens are always formllable and popular visitors to Warrington, and the attraction of their corn- ing this year is mad.. all the greater by the fact that they have done exceedingly well in the Lancashire Senior Competition. A fortnight ago they trounced Oldham, and are now burning to avenge t'aemcelves for the point captured by Warrington at the commenement of the season. Inlthe previous encounter at Wilderspool Warrington only won by a try of a doubtful character to nil. In fact the contests between the clubs have generally been_keen and chse.</p>
        <p>St. Helens have always been famous fir their strength and skill forward,and this season they possess one of the best packs in the county. They are strong, speedy,and splendid dribblers, while the backs are a well-balanced lot and good sorer. There is no doubt that the " Saints" have a good nail') this season, and. I think, there is no doubt also that Warrington have an equally good team. We cer:ainly ought to win on our own ground, and providing the forwards can only bold their own the issue should never be in doubt. Rut with two such good men as Eden and Seholtze off, I fear the visitors will have a slight pull forward.</p>
        <p>In any case there is certain to be an exciting and well- contested game at Wildierspo3l, and the speltators ought to muster in their thousands. In another column wilt be found a history of the St. Helens club which makes interesting reading.</p>
        <p>The Warrington team was chovn on Wednesday night and is as follows :—Boscow, full back ; Fish, Isherwood Smith, and Jones, three-quart-2rs ; Bate and Duckworth, half backs ; Taylor, Morron, Swift, Boardman. Fair- hurst, Merryweather, Jolley and Hatton, forwards. The two latter take the places of Eden and Scholtze, both of whom are on the sick-list.</p>
        <p>There are going to be other attractions besides the football. for His Worship the Mayer has promised to kick off, and Messrs. Crosfield's band Nr,ll play selections of music. The Mayor has, on more than one occasion, been an interested spectator at Wilderspool, and rumour even has it that once, "once upon a time," he used to play Rugby football and was a particularly plucky tackler of big and burly opponents. He will receive a cordial welcome on the field of play occo aa,)re,—and, may he give a good kick!</p>
        <p>Salford defeatei 'Widnes at New Barns on Saturday, this beinr, the only clmpetition match played. The League table up to date is as follpws For. Against.</p>
        <p>P. W. L. D. (1. T. P. G. P. P. P. Runcorn 12..11.. 1. 0...21 29-129... 4 4... 20...22 Oldham 12...10... 2.. 0...15 43-159... 6 11... 45...20 St. Helens 13. 9.. 3. 1...13 32-122... 4 14... 50...19 Swinton l2. 9.. 3. 0...11 22 88...14 9... 55...18 Widnes 14. 7. 5. 2.. 9 24 90... 6 17... 63...16 Salford 14.. 8. 6. 0...19 36-146...17 22...100...16 Warrington 13.. 7. 5. 1. 10 26 98.. 8 14... 53...15 Leigh l2. 7. 4. 1...17 22-100...12 18... 78...15 .Brought'n Rater'sl2... 6... 6... 0... 6 15— 57...10 16... 63...16 Stockport ... 12.. 4... 8... o. 7 12— 50...10 22... 86... 8 *Rochdale Wnetsll... 4... 7... 0... 9 6 36... 7 21... 80... 6 Hillom 12.. 1..10 1. 812 52...11 36...130... 3 Wigan 13. 1...12 .. 0... 2 9 31...15 36...138... 2 Tyldesley 14.. 1...13... 0... 6 7 33 ..29 54...220 2 *Broughton Rangers and Rochdale Hornets hvsa each had two points deductei for breach of rules.</p>
        <p>Sankey Harriers. Much interest was manifested in the inter-run with Warrington Harriers (late Orford) from the Sloop Hotel, Liverpo 11-road, the headquarters of the above club. A let of talking has been going on for a long time between the supporters of each club as ta which is the superior, and on Saturday they met to try "conelusions." The visitors were well represented.whilst Sankey were with- out the services of their captain, G Esstwood, who was suffering from a severe cold. It was decided not to go over country on account of the thaw, but to race to Bofd Bridge and bir.k a distance of about 7 miles. The pack of 33 runners (20 of Sankey and 13 of Warrington) were started at 3 50, and went at a good pace, each man doing his utmost for his club, and the result of an exciting race saw T Devlin, of Sankey, the first man home. with S -Parson- age. of Warrington, a good second. The teams finished as follows :—Sankev : T Devlin, 1; Jvenh Riley, 3; L Green, 5; John Riley, 6; R Throssell, 7; J 10.; 32 points. Warrington S Parocnlzn, 2: J Robinson, 4 ; J Mather, 8 ; R Price, 9 ; W Evans, 10 ; P Holland, 11 ; 44 points. Majority for Satiliey by 12 points. The fixture for to-Jay is a Christmas Cross- Country Handicap, when 6 prizes will be compet,d for, and a special prize kindly presented by Mr. T. Burrows will be given to the man doing the fastest time.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>OUR</head>
        <p>OUR</p>
        <p>FOOTBALL COMPETITION</p>
        <p>(COUPON ON PAGE 7.) £5O Offered Weekly—Xloo Aggregate Prize.</p>
        <p>RESULT OF LAST WEEK'S COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>ONE COMPETITOR WINS £2O</p>
        <p>- The following were he results of the niqtches selected for our last week's competition —Preston North Knd drew with Burnley. Stoke drew w'th Newcastle United. Sunderland drew with Sheffield United. Wolverhampton Wanderers best Derby County, Notts County beat Nve,ton, West Bromwich Albion drew with Manchester City. B istiol City beat New Brompton, Salford belt viridn3s. The other two matches were abandoned on a, :twilit of the frost. Mr F Lindsey,37,Harrington-road,South Kensington. S.W. was the only competitor who gave eight correct results, and therefore wins the £2O prize. rHE CONSOLATION PRIZE.</p>
        <p>We do not undertake to give the RIO prize when neither £5O nor £23 is won' but have decided to give it this week and it will therefore be divided amongst the following com- petitors who gave seven correct results Twining,Strou€l; E Johnson, Wimbledon; G H Day, Daftington ; P Norman. Leicester; T Paine, Balham ; P Penley, Well- ingborough ; B Funnell, Carshalton ; S Stlmpson, Nottingham ; G Wilson. Twilkenham ; D Parry, Wolverton; H Chandler, Ruslombe ; C Fowler, Maidstone; J Tyler, Leicester ; C Williams, Cardiff; F Hill, Beswick, Manchester; B A Cook, Wrexham : J Daniels. Grimsby; B Dixon, Glou- cester; W P Turner, Belner ; G Dean, Sla:M?ld ; A Tin- dall, Kettering; W H Cox. Melt In Mowbray; S Chadwick, Warwick; G Jackson. Bristol ; J Ches- ter, Turvev ; S Ball, Plymouth ; W Smith, South - ampton ; J Bryan. Southampton ; H Hanley, Derby ; C Jones, Bromyard ; P Taylor, Le!cester ; J Wright, Alfreton ; B Peabody. Wlissto ; S Orton, Wal- born Green ; A Webster, Lincoln ; J Watson, Ports- mouth ; W Pe ry, Nuneaton.; J Palmer, Bury; F Nightingale, Lancaster ; 11 Payne, Ilkeston ; J Law- son, Barrow-in-Farness ; C Keen, Wood Green ; A Lowe, Burton-on-Trent; T E lwa-ds. Abergavenny ; A Mas-.,n, Walsall ; G Smith. Gorton; J Freeman, Birkenhead; W Kelly, Long Baton; A Marriott, York; C Chapman, Altrincharn ; W Sannders. Loicester ; F Eastwood, Chelten- ham ; D IJnwln, Newcastle-on-Tyne. THE COUPON PRIZE.</p>
        <p>!iThe £3 coupon prize has been won by Mr H E Pittarn, 72. Fore-street, Lo 'scion. with 6,850 C',1191113. Mr H Procter. 5. Spring Vale. Haslingien. Lancs., has scored 21 points for the Avregate Prize ; Mr Arthur 17 points. Mr Maple 16 points, Messrs Ma-on and Pittam (M.). 11 each, Messrs Priageon aid Hann 10 each. M ssrA Wood and Pittatn (I-1 E) 9 inch. Messrs Cook aria Rodgers 8 each, Messrs Astles, Uritchley, Pannell and Twining 7 each Messrs Millar and Hutchings 6 each. Messrs Rowley, Fletcher. Harris, Price, Sranlev. Salt. Tug well. Dean. Am bidge. Nelson. Marsh al and Taylir 5 eieh. A number of others are close behind.</p>
