        <head>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES</head>
        <p>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES</p>
        <p>SHOCKING NEGLECT OF FAMILY —A WARRINGTON FATHER SEVERELY PUNISHED. At the Warrington Borough Police Court 011. Monday, before Mr, F. Monks (in the chair) and other magis- trates, Semuel Ten is. Silver Squire, was charged with neglectine his two children in a manner likely to muse them unnecessary suffering and injury to health.—Mr. A. Browne preeecut d on beh elf of the N.S:P.C.C. and stated the facts of the case, which he said was a very bed one. Drink -sae purely and simply the CaUS3 of it. eereiglat ago last Tuesday, when he (Mr. Browne) applied for a warrant, they hid evidence to show that the men bad been drunk the whole of the previous Men- day, Tuesday. Wednesday and Thursday. Whenln- specthr Flackstt and Sergeant Bell visited the house,they founi him in a beastly state of drunkenness. Ha ran his wife out of the house, lossi made use ef filthy lan- geage. There was very title food in the hnusa. The eldest child a girl of fourteen, had to go out and . pick cinders to make a fire with, and at times bad to sell cinders to buy food with the money For the last six months prisoner had, on an average given his wife Is a week. He paid the rent, but was six week: in arrear. Neighbours had had to Rive the woman and children food, time after time. The house and the children were clean. The prisoner would not work, his excuse being that he had a bad eye and a bad leg. 'le (Mr. Browne) theught he had else a teed heart. He should be abs to .earn at least Ma a week.—Evidence having been given bearing out this statement prisoner was cernenitted to gaol for four months with hard labour. Ice-Careen DEALER FINED —A WAIINP.7G. On Monday at the Boroueh Pence Court, before Mr F Monks and other magistrates, Barnado Monfredi, an ice cream maker, was summeceil ler keeping his licensed refreshment rooms in Irlem-street open for the sale of refreshments during pr )hibitEd hours—at 5.30 on Sun- day afternoon, December 10.-h ; and at 5 o'clock on Sun- day afternoon, December 10th in Winwick-street.—De- tectiye Barlow stated that at 5 30 on Siuniay afternoon De.cenaher 1011, he, in company soi:h Detecive Ryer, visited the licensed refresnment bongo of the defendant in Irlarn-street, where they found 19 people partaking of ice cream and biscuits. There was only one apartment, and that was a small one. Ile had visited the place in consequence cf in- structious which had been receieed from the Chief Con- stable. Mr. Monfredi was in charge of the place, and he informed him that h. would be reported. Barlow added that he subsequently visited the house of the de- fendant in Winwick street and found eight persons similarly engaged. There was only one apartment.— Mr. Browne, for the defence, said this was one of those vexed canes to which reference was made from time to time, people leaving met to consume ice cream and ' wafers on a Sandey afternoon. Some peeple did object to these shops being openen. In this case the defend ant was the proprietor of several ire cream shops, and until last May he had no license and did not know he rrvired one, and he would net require one it he closed at ten o'clock at night. If he din not open till four o'clock in the rnornhag, he would reneire no license at all, in fact be could Itsep open all day on Sunday. He had been put in a ridictilous position.— The Chief Constable : I object to that statement. I think the Clerk will rule otherwise Ho would not be able to heep open al day —Mr. Browns: The editer of " Stoee " has an entirely different opinion, namely that he hes a perfect right, if he does not take Oat a license, to open the whole of Sunday until ten o'clock at night. —The Chief Constable: I don't eduit it.—Mr. Brnwne: Don't ieterruot.—The Chief Constable: I must if you make false stVements.—Mr. Browee : The editor says. " It is to be observed that by what is, perhane, a feels- lative oversight the time of openiog remains unaltered." With regard to the preseet case,howeyer, the defendant - had no right to open ; aed he now aireitteci that he had made a mistake. Next May he would ahaelon hislieense and go oe under the old srtem and keep open —The Chief Constable : And vrff3hall preeecute him.— Mr. BrDwae : You cannot.—The Chief Constable: I have Peen the defendant personally several times, and have cautioned him th-it he was doing wrong., and I have been generally met with the remark" We don't understend Enelish." (Lsuehter.)—The Chairman : Be knows English money. (Leueliter.)—A fine of Si. and costs was inflicted in each cese.—The Chief Con- stable wished it to be made known through the press that if some of the other refreshment houses were not better conducted mere drastic Mears would he taken of bringing the proorieters to beet.--enuncillnr Shaw Green: Something should be done.—The Chairman : And the Bench will support you.—The Chief Constable : I am sure of that, air.</p>
      </div>
