        <p>TRI,tVE3 4.ND SECEIVICR9 SMARTLY ITRALT WlTR.—James Grindley, 14, Arthur Gibbons, 13, William Moores, 12, I Harry Davies and William Allman, 12, were charged with stealing 18 packets of dry stap, value Is 2d, and six tablets of Alpine soap value is, the property of Charles Seddon, at Frodsham, on the 14th December.—All pleaded guilty with the exception of Davies, and there being no evidence against him the case was withdrawn.—Walter Rose, 25, Linenhall- street, Chester, said he was a driver in the employ of Mr. Seddon. On th ) date n :med he was in Frodsham with a ear, containing about 4 dozen packo s of dry soap, and 3,' dozen Alpine soap. At about six o'clock in the evening he went into the Bridge Inn to bait his horses, and get something to eat, leaving the cart outside. On starting for home some two hours later he missed the soap to the extent mentiened in he charge, and subsequently gave information to the police.—Janet Grindley, a little girl, ester of one e the prisoners, stated she saw Davies neat the cart, but did not see him take anything. In cross-examiaation however, she admitted telling the police a different ta.le.—P C. Dicken stated that from information received he made inquiries about the robbery, and apprehended all the boys about eleven o'clock the same evening, with the exception of Allman whom he aporehensied the follewing day. He charged them with the offeac). Gr:ndley, Allman, and Gibbons each replied that they took two peek-ts, efoore, said he took eight' five of which he gave to Mrs. Cunliffe, and three he bid le:hind a rain tub. Davies denied taking any, but said he knew who had stone so, Witness afterwanls fonad two packets ef soap at Grindley's, Gibeons' and Allman's : five at Mrs. Cunliße'a house, and three under a reintub.— Elizabeth Gibbons, was then charged with receiving two packets of soap well knowing the same to have been stolen Elizabeth Cualiffe. with having received 5 rackets of soap, and Mary Ann Grindley, with receiving two of the packet's knowing them to have been etolen.—Gibbens and Grindley were the mothers of the prisoners of those names.—P.C. Dicken stated that upon going to Mrs. Gibbous' house he" boy began en lug and said he bed told his mother he had taken some soap from the cart, and the replied" put it on the drawer." and he did se. —Mrs.Clunliffe and Mfrs. Grindley denied that they received the soap, knowing it to have been stolen property.—Mrs. Gibbons said she tole her boy to take it bank at once, but he said it would be no use doing that as the cart had gone.—The Bench ordered the boys to receive six strekea men with the birch-ral.—Mrs. Gibbons was lined lOs and 10s 6d costs, or one month hard labour in default ; Mrs. Cunliffe. 10s and 12s 61 costs or one month hard labour; and Mrs Grindley, 10$ and lls 6d costs, or one month bard labour. THE ICFFECr OF THE WAR UP3N A DRUNK /RD.—SamUel Brown, for being drunk and disorderly in Ship-itrett, was fined 5s and 86 6d costs, or 14 days hard labour.—John Brown, his son, also eh +reed with drunk cud disorderly con- dust in Main-street, on the 9th of December, eaplained that he had been having a bit of a discussion with a young man about the Trausvral war, when he got excited about the Boers, and consequently came out with Stronger language than he ought to have done.—Fined 5s and 83 6d costs, or 14 days hard labour in default.</p>
