        <p>A --WW.rrThitOn- Man-Heavily Fined at Chester. On Saturdac morning at the Chester -minty Police Cour"-„Albert Payne, a builder who resides in Liverpool-road, Warrington, and is the occupier of brickworks,. at Stanley-road, Ellesmere Port, was summoned for not keeping the same in conformity with the statete, in that he failed to fence securely the fly-wheel and every part of a certain steam engine therein, by which he had reniereJ him it'll liable to a penalty' of .Elo.—Sidney Erant,one of her Majesty'a Inipec:-,ers of Factories, said that oh the 21st Novembir he found the engine in the enerine- room entirely unfencel. The machinery had r'been working that Tnorning, but was not then working, as it was the dinner hour. There were no guards whatever. He had visited the factory previously on the 2nd March and had then warned the manager and given him precise instructions ai to the carry- ing out of the fencing, but nothing had been done. It was a dangeroas thing to leave an engine un- fenced, and as it had been going on for so long, he must ask for the infliction of a substantial penalty. —Mr. Payne's manager appeared and said that owing to an important engagement Mr. Payne was unable to attend. He was authorized to admit the offence, which Mr. Payne regretted! :but the matter had since been attended to. Ile resided in War- rington and left everything to a manager, who had neglected to have the machinery fenced. Witness had only been appointed since the offence was cam- mitted.—The Bench considered it a bad case, and imposed a penalty of £5 and costs.</p>
