        <p>"A Merry Christmas, Bob." said Scrooge, with an ear:wetness that could not be mist aken, as he clappe him on the back. "A. merrier Christmas my good fel low, than I have given you for many a year. I'll raise- your salary, and we will disluss your affails this after noon over a bowl of smoking bishop. Make up the fires and buy another coal scuttle, Bob Cratchit." And as to the big prize turkey which the regenerated Scrooge sent to Camden Town, do we nct all know what sort of bird it was P That turkey never could have toed upon its legs, Mr. Dickens says. They must have snapped short off like sticks of sealing wax. How they managed to cook and eat that phenomencn the author doesn't say, as it was twice the size id Tiny Tim. Yet they did it somehow ; and it was a mercy if the family (previously half stave, e d) didn't all fall ill of the unwen ted gorging of that wonderful Chrietmes day. Even assu ming (as we easily may) that the transmu- tation of an old skinflint like Scrooge into a high priest of charity was quite beyond the COMMOR run of modern mirecles, and that dinners from such sources are as rare as they are welcome, it still remains true that human nature is apt to be at its best at Co ristmas, especially in England, where, of all countri es in Christendom,most le made of that gentle and kindly holiday. Yet, sad to tell, the very jollity and generosity of Christmas leads to regretable exceeses. People eat and drink too much, and suffer limo rdingly. The roast and the boiled, the mince pies, the plum puddings, the tre- mendous fluehinge of wine, beer and other hever.ages, these things ovarloed the stomach and give the digestion more work than it can do, and punishment fellows. For Nature has no favourites; she treats those who violate her laws on the 25:h December exactly as though they had committed the offence en the first Monday in August And a 4 to the chronic dyspeptics, cf whom there are enough in this old island to cram all the parka in London they catch it fearfully about Chrietmas time. For, in pits of all warnings, they will go in for heavy meals and talus the chances. Often the penalty is an acute attack which may threaten life, and nearly always weeks of paia and digestive die crders, whereof there are as many kind as the body has or gans and functions. To them Christmas may be" m erry "for an hour or two ; then their merriment turns into mieeryets October fogs and rains succeed a hot summer. Now the writer is far from advising a free indulgence in the gustatory and hibulous fcstivities of the country's most popular holiday, but there is no use protesting against the fixed reeolutien of t he people to enjoy them- selves on that occasion. The cue toms and traditions of centuries arc behind it, and you might as well whistle , in the teeth of a north east gale. As a prophylactic and a cure for the evils alluded to. I can only recommend the universal use of Mother Seigel's Syrup, known to all as the best stimulant and digestant in the world. In a letter dated January 10, 1899, a gentleman Birrniugham, who loves the • pleasures of the table, and has money enough to buy whatever he wants, writes thus :—" It is only fair that I should thank you, as I do with all my heart, for the good done me by Mother Seigel' s Syrup. For years I have been vexed with indigestion, and always had a tormenting spell of it after any public banquet or uncoivinson intake of rich focd. Still, I was seldom able to resist tempta- tion, %Ph nigh I understood perfectly well what yielding to it was sure to cost me. I have sought a preventive or a cure in vain at the hands of the best physicians. all ,•f whom assured me that there was but one way, namely, to live on a low diet, and let luxuries alone. " But this did not suit me, and I broke through the regulati ens frequently, with the inevitable results However, when I began to use %ether Seigore Syrup I had (almost) a license to do as I pleased, After an in- viting but most risky dinner last Christmas I took a double dose of Mother Seigel,' and scarcely suffered at all. I told my experience, and a dozen friends adopted this arnaz7ne remedy right off. I am convinced that, with a little caution,and a dose of Mother Seigel's Syrup now and then, one may bid defiance to dyspepsia and the abominable ailments it sets up. Thanking you a thou- sand times, I am, yours truly, John Macfarlane." With these facts we wish a merry (and a healthy Christmas to the British people.</p>
      </div>
