        <p>MINOR OFFENcEI,-14enry Taylor, 20, Okell-street, for using obscene language was fined ss; and Robert Derbyshire for driving a cart without a light. was feed 58. .CRUELTY To A HOBSE. A WITNESS COMPLIMENTED.— William H. Pencil', carter, in the employ of Messrs Fair- dough and Co.. Warrington. pleaded guilty to violently beating a horse and otherwise cruelly illtreating it, and also to working the animal whilst in an unfit state for work, sloth of which offences he committed in Relent-street, Run- corn, on the I.lth of December,—lnspector Hicks, deposed to being on duty, at Top Locks, about 5 o'cloak on the day in question, wnen he received complaints of the defendant n'eusing and illtreating the horse. He seat Constable John Wilding after the defendant, and the horse was taken to the police station. The animal was in a very poor condition. and was bleeding from its top lip, from which a piece of Rosh was hanging like as if it had been kicked off. Both sides of the animal were cut, and blood was running down its legs. Altogether the animal presen- ted a sorry appearance.—Mrs. Blizabeth Bazley. 24, linglish- row, Runcorn, said she vets in Res:eat-street, and saw the defendant and another man abusing the horse. Defendant was kicking it all over the body, both when it was standing up and when it was lying down; whilst all the time he was using very foul I.nguage. She remonstrated with the defendant, but he only replied that she evidently had never had anything to do with horses or she would not have talked in that manner. She replied that if she could find a constable she would see that the d-fendint did not get off lightle.—Defendant handed in a character, and seld he did not kick the horse end that its lip got cut when it fell down on to the curb He, however, admitted the animal was not fit to work.—The Chairman observed they were taking a light view of the cases in fining him 5s and lie 6ti costs in each. He (the Chairman) wished also to thank Mrs. Bazley for the satisfactory way in which she had given her evidence.</p>
