      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>EXAMINER, FRIDAY, DECEMBER 22, 1899.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, DECEMBER 22, 1899.</p>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES. The Rev. F. E. Powell and the Reverses in South Africa.</p>
        <p>Preaching at S;. James' Church on Sunday evening. the Rev. F. E. Powell, the Vicar' referred to the British reverses in the war in South Africa. He said that had he not announced his intention of preaching that night upon the observance el Sunday he should have d.velt upon some obvious lessons which had beee taught tbe nation during the last week. But, as an Englishman, with head and heart full of what was happening in a part of our great Empire, be felt compelled te say a few words. There was no need te exaggerate the gloom and despondency which bad fallen upon them. Three serious reverses in three different perts of the field of war, while not cause for uniuo Mere), were yet food for solerna reflectioe. And to him, at arty rate, they con- veyed one very solemn lesson. How did we enter upen this war? Ile was not referring to the political events which preceded it, or to the diplomacy, whether British or Boer? But when war was declared bow did the nation regard it? In what spirit? He feared in a very wrong spirit. Few seemed to realize the awfulness ef war. Few seemed to regard it, at its best, as a frightful necessity, for the furtherance of equity and righteous- ness. NI ; the great majority of the nation seemed lost in a spirit of unhealthy excitement. Tie-re seemed very little recognition of God, but a great deal of unwholeseme brag about our military powers, about the men and the money we could and would eroduce When President Kruger wrote that the cost of the war would stagger humanity we seemed only to sneer. We sent our soldiers away, seldom in a spirit of prayer, too often in a spirit of intoxication. After one of the first Boer victorier, they read that the host of armed men knelt on.the ground bareheaded and returned thanks to God. Last Sunday afternoon ono of the shells fired from Lord Methuen's artillery fell in the midst of an open-air prayer meeting. Would to God they saw tnov of this spirit in their midst ! Reliance upon material resources would fail them and deservedly fail them. " Not by might, nor by power, but by My Spirit, saith the Lord of Hosts." He thought a wri'er in a magPzine just pub- lished was not quite just to us Elglish in this respect. Allowance should be ma le for Enelish reserve in matters of religion. But he must say that when he read last menth of the way in which our Canadian fellow-soldiers attended a celebration in Quebec Cathedral on the day before they sailed for Snatti Attlee, when the whole staff, including the governor-general and 300 soldiers, received the hdy communion, he did wish we had not sent our men away with a more "Geed luck to you," or to the strains of mere music-hall songs. Let them hope that the irreligi ais spirit in their midst was more apparent than real. He believed it is, but these repulses would serve a goo I turn to our nation if they charened us, and made us remember that it is " None other than fighteth for us, but only Thou. 0 God." They had far better recall Rudyard Kielinee Recessional instead of the ditty so largAy now in mini: "Len we forgot, lest we forget."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>Christmas Choral Service.</head>
        <p>Christmas Choral Service.</p>
        <p>Wycliffe Church was well filled en Sunday evening when the annual Christmas choral service was held, and eelections from the,"Moseiah" given. The choir had been augmented for the occasion and a small instrumental band introduced. Mr. W. H. Payton, Mus. Bac. was the conductor and Mr. F. Hickman presided at the organ. The orchestra consisted of the following :—Fir4 violins, Mr. R. H. Pearson and Mr. W. Brown; second vielius, M. A. Knowles and Miss B. Bouchier ; 'cello. Mr. B. Bond; viola, Mr. T. Peplow ; bass, Mr. R. Whitfield. After the overture to the "Messiah" hai been played, the congregation joined in the singing of a hymn, and die Rev. J. Yonge offered prayer. The beautiful recit and air "Comfort ye" and "Every valley" were sung by Mr. Arthur G-iffiths with hia accustomed ability. Mr. C. A. Goldthorpe essayed the difficelt recit and air " Thus saith the Lord" and" Whe may abide ?" Mis F. Booth's contrklto voice was heard to advantage in the recit " Behold a Virgin," and the air " 0 Thou that telleet." Mr. Geerge Clay is the, possessor of a deep bass voice, and is coming to the frent as a local singer. He gave an excellent interpretation of the raele and air "For behold darkness "and "The people th4t walked!. Mrs. A. Booth contributed the recit " There were Shop herds" and the diffieult air" Rejoice greatly," both of which were carefully sune.. Miss E. Kershaw very sweely sang the last of the solos " Then shall the eyes" and " lie shell feed fits flock." The choruses were especially well sune, the test being" Glory to God " and" Ballelujah." The orchestra played the" Pastoral Symphony" with much skill and taste. A very success- ful musical servico concluded with the Vesper hymn. The collections taken were on behalf of the choir funds. ‘`Pound Sunday," at St. Paul's Church.</p>
        <p>' feeling people to engage in hilarious festivitioS in an hour of national crisis and peril. The special point, however, on which he would insist mos', was the need of a patient, firm, stedfast confidence in God in view of this national emergency. Of al the Limos in life, personal or national, when the comfort or help of religious faith wi re most valuable, such a time as this was conspicuous. If they bad a true, personal faith in God as ckuocerned in their welfare, as overruling their life, therm that faith was tnere,preseot in the heart, for them to fall back upon in toe eeason. ef trial, a grownd of confidence that nothing could shake, nothing could destroy. We needed a better refuge than their own nerve and courage. If they had no reliance in God, no habit of trust, no int3lli4ent coofiience in His reality and His wand:p..Bs and faithfulness, they would find themselves in sorry straits in the hour of need. If evil tidings from trict !show(' ointinue co coma (whic a might God forbid) the Caristian spirit of believing Christians wouln be needed to control this oration,to pre- s,rv4., it from panic and from all kinds of hasty im- petuous speech and action. We were not a people accustomed tn defeat.. .T.,his generation had no recollec- tion of any dere at, xeplu unlookod for circumstances against svii;ch new it was thought all precaution had been taken. SOUCed3ive huiniliatious such as those were unparadelei in the history of their army, at least in the tar 70 ,re 80 years. I was the duty of the Christian orlopte to moderate and control opinion, to deprecate hasty juiemeat, to stein the tide of anger wheth.r against th-ir owa leatleri or the stubborn dauntless forces of the enensy.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <p>At St. Prturs C'aurch on Sagd ty afternom the offer- ings of the congregation were given for the benefit cf St. George's Hone, in Brick-street, for Waifs and Strays. The off-rings took the fora of pounds of sugar, tea, coffee, rice, flour. ciirrants, -aisles, sago, &amp;c., the chil- dren of the schools and the adult members of the con- gregation walking in procession to the altar rails and handing the gifts to the clergy, the 114 V. E. R.Tarbuck, vicar, and the Rev. R. Hawley Sharon, the clerical or- . . ganng Eelretary at the Church of England Incorpora- ted Society for Provieing 1303103 for Waifs and Strays. The packets were placed inside the chancel rail, and the whole presented a very good show, all the parcels being wrapped in white paper. It was the first time a Similar service had been held in this church, but it is expected that in future one will be held annually. It was explained by Mr. Tartan* that the goods wool be sent to the home, and wou'd afford an excellent supply of necessaries for the eight boys maintained there for some months to come. rhe Rev. R. Hawley Sharps in- formed his hlarers that over 2,800 children were care- fully looked after in 86 homes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>Rev. J. Yonge and Our Reverses.</head>
        <p>The Rev. J. Yonge and Our Reverses.</p>
        <p>I At Wycliffe Coneregational Church en Sunday morn- ine the Her. J. Yonee preached an appropriate disoeurie on the text "Efe shall not be afraid of evil tidings : his heart in fixed, trusting in the Lord." Ps tlm 11.2, 7e. Efe remarked that we were becoming only toe sadly and painfully acquainted with "evil tidiegs " Se much so that no we daily watched the news from South Africa with feelines that wore very mueh akin tolerebneing and fear. The terrible war in which ear nation had be.. come involved held the supreme plane in all their think- ing and converse. It was casting a dark and dreadful shadow upon thin Christmas seasoo It would be the universally present soectre at all their festivals, in all their homes. Indeed in bernrs only toe terribly numer- ous it woeld blot out the Fettival cempletely, and sub- stitute the gloom and anguish of anxiety and bereave- tur_nt and desolation. In families exalted as well as lowly there was bitter lamentation this Christmas. Not alone had the war made havoc of the rank and file of our brave army, but, in proportion it tied made Null or even larner inroad upon the officers of every rank and degree. 'Tnis expetience was all the harder to bear, be- cause it was se utterly the reverie of what we had been enceuraeed to antioipete.The whole netien,comp +natively speaking, was led forth into this conflict in the confident expectation of au easy and speedy victory. Eveots had completely falsified all this ton easy confidence. We were finding to-rilv that our empire ie South Africa was seriously imp-rilled, and that we were involved in the severest and costliest warfare we had encountered since the Crimean campaign. This was indeed a startling cue of the happenings of the unexpected. Some even of their own numher confidently spoke of the South African expedition an a " pic.nic "—an outing fer the army, I from which they would learn useful military experience, I and soon satire with ranks untouched, colour: flying and triumphant music. But now they were learning— and the whole. nation was learning also—that the Went, capacity, resolircee and persistent courage of the defen- ders of the Transvaal Republic had been utterly mis- calculated ; and that England was called to grappler with a re Joubtanle enemy, who. although bound to be vanquished in the end, would he OVVICOM9 only at ternfie ceet and incalculable sacrifice of precious human life. Such were the distressieg facts of this present sea.yen. Some of them were find- ing them difficult to accept. The whole nation— erp ciAly after the astatindine news of this latent reverse would find them intensely hard to endure. In- deed, many OPUS of impatience were already showier.; themselves. There was a disposition arising in some quarters to apeek in terms of censure, and even ineig- nation, cenct ruing Generals in the field and adtrainistra- ti sn at hetne, terms which, to say the least, wore impetuously rash and might be altoenther unwarrant- able and unjust. It seemed to him that both Press and Pulpit at this juncture would serve a truly patriotic and enlightened end if they would comniend to the people the wisdom of a resolute self-control, and patient self- suppression, in view of the present experience. Con- tinental observers, none too friendly as a whole—had been constrained hitherto to note with approval, and even some measure of surprise, that the first tidings of reverse and difficulty were received by fair nation with calmness. 'They admittei it and the reception of such news had probably been other amongst them. Surely Our people would do well to maintain that calmness. They alsould not per- mit the grievous disappointment of General Buller'e repulse, even cemine as it did in repid succession to those ot Lord Metheen and General Getacre, and des- troying for the time the high hopes entertained of his'l especial skill and strategy. They stieuid riot snffer that reverse to overthrow the patience hitherto displayed. The time was manifestly one of severe national testing. It called for endurance, for c-urage, and for manifold sacrifice. At this Oeistrnaa s anon thee might have to deny thems-lveg—indeed ho thought they even oirght to deny them,:elves--pleasuree and indulgencies which in happier times were customary and perfectly legitimate, so that they might mitigate the Hufferinee and the needs nf tho,e who had 10-t husbands or sons in the battles. Beside's, it was not easy for right-thinking and right-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society.</head>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society.</p>
        <p>There was an average attendance at the meeting of this soliety on ounday afternoon. Mr. George Walling- ton pre&amp;ded and made a brief reference to the war in South kftica. ribilat admitting the serious losses we had suffered he said it was necessary thee*, ws should prosecute the war to a .4uccpsgful isiae in order to main- tain our preatige. He did not speak as a J•ngo but whatever the cost might 1:P, we must even'; ally suc- ceed. He paid a toucoing tribute to the womanlinrss of the Princess of Wales as displayed in her recent lett?r to the widows of the slain in war, and also referred to the remark of a soldier in South Africa that a Queen who had s4own such generosity and concern for her soldiers vvaa worth fighting for:—The address for the afternoon was delivered by Mr. Smiler, of Warrington, who based his remarhs on the text : "Toe Sow: of Man came into the world to seek and save th t which is lost."--Mr. G. Clay sang a solo from " The Messiah " entitled " The people that walked in darknesa." The service was also taken part in by Mr. Coates, Mr. Farrar and the Rev. J. Yong e.</p>
