        <p>' LINSEED COMPOUND' (Trade Mark) gives ex- pectoration without strain, 9d., 130. Sold by Chemists only.—Advt.</p>
        <p>The Dean of Hereford vrno was cordially received said that many year, ago he attended a meeting in Warringtora on the occasion of the formation of a federation of drffer- ent temperance societies in the town. He came at the re- quest of the Rev J J Laugh= who had a great deal to do with the formation of that union of societies. (Hear, hear.) He (the Dean) took some slight credit to himself for having a good many years ago started thiu idea of federation societies. It was something like 25 years ago when it occurred to him that union was strength. That occurred to a good many, yet they had not carried out the idea, and for many years there was a jsalous rivalry between different societies' whiz!' simpee retarded the progress of the great object they all had at heart. Now they found unions and federations everywhere. (Hear, hear.) Thereat raeeting at. the Queen's Hall, London, the other day, of representatives from the united societies throughout Great Britain was a great sign of progress. They had at length recognized the necessito of uniting against the common foe. (Applause.) He (the Dean) bad .to congratulate them upon having in the chair the head of the Corporation and the magistrates of the town. (Applause.) If they could get all the chief magistrates of England on the side of temperance tt set a grand example of total abstioence then they would be progressing towards victory. He once attended a meeting at the Mansion House, London, on which ocsasion there were forty teetetal mayors pre.ent. Hs did not know that they had ever yet had a Lord Mayor of London who was a teetotaler. (Laugh- ter.) When they got that they would almost have accomplished everything they could Oesire, because the Mayors were at the head of the magistrates and the magistrates had great power. (Hear, hear.) If the magistrates and the clergy wou'd support the temper.' ance workers they would have no difficulty in carrying out the necessary reforms. One of ths great qoestions in which the different societies were united was the question of Sunday closing. They believed that question was ripe for legislation and every effort would ee mede to convince our legislators that the time had come for something to be done in this direction. (Hear, hear.) The Mayor had alluded to the tied-house system. That was to his (the Dean's) mind, one of the greatest otstacles they had to contend with. Brewers formed limited liability companies and were not satisfied with being whohoale manufacturers of the drink, but owned' vast numbers of public-houses. He believed that in Liverpopl one firm owned about 250 eoto.+es. It was dis- tressing to see that men who were responsible for so much misers' were rewarded with titles and had henour shown to them. Speaking of the large brewery com- panies with so many shareholders he said that high divi- dends meant an increase in the sale of drink, that meant drunkenness, and drunkenness meant an Nero 'se of poverty, wretchedness, lunacy and crime, and the shareholders indirectly contributed to all that by their thirst for gold. (Hear, hear.) rh9 Mayor had touched upen the most serious cendition of things in South Africa which they were now deploring, and which had csst a deep gloom ever the country. They could scarcely think or talk of anything' else, but as the Mayor said they must net neglect affairs at home. (Hear, hear.) With regard to the reverse, some people found fault with the generals and others found fault with the Government, wople who knew nothing at all about the affairs being perhaps the most ready to criticise. 'These reTerses might possibly ho a judgment upon tha nation to make them snore humble and to make them less seekers of gold--(hear, hear)-- with lees greed of the mammon, When they came to consider the matter.the losses they admitted had bean terrible something like seven thousand in one way or other being placed hors de combat. If in the war now going on we were ultimately to lose 50.000 menotither killed, wounded or imprisoned, they would be perfectly horrified at sn trrribse a sacrifice of life. And yet if they reflected, the number was small when cempareti with the loss that was going on year by year owing to alcohol. Sixty thousand a year were killed directly by alcehol. The number of wounded was in- calculable. War would cost a grett deal, but little in comparison to the money spent on alcohol. He appealed to them to think over this matter seriously and see how they could help them to overthrow the greatest enemy in the land. (Applause.) The choir then sang '• Hear your country's call." ARCHDEACON MADDEN'S WARNING.</p>
        <p>Chd.aCOn MADDEN, who had a hearts' reception ob- served that that was the third time thia year he had spohen at temperance nieetinas ifi Warrinetln. It was, therefore. possible that they had had enough of a good thing. (Langht.r.) If thy had not the L-irci Mayor of London on their temperance p!atform thev!harl the reel e- STOP A COUGH IN ONE NIGHT. -- Take VENO'S LIGHTNING COUGH CURE. It stops an ordinary cough in one night, and cures chronic coughs, bronchitis, asthma, catarrh, influenza, and whooping cough rapidly. Its vast superiority over the different emulsions and ordinary cough mixtuns cannot be esti- mated. It has saved thousands of lives after they have been turned out of hesoitale. It is a new scientific remedy end-Tged by medical men. Ask for VENO'S LIGHTNING COUGH CURE, and see that you get it, price is. 44. and 24. 9J. per bottle (copyright). Sold by fol. lowinc., chemists :—ASHTON, KNOWLES, Warrington; WiLLBRIDGE, St. Helens; "'RAKE, Earlestown and Newton ; FOGGITT, Widnes; WEsToN, Runcorn ; and all chemists.</p>
        <p>To MOTHER9.—Mrs. Winslow's Soothing Syrup has been used for over fifty years by millions of mothers for their 3hildren while teeth/nu, with perfect success. It will relieve the poorsufferer immediately. It is pleasant to taste ; and produces natural quiet sleep, by relieving the child from pain And the little cherub awakes" as bright as a button."Of all Chemists Is lid per bottle.—Advt.</p>
