      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>-TEMPERANCE RALLY.</head>
        <p>-TEMPERANCE RALLY.</p>
        <p>SPEECHES BY THE DEAN OF HEREFORD AND ARCHDEACON MADDEN.</p>
        <p>There was a very successful temperance meeting in Parr Hall on Tuesday evening when a grand united rally in connection with the Warrington Temperance Federation was held. There was a fairly large attendance and excellent speeches were delivered. On the platform there were the Dean of Hereford (the Hon Mr Leigh), Archdeacon Madden, the Revs Canon Willis. F E Powell, W R Hart- wright, W A Knight, J T Schofield, T Bishop, John Yonge, C Finch, W Bracecamp, Ii G Roberts, James Davis, E T Reed, 0 11 Hocken, J J Langham, Dr Gornail, Councillor Ellison, and Mears Richard Storey, J Mounfield, S B Stringer, J Skelland, W Breugh, Henry Thornton, William Roberts, El Lloyd Jones, Thomas Dale, I' T Winskill, .T G Thompson, John Hallows, and J Ditchfield. The RECTOR having offered a prayer, the choir, which was under the conductorship of Mr W Brough, very efficiently sang "Now pray we for our country," a fine composition of appropriate sentiment, Mr. Wilkes presided at the organ. THE Et&amp;YOE'S SPEECH.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR said they were met under sadder conditions than had fallen to the lot of many of them during their Live'. The unfortunate trouble in South Africa as to the cause of which they were not concerned at all that night followed by the reverses of the last few days, had pro- duced a feeling all evsr the country of extreme sorrow and regret, and he had no doubt that that feeling had to some extent affected the attendance at that meeting. Sad as this condition of things was, he thought they would do well to take to themselves the sage advice of the Duke of Devonshire, believing as they did that their cause weal ultimately triumph, although it might be at a very bitter cost, it was their duty to mind their own basiness at home, and see that domestic altars were net neglected while they were embroiled in war abroad. (Hear, hear,) He need not apologize for appearing as chairman of that meeting. (Hear, hear.) As one who had been a life teetotaler, and took an inteuse abd deed interest in everything that con- cerned the welfare ot temperance in all its phases, and for the moment as chief citizen of the town, he con- sidered he was in his right place on that occasion. (Applause.) There had been times when he had felt a greater sense of freedom and less responsibility, and under those circumstances they would not expect from him perhaps the usual outspoken kind of thing they had been accustomed to in days gone by. (Laughter.) He ventured to say that black as the prospect of the tem- perance party looked a short time age,it had neeer been brighter than it was to-day. He remembered a few years back when tint remarkable development occurred in regard to the proprietary interests in connection with the great drink trade, when many of the largest firms ceased to be private concerns, and beceme limited liabil- ity companies, he was awfully afraid of what the result of that spread of interest would be for instead of having a few capitalists which would have been the case if it had been kept to its original proprietors they had shareholders' lists of enormous magnitude,including all classes of society, and in regard to some be wondered they had not blistered the pages on which they had been written. (Hear. hear.) They had now to contend with so far as money was concerned— which was the most powerful interest they had— a widespreadiug influence, but on the other hand, they had recently an important Commission sitting which had produced two remarkable reports and he said, without hesitation that he was pertectly satisfied to accept the majority report, even for a few years, and work upon it and when he saw, as he did, tee joinine together of all sections of the temeierance party for the purpose of press- ing upon Parliament legislation founded upon Lord Peel's report with great prospects of success he thought they had reason to be thankful ard take courage. (Ap- plause.) He recognized that this trade was a very difficult one to manage. He had personal friends in the trade, men whom be bad worked alongside of in public affairs, and who were honourable and straightforward men, and from what they bad told him he knew that this trade was enormously difficult te manage, and that those men were anxious to comply with the requirements of the statute and keep their business properly. Those men would have his cordial and hearty support in the conduct of their business. But owing to the enormous competition it was impossible—be did not say in War- rington particularly for it was not worse but a good deal better than others—tor some licensees to make a liveli- hood if they carried on teeir trade within the strict limits of the law. It was that kind of thing that Om+ wanted to abolish at any cost. (Applause.) He would not intrude between the audience and the speakers of the evening any longer bet would now call upon the Dean of Hereford to address the nieeting. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>THE DEAN OF lIERFFOND AND THE HEVER-SEE-IN SOUTH AFRICA.</p>
      </div>
