 ...4;:•:"........:•::::::::".45.:*:%*:.:',0:,, --",.:-::::-:::.::::"...t.:.•:•:',.".f.f.:',..:).:*::::.•.-;;•.::::::„.;;;•;•:.f:•:.;•...•....:.,..::::,...• ",;?::::':!,:',•;:::::::**::.:;if•::::::;•:'-'4"&lt;f,-,;.-:•::',::::::::•:,•.•,-.•.::::::.-..y..2.:•••.....7..!..,..,,,,,,.......:..:,....,..„......,. ' /•,,•••••:•:::,:f.;_ • • --- • .-- 4.lfirdliit, al</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <p>siastical bead of the Church, a strong, manly, robust eommon-sense total abstainer, and every curate ought to follow such an able lead. (Applaute.) Alluding to the speeeh which had been delivered by the M iyor, he said he did not think the responsibility of his office bad in any way taken away from him the freedom of his u:terances; snd he hoped it never would. A man's responsibility could be no excuse for him holding his tongue when there was a crying evil that was damaging all his fellow citizens. He objected to the principle &amp;together. The fact was that position had its responsibilities, and paid- tiara gave weight to one's utterances, and a man in a responsible position, whe spoke from years of experience is his town was the right man to speak out. Drink was doing a dastardly wrong to the people and [no Mayor lied, thereforeee right to speak out in the name of the citizees and eo condemn evil in the name of his G.A. (Applause.) It was difficult for the nation to bring in a verdict against the drink because so many had. a share in the profits. The strength of any countre lay in its moral stamina, in. its righteousness and in everything that made for the moral and spiritual well-being of the people. They bad seen great nations, great and mighty natione, perish, and not from foes without but from the °nerve- inginfluencee of prosperity that was net used for God's glory, but wee spent in vicieue, sinful anti immoral ways; and God who reigned above was no respecter of nations. Nations had no hereafter. They must be judged in time. .and God's judgment angels were peetil- care, war, famine. God help thin to repent of their sins, to humiliate themselves under the miehty hand of God. The nation that spent £150,000.000 a year in degrading the manhood and wornanhooi of Eeglaed, and only £2,000,000 in sp:.eadiee, the glorious Gospel of Jesus Christ was hardly worth the name of a Christian nation. (Applause,) For the sake of a few, God was merciful, and might they remember that the enemy England had to fear was not a entinental nation but their own household, and if they lose out of the present war in South Africa purified, cleansed and sanctified, and put away the unclean thing, they would be more than ever the home of the free, the home of the righteous, the home of those who lovee Geed and who did their duty everywhere' (Aeplauee.) They must not blame the House of Coanneus because they had had no temperance leeisletion but they must remember that the House of Commons exired by the fiat of the country. Their representatives were what teev made them and wore no better and and no worse than thernselvi s. If they wanted temper- ance reform they must elect men pledged to sueport it. (Elear, hear.) He believed temperance workers had been to blame in the past because they had not been satis- fied to grate) what they could get in the way of temper- ance legislation. (Hear, hear) He would like to see Sunday closing entirely, but if be could not eFt licensed bermes closed the whole day he would be prepared to accept an instalment of reform in this direction. He was prepared to mink his own individual opinions in order that they should get rmie leeislation. (Hear. hear.) They must get what they could and then agitate for more. If they were earnest in this matter let them begin with themselves, and each do what teey could to pennote temperance. If they would st sere on the side of thmperance the country would come round to them, and as slavery was abolished in America, an even wore slavery in England would he abol,shed.that of the drink and sinfulness, and the people would march out of Eeyp,ian bondage happy and free. (Cheers.) Tlest would lift the fallen, and tell them of Jesus the mighty to save. Let them never forget that God was mercbing on.. (Apnlause).</p>
        <p>The Rev. J. J. LANGHAM, in proposing a vote of thanks to the Dean and ArmnFacon, congra'ariatel the town upon having at last a Mayor who was both able and willing to take the chair at a meeting Ella the pre- Free,. (Applause.) It looked, provided they only waited lona enough, that they would get most of what they wanted. reey might learn some lessons from Canada in its conduct with referenc', to the drink question. The colonies had set them an excellent example in the mat- ter, and they wished England to come into line with the rest of the Empire on that great question. (Applause.) The Rev. JOHN Yorroz seconded the motion, and re- marked that he felt it to be a great pleasure to do so. They had all immensely •enjoved the proceedings,and the vote would not te a mere matter of form. (Hear, bear.) Not long ago.in company with the Mayor.he had the un- usual privilege for him of A t tend in g the Parish Church and listening to a remarkable sermon from the Rector. Be hoped that snme of these days it would be a great gratification to him and many others, if it could come about, that the Rector would listen to a sermon from him. (Applause.) He could assure him of this—that he would receive a most respectful welcome. He thanked the Rector most cordially for the public reference he made to himself in the course of the evening in question. He referred to the vrmon of the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <p>Rectrir's for the reason that in the course of it the Rector properly called atteotiln—aod he hoped very, influentially—to the question of Vie number of licenses in that town. He felt grateful to hilt for the retnartcs he made, because, although it was a kind of thing some of them as humble Di,senters had been say- ing many times in the past, they hAd not been listened to by "the powers that be ;" but; when the Rector spoke upon the matter perhaps he would b 3 listened to. He r•ferred to the disproportion between the number of licensed houses and the population'. He hoped his re- marks would receive attention in the proper quarter. He felt perfect'y sure that if the magistrates would have the pluck to deal with the question they would find a large majority of people at their back. It was a question that should be dealt with, ir. had been trifled with too long. '(lear, heir.) The magistrates had been afraid of the interests of a great trade and the sloner they got rid of that unworthy fear the better for the interests of the t iwn and the promotion of temperance. He hoped that come bold step would b 3 taken t) bring the nuniber of licer.sea into more reasonable proportion to the re- quirements of the town. (Applause.) rhe motion was heartily aurae to.</p>
        <p>The DEAN replied, aud-prc;plsPd a vote of thank 3 to the Mayor for presiding over the meeting. He hoped the Mayor might eunceed in reducing the number of license 3 in Warrington. _ _</p>
        <p>Tit? motion was seconded by the Rev. F. E. POWELL and heartily agreed to. The MAYOR, in reply, said the Dean of Hereford had given him a big order. He did not know whether he was equal to carry it out. If he might make a sugges- tion he would throw out an invitation to the two brew- ing firms in the town to do what bad ban done in Mr. tningbam. (Hear, hear.) If the two big firms in War- rington would come forward and surrender a certain number of lic.mses he thought they might take it for 2rauted that the apOestion would be gratefully aceep- teJ. That was a very Fee plsition for him to take up. and they must trust him to do his best. They had been a long while in obtaining a tfe'otai Mayor, and they must not now want the pendulum to swing the other way. (Laughter). So far as reasonable and slasonable opportunity preiented itself of doing anything for the temperance cause, he could aPure them that it would net be missed. (Applause). The meeting. concluded with the singing of the National Anthem</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>'.tHE NEW FILENOit</head>
        <p>'.tHE NEW FILENOit</p>
        <p>TJFIE APION . This successful and highly popular rern,dv, as em- ployed in the Continental Hospitals by Hicord Rostan. Job e t Velpeau, and others, surpasses everything hitherto emoloyed for impurity of the blood, spots, tdotch,,s, p Sins and swell- ings of the joints, kidney, bla.ider. and urinary diseases stricture, discharges. piles, gravol, pains in the back, rheu- matism. gout, exhaustion, eleeplessuess,nervous and physical etc. Therapion is prepared in three different forms Nos. 1,2, and 3, acoording to diseases for which intended. Full particulars send stamped adaressed envelope for pamphlet to --</p>
        <p>MR. R. JOHNSON, 43, trOLFORD-BQUIRR. DINPON, W.C.</p>
