      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>WARRINGTON SAVINGS BANK. A Successful Year.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON SAVINGS BANK. A Successful Year.</p>
        <p>One of the most useful and flourishing institu- tions in the town is the Warrington Savings Bank, which was established in the year 1818 and certi- fied under the Act of 1863. At the annual meeting on Tuesday, the repor; snowed ttlat solid prugr,:sA bad been made during the year. There were pre- sent Mr William Long (chairmen), Mr John Geddes (vice-chairman), Alderman Roberts, JP., Messrs J Hepherd, W Tinsley, and C Jackson Holmes (trustees). the Rev Canun Willis, J.P. Whittle, J Yonge and W H Drummond, and Messrs T Birchall, J Dewhurst, D W Finney and J Earrison (managers), and Mr Thomas Tunstall (actuary).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>ANNUAL REPORT.</head>
        <p>THE ANNUAL REPORT.</p>
        <p>The ACTUARY read toe report of the Committee of Management venial stated thet the committee bad much pleasure in draceing attention to the very satis- factory progress of the business of the bauk. During the year 873 accounts had been opened, making the total number of deposisors 12,352. 23,808 transactions were recorded as cernpared witn 23,001 last year, show- ing an increase of 804. The increase in the amount owing to depositors in the same period was £10,607 24 3d, making the total amount now due to them £345,916 5s 4d, in add tion to which there wore invest- ments on their hehalt to the extent of £7,690 23 6i- -21 per cent. consolidated stock. The acise's ot the bank were £351,348 133 4i, showing a surplus of £5,43? Bs, after deducting the whole amount due to the deposi- tors. The National Debt Ceramics', 'wore held on behalf of the institution a sum of £348,470 93 33, which was considerably in excess ot the total liabilities. The continued steady increase in the depositors' accounts and in the number ot transactions, was a most gratify- ing feature of the work of the bank. The greater pro- portion of the transactions were of small amourate,which went to show that the bank was taken advautage of by the class of depositors for whose bz-aelit the institution was estab!ishee in the year 1818. "nem wore 4,970 de- positors, whose eccounts do not exceed £l. The CIIAIRMAN in moving that the report be approved and copies transmitted to the National Debt Commis- sioner and to the Committee ot Inspection, said there were one or two itemt which shooed that during the year the bank had been (icing useful though quiet work. They had nothing very etartling to speak et, but they hoped the action of the b,ina had been for thE, good of the town. One of the most useful items in the report, as showing what the bank was doing was that which showed that they were drawing tho smaller depositors around them. There were nearly 5,000 depositors whore accounts did not exceeu £l. 01 coerso they would be glad to see them rise, and ia the natural order of things they would rise, for these depositors represented fresh people. The bank was thus cloiug a satisfactory work in the direction of inculcating habits of savicg,especiall among the poorer class of people. When driving through the town ien Monday he had his attention called to a number of children with bundles,and was told that they were taking them to the pawnshops to narrow money upon them for their parents. That seemed to him to he a very lamenteble thing. If those people would only bring their mooey to the bank and make a start they would cease such dreadful hatits. He hoped therefore that everything that could be aorie either by those con- nected with the bank or terough the medium of the Press to personally represent to the working classes the value of thrift would b done, and then, he thought, Warrington would b • happier and better for the change in:the habits of its people.</p>
        <p>Mr. GEDDES ,Se2tllaed . and thr motion waeg carried. THE COMMITTEE'S SERVICSS.</p>
        <p>Mr. FINNEY moved "tat the rep.-irt: of the Cnm- mittee of Managemen; be A.pproved and that this meet- ing acknowledge the valuable services rendered to the institution by the gentlemen acting on the comonitze during the past year and tenders its bet thanks to them." He said the Chairman had already mentioned one class of people who, it was desirable should bring their little savino to the hink. There wa3 another class who should be qaiy encouraged to use the bank in- stead of wasting heir mosey in putilic-houses. They were the labouring men and carters. The CHAIRMAN : lam afraid that whlt I referred to is the cause of that.</p>
        <p>Mr. FINNEY said he weuld be delighted if they could do something in that direction. He thou2ht the gentle- men who composied the Committee of Management deserved their sincere and hearty thanks. Mr. DEWBURST in seconding, complimented the staff on the manner in which they perfo:med their duties and referred to the civility which was always shown to de- positors. Tnere were occasions when people were shy of entering a bank, and the proper thief; then to do was to treat them so that they would have no diffidence about going a second time. It was a great sin for a man who earned good wagiqi not to make prorlsiot Uir the future. There was not only nis own future to consider, but the fature of his wife and farnilp. They all knew of heart- rending cases in which if the man had bi.'en more provi- dent he would have been perfectly independent in his latter days and would not have bad to ectk relief from the rates.</p>
        <p>The vDte Qs cordially approved. The Rev. W H DRUMMOND replied ou bt half of the committee. RE-APPOINTMENT OF CHAIRMAN AN D VICE-CHAIRMAN.</p>
        <p>The Rev. JOHN YONGE moved the re-appointment of Mr. Long as chairman and Mr. Geddes as vice-chairman and said those who were privileged to sit on that Board knew perfectly well how devoted those two gentlemen were to the interests of the bank and how valuable their services were. They hoped the time would be far Ws- tent when either of them would retire from office. Ho would like to add-a word to what had been said regard- ing tbe desirability of poor people and others who were net very poor cultivating the habit of thrift rather more than the report seemed to indicate. Looking at the statistics they would see that the number of new accounts for the last year was something like 50 leas than in the preceding year, whereas the number of closed accounts was rattier more. That indicated that there was not quite as much thrift practised during the last twelve months. It was very deairable that the attention of the public should be called ta the virtue of the bank and the privileges which it afforded. The R-v. GANQN WILLIS seconded and the motion was agreed t Mr. LONG, in accepting the position of chairman, said he would have been rather gratified bad there been a change. When he first took the position ho expressed the opinion that the management ought to be change- able, and that no one gentleman ought to occupy the position of chairman year after year. To some extent .ne had modified that opinion, but he consiiered it would be a good thing if he and Mr. Geddes changed positions now and then. Mr. GEDDES thanked them very much for the honour they hhd conferred upon him. It had always been to him a great pleasure to attend the bank and do all be could for it. It had been a pleasure also to work with Mr. Long. He hoped ho would retain the chairmanship for many years to come.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>COMMITTEE OF MANAGEMENT.</head>
        <p>COMMITTEE OF MANAGEMENT.</p>
        <p>Mr. H3PHERD moved that the following managers be elected to j du the trustest on the Committee of Manage- ment for tee ensuing year :—Tho Revs. Richard Greenall, .1 Yongo, and Vt. H. Drummond, and Ws:ma. T J Harrison, J Peers, and T Birch&amp;ll in seconding sp Ace appreciatively of the work of the hawk, and trusted it would continue to encourage thrift among the people. The res.lution wait Approved.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>ZW ILANaGERS,</head>
        <p>ZW ILANaGERS,</p>
        <p>Th,, Actuary reported that the „only alteration in the list of trustees and maxas,,cra was canoed by the death of two maaagers, Messy'. loornai Ea.nde. and Samuel Lees. The follouing, Aditional managers were ap- pointed Revs. E It Tarhuck, F A Screeton, J a G Hollinquortn, A S Cameron, a. F White, T Bishop, and Mesits. Fl E Ramome, It J Gourley, C Chorley, W Richardsen and C Bunch..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>MOUES TO THE STAFF.</head>
        <p>MOUES TO THE STAFF.</p>
        <p>Tho CHAIRMAN, in proposioz a vote of thanks to the staff said he, couid spyak of their uniform go,d conduct and painitaking applico.tion. Finm the ac nary down to the junior cleik they all had the intercsls of the balk they servedheart. It was very pleasing to be able .to bear WittleSS to that.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>SIIGGE,,TION.</head>
        <p>A SIIGGE,,TION.</p>
        <p>Thft Rev. W. H. Densmore]) Boggs:AM that the management of the bank should encourage life insurance among the people it had husiness with. Bie experience on the Bard of Guardians had led him to see the necesaity of the average man kineuting, himself. Ile had known widows of men who had earned £2 a weA apply- ing for relief within a month of hie. burial simply because the man had not insured himself. The RECTOR agreed with the principle, but did not think it w.'ud some within the province of the hank. The ACTUARY s4ld the bank had no powe^ to do any- thing directly. They could 'distribute pamphlets on be- half of any install:ion. On the mcr ion of tho R,v. JOHN YONGEL seconded by the Rev. W. Fi. DnuarmoNn, the cons,deration of the matter we reforroo to the C:lntnittes of Management. The miwting cooe:uded with a vote of thanks to the Chairai4n..</p>
        <p>LINSE D CO rAI.POUN D ' fo” fuld Colds allay* jitl.Lllve3 I.,amediatt</p>
