      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>HIS COUNTRY AND HIS PEOPLE.</head>
        <p>HIS COUNTRY AND HIS PEOPLE.</p>
        <p>Mr. Monks on the War.</p>
        <p>There was a large attendance in Wycliffe Hall on Mon- day evening when a lecture on "Paul Kruger : his coun- try, his people, his neighboucs, and war with Great Britain" was given by the Rev. J. H. Riddette, of Hay- dock, who was for three years an "Outlander." The chair was taken by Mr. F. Monks, J.P., C.C. The Gnus/men who was cordially received, said that in view of the present condition of affairs in the Trans- vaal, he was sure that whatever Mr. Riddette bad to say srould be exceedingly interesting. With reference to the war itself be imagined that they had all made up their minas that it Ilea to be seen through thoroughly. (Ap- plause.) For himself he believed that if ever there was a just war this was a just war. (Hear, hear.) He must protest against what he called the snivelling and crying out of some people because they had lost a few thousand men. If they went to war they must expect that there wouli be a large number of men killed and wounded. Thar was the inevitable result. If was the case even dowuto the lad at scncol. If he had to fight he would not win by simply bluffing and crying over what he could not help. He had got to stand up and beat his a.ntagonist if he possibly cou.d. If he could not.then be was beaten. If Great Britain were beaten in this war he thought they might bid good-bye to the prosperity of the country. There was no question that there was a very strong feeling of antagonient on the Continent against this country, and he believed himself it was due very largely to jealousy and mis-understanding. There was DO country, he might be permitted to sayealthough he was an Englishman, as far as he bad been able to judge in his travelling and reading, there had been no country, no government and no people so anxious to give good government to the world as were the English people. (Applause.) What was it that they week- confronted with ? fhey were confronted with a rebellion as great as any they had ever had to meet by a people who would have been wiped off the face of the earth entirely but for the protecting arm of the Govern- meet of this country. (Hear, hear.) The Dutch people in the Transvaal awe in the whole of Africa had an ambition to clear out the Englishmen altogether and to , govern Africa on their own lines. It was only necersary for theist to go to Java and the other Dutch coloniee to see that the Dutch Government was as corrupt and as nen-progressive a government as they could find on the face of the earth, and if they gained their ends in South Africa they would set up a series of republics as corrupt eud as unstable as the republics of South America. What they, (Great Britain) were fighting for was to protect their own countrymen, anci he had co sympathy with any Englishman who was net prepared to do the best he could to give that protection to every Englishman, wherever he might be, that be was en- titled to expect at their hands. (Hear, hear.) He had nothing more to say except that he for one, as far as be could, would do what he could to make this war a suc- cess in the interests of good government and their supre- macy, belteving as he aid that it would be ultimately of benefit to the whole of Africa, and not least to them- selves. If the war tended to knock cut the blatanc Jingoism of some people it would not be a bad thing, ana if it brought teem to realise what war really meant, because the present generation of young men did not know anything at all about it, if it brought re their minds a conception of what war meant in a country seven or eight thousend miles away that in itself would be no slight advantage to the country. (Hear, hear.) He was satisfied that this war would unite the country. There were many worse things than war. There was bad government. (Hear. hear.) If any man lived in a country where the Government was as corrupt as it had been in the Transvaal, he was certain he would value the more hiehly the good government they were blessed with in this country. (Hear, bear.) They had a tough job before them. Let them not lose heart and let them not lore their heads. (Applause). The Rev. J. H. RIDDIUTH then proceeded with his lecture,lwhich was illustrated by a series of remarkably fine pictures. The first picture shown was that of Paul Kruger, the President of the Transvaal Republic. Paul Kruger, said Mr. Riadette, is in many respects a re- markable man; he rules over one of tbe most remarkable, if also one of toe smallest and least progressive nations of this world. Massive in build, heavy in feature, with smallish head, surmounted by high shoulders,that stoop somewhat under the weight of his 74 years ; such is Paul Kruger as he strikes the eye. As a nation the Boers could claim high lineage and ancient blood,many among them being able to trace their eltncent !rem some of the best men ever known to the world. The Cape belonged to Holland at one time, but 100 years ago became a British poisession. Whilst English boys were learning their tables Paul Kruger was using the rifle and he shot big game before he reached his teens. He shot his first lion at le. Altheugh several times over a millionaire,he lived like a peasant, primitive, simple and quiet,smoked and drank coffee a great (dal. His political ambition was represented in a view wheel showed him putting a crown on his head. It had Lecome more and more evi- dent to the English that the President had aimed to con- stituting lemself the head of a United Du:ch Soutii Africa. By means of a series of fine pictures the diversified physical features of the country were indicated, " the oppressive vastness of the mountaius "being apparent, and one :could easily understand the difficulties the British soldiers are gallantly facing in beating the Boers from the mountain fastnesses. The most regretful inci- dent in the political history of the Trensvaalt said Mr. Riddette, was that Swaziland happened to be ceded to the Boers. The natives formally accepted the transfer, but secretly resented it. Mr. Riddette then proceeded with the history of the Dutch in the Transvaal, referring to the Greet T,:ek, when 6,000 Dutch moved into the Interior. Why did they wish to escape from British jurisdiction? He quoted David Livings:tone, who said the Boers felt aggrieved by their supposed ICCitS in the emancipation of their Hottentot slaves, and determined to erect theneselver into a republic under which they might pursue, without molestation, the proper treatment of the black. It is needless to add that " the proper treatment of the black "always contained in it the cseential principle of slavery, compulsory unpaid labour. Mr -Riddette pointed cut that this " great venture into the unknown "as he called the Trek, resulted in the Boers becomine exeert in the manipulation of the rifle, as they had to contend with the wild beasts in thei r march. The diversity of type presented by the Boors was next commented upon, as were their principal characteristics. Coining dawn to more recent events the audience were aroused to enthusiasm by a fine picture of Colonel Baden-Powell, the gallant defender of Maleking, who, said Mr. Riddette, is pursuing, under terrible disadvantages, his tack with a resourceful dash and courage. Some features of the Orange Free Slate were referred to, the peeple being more progresieve and better educated than the people of the Tranevaal, and Mr. Riddette took occasion to remark, that if President Sceyn anti Dr L oyds could have been transported to some distant laed, we should have got through our troubles without resort to war. The difficulties Kruger bad to contend with were that he was trying to apply seven- tsenth century adininiatative principles to the require- ments of a complex modern system. There was a pro- gressive element in the Raed,but it was a small minority. If their counsels bad prevailed, we should have had a different state of things to-day, and it might have been tee best thing for South Attica in the end. Mr Riddetto thou described toe arraneemente under which the natives worked at the Kimberley mines, an arrange- ment that worked out to the advantage both of the natives thrmsalves and of their employers. The four out-standing grievances in the present trouble were the high duties on the necessaries or life, the franchise, the administration of the law, and education. fie then passed on to deal with the military organisation of the Boers, which he described. The Boers had the faintest conception of the force of the British army. The victory which, unfortunately for themselves' they gained at Ildaeubinand the events which followed upon that day had done more than anything else in Boer history to give the Beers a wrong idea that they might clear out the British from South Africa.</p>
        <p>The views wlaich were cxhibited by Mr. Wilkinson, included pictorial representations of Pretoria, Johannes- burg, Kimberley, Mafekieg, Majnba Hill, Ladysmith, Bloemfontein, Durban, etc. ; also types of the British army and portraits of the leading military offieere. A most interanting and instructive evening was brought to a close by an acknowledgment of the Chair- man's and the Lecturer's servicos.The Rev. JOAN YONGE said he was sure they had all profited by the lecture.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
