      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>Nation and the War.</head>
        <p>The Nation and the War.</p>
        <p>Sir,—The concentrated attention of this vast Empire is focussed upon S,)uth Africa. Toe gallantry of our Sailors and Soldiers has been beyond all praise, but we have not carried all before us, and our reverscs, not to say defeats, have been numerous. We are a brave and a great nation, but first and bremost,we are a Christian nation. While our Generals are planning and our forces are fighting and suffering, are we placing God first, and do we remember that Be alone gives the victory? It is a painful fact, that as yet, no day has been set apart for national humiliation and prayer ; our national sins call for repentance, and at this critical juncture in the history of the Empir?, surely our right position would be to recognise and honour that God, win alone has made us what we are, and who alone sustains us in our high position. We have shuwn our active and sym- pathetic help on behalf of the families of the killed and wounded, and we have forgotten that our old battle cry was "For God, and Merrie England." I know of many whose hearts would be rejoiced to hear that our clergy and ministers were taking some definite action in thi • matter, and were combining together, to ask God to guide and direct our Generals, to nerve our forces, and to give victory to our arms, and I should rejoice still more, if by a day of national humiliation and prayer, we showed the world. that it was still, " Gcd First."— Believe me, yours very truly, AGNES E. WESTON.</p>
        <p>Royal Sailors' Rest, Portsmouth, 20:h December 1899.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>Alleged Leakage of Brandy at the</head>
        <p>Alleged Leakage of Brandy at the</p>
        <p>Hospital.</p>
        <p>Sir,—ln a lengthy bat somewhat involved defence of the matron and nurses of the Runcorn Hospital respect- ing the alleged leakage of brandy at that place, a corres. pondent "Murdock McKenzie, L.F.P.5.," through the medium of a contemporary, practically admits a leakage of 7s. 31 out of an amount of £3 ss. 9d. (slightly over lOper cent), and then goes on to commit the same fault he attributes to the Health Corn- ittee of the Urban District Council. After stating what be calls the facts of the ease he adds : "The price of a pint of brandy is 4183, and 13 pints at that price totala £2 18s 61 Where has the remaining 7s 3d gone? It certainly did not arrive at the hospital. As the public have been placed in possession of a state- ment which is slanderous and libelous in its imputation, the Council ought to see that the public are put in pos- session of the whole truth. The public, perhaps,will be surprised to learn bow supplies are sent to the hesp'tal. The orders are sent to Mr. Percival, who makes the pur- chases, hands them over to whoever happens to be pass- ing the hospital, to take them to that institution. The brandy is in a bottle, the cork being unsealed, and the matron has frequently complained that the bottles do not contain the full quantity. At one tilllll the medical men ordered bottles of brandy or whisky, as tbe case might be, and there the bottles were sealed, but Mr Percival stated that the Council objectEd to bottles being or- dered and that pints were to be substituted." He concludes his lengthy letter with a request for "an apology or explanation of Mr. Dean." It clearly appears then that the worthy doctor admits a leakage of 7s 3d ; but be does not stop therm, an shown above, but places an equally "slanderous and libelous imputa- tion" upon Mr. Yercival or his messnagers as that which he alleges has been imputed by the Health Com- mittee upon the matron and nurses. To be consistent should not th,3 writer apologise for his offence, er prove where the leakage does occur, and not stop at so illogical a conclusion.—Yours faithfully, "MACK II."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>Christmas.</head>
        <p>Christmas.</p>
        <p>Sir,—A few more risings and settings of the winter sun, a few more brief and cloudy days, and 1899 will be, as Young says," With the years beyond the fl iod." It came lido our world in the gloom and stillness of mid- night skies, with a calm and solemn dignity befitting its eventful mission ; and similar phenoleiena will mark its entrance inie) the eternal past. And when the curtain falls and the last chime has died away, where will be the hopes and fears, the joys and sorrows, and the am- bition and the crimes of men that chequered its career ! All, all will have gone for ever to ferm, it may be, de- termining elements in human destiny beyond the grave. And dim and gradually fading memory alone will remain to remind us, regretfully or otherwise, of the part we played in the affairs of life during its course. But in the midst of the dreariness and desolation of nature, and the shattered dreams of human hearts that mark the year's last stage, there comes a day so blessed that devout men turn away from their bleak surroundings to contemplate its holy memories and traditions with a quiet joy. That day is Christmas so loved and longed for in every English home from the palace to the cottage. When its morning opens and bright orbs still look down on the quiet earth we think of the little company of Magi who, under the guidance of a wondrous star, journeyed from a: distant land to the Jewish capital in search of Him who was born King of the Jews But His birth place was not in the palace of Herod but in a lowly Khan in Bethlehem, a mountain village six miles souda from Jerusalem. And thither led by their heavenly conductor the "wise men" found "the Desire of Nations" and reverently laid at His feet costly offerings typical of his glorious offices as King and Saviour of the world. Nor to those strangers alone were his comineand whereabouts revealed. A few shepherds on the neighbouring hills were, about the seine time, favoured with one of the most wonderful revelations that God has ever yet made to mankind. While scan- ning the starry heavens, as David had done from the very same hills eleven hundred years before, their at- tention was suddenly arrested by the voice of an Angel who announced, "Unto you is barn this day in the city of David a Savious, which is Christ the Lord." And so momentous and far reaching in the sight of Heaven were "the glad tidings" that alnultitude of the celestial host sang, "Glory to God in the highest, and on earth peev, good will toward men." Echoes of that glorious song have floated over the world for the last nineteen centuries, bringing comfort and joy to many a weary soul distressed by the ceaseless turmoil and strife of men. But the Heavenly Kingdom for which they prayed and hoped has not yet appeared on earth. Rulers and statesmen in all lands seem to care more for their own than for the glory of God ; and peace, like Noah's dove, can find no place among the troubled nations whereom to rest the soles of her feet. Indeed the trend of the so-called Christian world appears to the growing more and more in favour of war as the years roll on. 'A German military officer has computed that not less than thirty millions of civilizod and cbris- tian men have perished in war during the century now closing And to-day our hearts are stirred with serrow by the long lists of killed and wounded men supplied by the daily press from South Africa, where Eng:ish and Dutch Christians are destroying one another with terriele ferocity ! Nor will the advent of Christmas with all its hallowed and peaceful memories put a stop to the dreadful carnage. On those distant battlefields in all probability the roar of cannon and the cries of wounded men will mark the birthday of the Prince of Peace who came to save, and not to destroy, human life. But here at home, far from the reach of shot and shell, many of these who cheered the poor soldif re on their way to the deadly conflict, will keep the holy day by a partial worship and the singing of Charles Wesley's noble hymn : Hark I the herald angels sing,— Glory to the new-born King; Peace on earth, and mercy mild ; God and sinners reconciled.</p>
        <p>All this will, doubtless. he trying to those who are looking for the literal return of the Lord to assume His Kingly functions. But gloomy as the prospect., cer- tainly is at present,from a human point of view,believers will continue to hope an, feeling that sooner or later their expectations will be realivd far beyond their most sanguine imaginings. They know that the darkest night Is often the prelude to a bright lovely morning ; and it may be that the war-clouds that are now massing over the world are indications of the nearing dawn of the blessed time when the Dayspring from on high shill b, manifested in all His glory. His first coming was the theme of prophecy from the earliest age.; ; and His appearing as the Prince of Peace to bring in everlasting righteousness forms one of the most absorbing subjects of Divine Revelation. He who was once the lowly Child of Bethlehem and " The Man of sorrows and acquainted with aria " sisll assuredly come again as a Glorious King whose reign on earth will verity the re- frain of the angels' song—'`Glory to God in the highest, and on earth peace, goodwill towards men."—Yours truly, lONA. 16th December, 1899.</p>
        <p>Cheshire Agricultural Society.—Next Year's</p>
        <p>Show.</p>
        <p>The adjourued annual meeting of the Cheshire Agricultural Society was held at the Royal Hotel, Crewe, on Monday afternoon, Colonel France-Hay- hurst presiding. A representative deputation from Congleton, headed by the Mayor, gave the society an invitation to hold their next show at Congleton. This was unanimously accepted.</p>
        <p>CHRISTMAS PRABENT3.—W. T. WILLIAMS'S Choice Assortment of Fur neaklets, Muffs, Glotes, Fancy Handkerchiefs Umbrellas, Fans, &amp;c. Sankey-street and Legh-street, Wirrington —Advt.</p>
