      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>EXAMINER FRIDAY, DECEMBER 22. 1899</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER FRIDAY, DECEMBER 22. 1899</p>
        <p>EARLESTOWN &amp; NEWTON.</p>
        <p>EARLESTOWN WESLEYA.N SUNDAY SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>On Sunday afternoon last the Rev. F. E. Miller delivered a very interesting address to the members of the Esrlestown Wesleyan Sunday school class, ln Temperance. His remarks were very pithy, and were attentiv9ly listened to by the young men present. P.S.A.</p>
        <p>The usual weekly meeting of the Earlestown and District P.S.A.. was held in the Co-operative Hall on Sunday. Mr. J. Smith presided, and there was a gnod attendance. The principal speaker was Mr. W. H. Dale, and recitations were given by Mr. Robin- son. Vocal sblections were submitted by Miss Stronach and:Mrs. Latimer presided at the piano.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>POLICE CASES.</head>
        <p>POLICE CASES.</p>
        <p>At the Earlestown Police Station on Monday morning, before Mr. C. B. F. Borron, Francis Holleran was charged with being drunk and disor- derly on Sunday and was fined 2; 61 and costs.— P.C.'s Clarke and Leming supported the charge.—On Tuesday morning, at the same place before Mr. Borron, Martha Saunders, for being drunk and dis- orderly the previous day. was fined 2s 6d and costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>HOLIDAYS AT EAILLESTOWN.</head>
        <p>THE HOLIDAYS AT EAILLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>As stated last week the Viaduct Works will close from Saturday next until Tuesday, January 2nd. The Vulcan Foundry, Messrs. T. and T. Vicars and Messrs. Slee and Co. having in view ths agitation for a week's holiday in the Summer, will only close on Christmas Day,Boxing Day,and New Year's Day, which the men recognise as being quite sufficient at this time of the year.</p>
        <p>THE CHRISTMAS DRCORAI /ONS AT EARLRSTOWN. The tradesmen of Earlestown and Newton have during the week been busily engaged decorating their shop windows for the Christmas festival, and somewhat the same lines are followed as on pre- vious occasions, every art and device being resorted to,to attract probable purchasers. The butchers, as before, are to the front with their shows of prize cattle and sheep from local farms, whilst several of them make a brave show of fine pqrkers with mem- bers of the feathered tribe,in the shape of geese and turkeys. The grocers have stored their windows with all manner of good things suitable to the occasion, and judging from their abundance they expect to do a good stroke of business. The drapers have dons their best to attract the eye by ingeni- ously decorating their windows with articles suit- able as seasonable Rifts, whilst a fesv have catered for the youngsters by a large display of toys, and dolls. Altogether there does not appear to be any falling off in variety or quantity and it is evident a good harvest is expected.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>PROPERTY SALE AT EARLESTOWN.</head>
        <p>PROPERTY SALE AT EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>At the Wellington Rote!, Elrlestown, on Tuesday evening before a fair gathering there were brought under the hammer four lots of valuable shop pro- perty. The first lot consisted of a shop and dwell- ing-house No 76 Market-street, corner of Peel- street, with the two-stalled stable and coach house belonging thereto, in the occupation of Mr, Edward Stayt. This lot was announced as having been se!d privately and was therefore not offered by auction. —Lot 2, was the shop and dwelling-house No. 78, Market-street. Earlestown in the occupation of Mr. William Derbyshire. This was started at £3OO, hut was withdrawn at £4OO, the reserve not having been reached. Lt 3 and 4. comprised respectively shop and dwelling-house, No. 80 and 82, Market- street and in the occupation of Mr. Ralph Smith and Mr. James Gavne. These last lots were not offered for sale. A nwel feature introiuced by the auctioneers was the illustration of the property thrown on a sheet by'means of the magic lantern.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>WARGRA.VE CHURCH EARLESTOWII.</head>
        <p>WARGRA.VE CHURCH EARLESTOWII.</p>
        <p>On Sunday evening after the usual service at Wargrave Church, the choir gave several selections from Handel's Oritorio "The Messiah." The fol- lowing were the items submitted to a large congre- gation ;—Overture (organ), Mr. E Leyland; recit, " Clmfort ye," Mr. William Carson; chorus " And the Glory," (Choir) ; recit and air, "Thus saith the Lord," and "But who may abide," Mr. S Drinz recit "Behold," air and chorus "0 thou that tellest" Miss ANI olyneux and choir; recit, "For behold, darkness," and air' The people that walkel," Mr. S Dring ; chorus" For unto us," choir; Pastoral sym- phony (organ), Mr. E Leyland, recite " There were Shepherds,"" And the angel said," "And suddenly there was with the angels," Miss Sheperr; ; chorus " Glory to God," choir; chorus, " Behold the Lamb of God," choir ; air, "He was depised," Miss A Molyneux ; chorus," Hallelujah "choir. A collec- tion was afterwards male in aid of the Local fund for the relief of the sufferers by the war, £6 .ss, being realised.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>PRESENTATION TO POLICE-SERGEANT LINGS.</head>
        <p>PRESENTATION TO POLICE-SERGEANT LINGS.</p>
        <p>The foarth annual re-union and presentation of certificates and medallions, together with a hand- some silver-mounted umbrella and gold medallion to P.S. Lings (of Earlestown), for his services to the Askam section, of the First Aid and Nursing Class, took place in the Askam Church Schools on Wed- nesday night.—Mr H Mellor presided and there was a large and representative audience.— Dr. Pooley said he had a most interesting duty to perform. He was very sorry he had come late, but he had to depend on the Furness Railway— (laughter)—as they had a peculiar way of running their trains. He spoke in high praise of Sergeant and Mrs. Lings and the services they had rendered to the ambulance cause in Askam, in fact,tbe doctor said, be did not, know how he himself would have done had it not been for Mr Lings. He then caked Mr. Lings to accept the gold medal- lion and he silver-mounted umbrella as a token of the very highest esteem,and as expressing their very best wishes for his future happiness and success The medallion bears the inscription " Prosent-d to Sergeant J. binge by the members of the Askam First Aid an'd Nursing Class, December 1899." Police-Sergeant Lingo, who was greeted with pro- longed applause, said this was one of the happiest moments of his life It had given him great pleasure to come from South Lancashire amongst them once more. (Applause.) He said that he found it difficult to express his feelings ts them that night for their kindness. Re differed with Dr. Pooley regarding the instruction of the class. It was Dr. Pooley alone who deserved the praise. (Applause.) For that expression of their goodwill he again tendered his best thanks, and any work which he had done for their benefit he was amply repaid for by their kindness (Loud cheers.)— Votes of thanks terminated the proceedings,which were interspersed with music.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>FIRE AT EA.RLESTOWN.</head>
        <p>FIRE AT EA.RLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>On Surday evening.as people were wending their way to church they were startled by an alarm of fire. P.C. Benson who was patrolling Haydock-st. at 550 said he smelled something burning and on making an inspection found it proceeded from the outbuilding of the 11,9 W premises in course of con- struction for Dr. Valentine in flaydock-street. He saw fire proceeding from a loft attached to the new stables, and ovar the coachman's quarters, which contained about a ton of straw, and which was fourd to be on fire. In the stables were two horses, and the coachman's room was furnished with a bed and furniture, and in another room was a quantity of furniture which was ready for removal into the new berate close by. The doors of the building were all locifed up, and there was no one about. Benson blew his whistle, and with the assistance of a man, wed a ladder as a battering ram to force the (,loors. He got the horses out, and assisted to remove the furniture, and then ran to the Gas Works for the fire extinguishing appliances, which he brought back with him, By this time Insoertor Morgan, Sergeant Banks, P.O 's Wooff and McHale had arrived on the scene and coupled the pipes to the hydrants in the street mains, and there being a 'good force of water they played on the fire which was confined to the loft containing the straw. About 10 o'clock the fire was put out, a portion of the Viaduct Works brigade with hose having in the meantime arrived at the fire, and rendered assist- ance. The cause of the outbreak is supposed to be spontaneous combustion brought about by the draught of the fire place in the harness room be- low going through the room where the straw was stored. The place was seen safe at 4 o'clock in the afternoon by the coachman Thomas Beard. Some of the furniture was"dameged during the process of removal. The damage is estimated at .E6o,which is covered by insurance in the Scottish Union Fire Insurance Company.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>SCENE AT Sr. HELENS RAILWAY STATION</p>
        <p>At St. Helens, on Tuesday, Michael R tfferty, of Ash- tom, was charged with asiaulting railway detective Davies.—The officer stated that about eight o'clock the previous inlet he fund prisoner drunir in the railway siation. He was using very bad language. He told him to go off the premises, but he refuaed to do so. He attenanted to remove prisoner from the station, when he kicked him on the legs and struck him violently in the chest. Pritioner's conduct was very bad indeed.—Fined 403 and costs, or a month.</p>
        <p>LINSEED COMPOUND' for Coughs and Colds, Asthma and Bronohi‘is. Of Citemis ..s oaly.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>WAR.</head>
        <p>THE WAR.</p>
        <p>LATEST TELEGRAMS.</p>
        <p>EIGHT THOUSAND YEOMNARY WANTED.</p>
        <p>Important Statements.</p>
        <p>We are in a position, sAys the "Daily News," to make two important statements concerning the new force of Imperial Yeomanry to be sent to South Africa. The first is that our military chiefs at the seat of war want it to mustar, if possible, 8,000 men. No indication of this fact appears in the official statement published yes- terday. No number is mentioned, but it may b 3 taken as settled that if 8,000 good men and true, fulfilling the conditions, prefent themselves they will sooner or later be steaming to the Cape.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>GRAVITY OF THE SITUATION.</head>
        <p>GRAVITY OF THE SITUATION.</p>
        <p>The Umbria "Ordered Out."</p>
        <p>The extreme desire of the War Office authorities to hurry out the reinfortements may be gathered from th 3 fact that the Cunard steamer Umbria, engaged to sail from the Mersey for New York on Saturday with the mails, has been chartered, and her Saturday fixture can- celled. The suspension of the Saturday mail between Liverpopl and New York is a thing uoh3ard of in th, steamship annals of the port.</p>
        <p>BATTLE OF MAGERSFON- TEIN,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>Deadly Effects of the Lyddite.</head>
        <p>Deadly Effects of the Lyddite.</p>
        <p>The " Standard " correspoodent with Llrd Methuen' column says :—The Bier commandant puts his casual- ties an Monday at a few hundreds only, but other evi- dence points to a far heavier lois. One eye witness states that he found numbers of the enemy standing dead but upright in the trenches with their rifles in their hands. They had been killed, not by projectiles, but by the mere shock of the explosion of our lyddite shells.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>ST. HELENS NURSE GOING TO THE FRONT. Chosen by the Princess of Wales.</head>
        <p>A ST. HELENS NURSE GOING TO THE FRONT. Chosen by the Princess of Wales.</p>
        <p>Mies Clara Evans, daughter of the late Mr. W. Evans, of Prescot, and sister of Mrs. Joseph Massey, of St. Helens, is one of the six nurses chosen from the staff ot the London Hospital by the Princess of Wales to pro- ceed to South Atrice on Saturday. Miss Evans has for the past three and a half years been at the London HoNpital, and has had a good deal of experience in the men's surgical ward. she is to have an interview with the Princess of Wales before leaving Ei4land. The nurses will probably travel in the Duuottar Castle, by which Lord Roberts sails on Saturday. The Mayor of St. Heleos (Councillor Beecham) has sent Miss Evans a letter of congratulatioa.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>WARRINGTON POLICEMEN AT THE FRONT.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON POLICEMEN AT THE FRONT.</p>
        <p>Interesting Letters,</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable of Warrington (Mr. Luke Talbot) has received the follsowing interesting letter from P.C. 8. Howard, a reservist who is serving with Lord Methuen's column in South Africa :—"26 November, 1899. 5412 Private S. Howard. 3rd Grenadier Guards,South African fi .1d forces. To the Chief Constable of Warrington. Dear sir,—l received my reserve papers a few days ago when we were on the borders of the Orange River. I should have given them in in the Orderly Room in the Barracks, but it does not matter now. I will send them back to yen in Case I lose them. We had a great battle last Wednesday morning at Belmont. You will have read about it in the papers. We were the first to charge the hills and start the battle. I have lost my company offi t!e• and another officer is wounded in my coenpany. The adjutant is killed too, and the commanding officer is wounded in to plains. I cannot express how sorry I feel fer my poor chums who are killed and wounded. The Boers are giving us "socka" and we are giving them the same. You will please excuse the writing as lam writing this on my knee sitting on the grass. We marched off about 1 a.rn. on Wednesday. Our battalion formed in single rank, about four peces distant from each other BO that we should not make a good target for the Boers. When we got about a hundred yards from the bottom of those hills, which are covered with great stones, tree day was just breaking, and the Boers jumped up all of a sudden and opened a terrine fire on us. Many of my poor comrades fell. We fixed bayonets and charged up the hill, and when we were near the top,the Boer's started running. I thought my time had come, but by God's help I man- aged to escape. The papers are true enough. They could not exaggerate it a bit. If we go on like we are going on now, losing such a lot of men there will be leaf of us killed. There was a chum that was firing next ti me got a shot right through his helmet. He said to me " Look at this. That's a good shot. I hope it does not get any nearer than that," but teem he had time to put his helmet on aeain he dropped with a bullet through his heart, it we could only get them out in the open we would teach them a lesson. They hide their dead and wounded away from us se that we may not know what damage we have done them. We gained a victory at Belmont. We had another battle yesterday in another place. We have chased them te the Medder River wh :re they have taken up another position. It Hurst and I get through this war we will be very lucky, but I thiek it is a caee with all of us. I woeld like to say more only we have not a minute to ourselves. Hurst and I send you our best resp.lcts and wishes. I wish you a Merry Cnrist- mas and a happy New )(ear, and remain your faithful servant, SAMUEL HOWARD P.S. Excuse the dirty paper.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>" Bullets Flying Round Like Peas."</head>
        <p>" Bullets Flying Round Like Peas."</p>
        <p>A friend of Private Howard, who lives in Mill-street, his received a letter frpm him, in which he describes the battle of Belmont. "We started out to meet the enemy," he writes 'at about true a.m., in the morning. It was a moonlight night. We went to the place where the enemy was in the mountains. Not a word was spoken by our troops. The night was very light. We were in a line about three paces from each other. We got about a hundred yards from the bottom of the hills. This was only my battalion. We saw the Boers getting up. As soon as they saw us they opened fire on us. We fixed bayonets and charged up the bill. It is a funny feeling. I nearly went mad. As we ran many of my chums fell. We got to the top, what was left of us, and scot the Boers down. lam a very lucky man. The shots were flying around my head like peas." He des- cribes the death of several of his companions, much in the same manner as tre loiter given ahoy-, and eludes by expressing the hope that he may be permitted to return to Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>Hardships of Methuen's Column.—" Bad</head>
        <p>The Hardships of Methuen's Column.—" Bad</p>
        <p>Water and Food."</p>
        <p>P.C. Peter Howard, of the Warrington Borough Police Force, has received the following letter from P.C. Hurst, a member of the force who is serving with the 3rd Grenadier Guards under Lord Methuen Monday, November 20 h. 1899. My dear friend,—Just a few lines to let you know I am in the best of health, and alive and kicking, hoping you and your wife are the same. Please give my best respeets to all men on the force. We had a very good voyage out here, but it was very rough the last few days. We are about 600 miles up the country, on the Orenge River, about 70 miles from De Aar, and we are still going up the country. We shall have a' ge ' at the Boers to-morrow or neat day, the sooner the le.tter. They are only eight miles from us. We keep fetching them in prisoners, two or three a day, so I think we shall have to fight for it. It is not going to be an easy job for us, but if I come back I will tel you all about it. We have to march to Pretoria, 500 milea further, and there are Boars to fieht all the way, so we shall lose a few thousand men before it is all over. If we don't get shot, they will kill us with marchine, and the bad water and the foo I we get. We have t ) wash in the water we drink, which you could cut with a knife. when we do have one. We are not shaving, and we look some of the funniest things von ever saw, and as black as ceal. There are men acing to hospial every day with feydr, and hurting themselves marching over the rocks. We have done the Boers a bad turn, for the officers are the same as the men, same kit and a rifle, wit'a a b Lyme, for they have been killing the officers, but they cannot tell one from another now. Howard is here. He has come up with Us. There are a lot left at Capetown to look after our food. I don't know when we shall be back, if we have the luck to get back, but if we do we shall be about April. I don't know when you will get this letter, but I wish you a merry Christmas, and we shall have done a lot of fighting by then. All at present from your old friend. As happy as a king. Hoping you will think of me, your old Chum Jack 33). My best wishes to all—Private (3341) Jorna HURST, 3rd Battalion Greqadier Guards, South African Field Force, Saab Africa.".</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>TO CARE FOR THE WOUNDED.</head>
        <p>TO CARE FOR THE WOUNDED.</p>
        <p>Another Warrington Ambu- lance Man for the Cape.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Corps of the St. John Ambulance Brigade is to have the honour of eupplsing a third man for service in tending the wounded in South Africa. Last week Privates H. Holloway and J. E. Newnsomem- hers of the corps, departed from Liverpool for the seat of .war on the s.a. " Majestic," and on Tuesday night, a thud member, Private J W Malkin, left Warrington for London. where he will be equipped and drilled and afterwards despatched to the Cape. Private Malkin is a carter, its the employ of Messrs Thompson, McKay, and Company. By way of a hearty "send off "a smoking concert was held at the " Pel:cen " fin, on Tuesday, when several interesting presentation. took place. Cap- tain Waring, the chief superintendent, presided and was surmorted by Dr Adams and others. After the toast of "The Queen" had been honoured, Captain WARING read the following letter which he had received from Privates Holloway and Newns Joha's Gate, Clerkenwell, E.C. Dear Captain,—We beg to enclose you P.O.'s for our fares, which you so kindly bought the tickets for us. We thank von very much for the grand send-off you gave us, and the Brigade chief, Church Brszier, seemed pleased when we told him all the ether fellows were in on Monday morning. We have been drilling every day this week, and we have smartened up considerably. We were inspected at the Duke of Portland's place yesterday, and you will find an account of it and a picture of us in to-day's " Deily , Graphic," page 13. We were taken to the Alhambra in Khaki uniform, and received a very warm receptions "The Absent-Minded Beggar" and "The Soldiers of the Queen" being sung, and then we were given three cheers by the audience. We are sailing from Liverpool on Wednesday, per s.s. "Majestic." We leave Easton by midnight, arrive Lime-street and breakfast, and then go on board by 10 a.m. There are 25 of es in this detachment, and we are the staff of the Portland field hospital. My regiment number is 86, and Newne is number 85. We have been told by the Brigade chief that he has had orders for 45 more men, so you may get a call for someone else, and you can tell anyone who comes down that they will have to smarten themselves ' up considerably, as they are not just, ambulance men— they are soldiers, and they will have to buck up. Con- vey our most hearty thanks to the officers and all our fellow brigade men. We remain, yours truly, Private H.BOLLOWAY (86), Private Jeerer E. Newers (85). Capt sin Waring said that he together with Deputy Commissioner Dr. Trimble and Mr. G. R. Crosfield went on board the vessel before its departure and saw Privates Holoway and Nswns. They were in very good spirits and desired to send their kind remembrances to their comrades. He was sure they would do their duty while they were in South Africa. (Applause.) After teasts had been honoured, Captain WARING then handed to Private Malkin, £1 the gift of of Mr. J. Charlton Parr, and cakes of tobacco from Mr. Harry Toft and Mr. Chalmers. At this point in the proceedings, the Mayor entered the r enn, and was heartily cheered on taking his seat. Captain WARING said he was proud to see the Mayor nrescnt ttat night to wish "God Speed" to Privet Malkin. He would ask him to say a few words and make a presentation to him on behalf of Malkin's fellow workmen. The MAYOR, who was warmly received, said that he snust, first of all, thank Captain Waring for having sent him word that the meeting was going to be held as it would have been a matter of great regret to him,if hav- ing been Present on a previous occasion, be had missed the opportunity of saying a few words of good cheer to Privet@ Malkin. He would at once, on behalf of his fel- low werkmsn, and he considered it an honour to do so, ask Private Malkin to accept a dressing case with their very gocd wishes, and also a box of cigarettes from Mr. Perris. They had met that night under exceptionally depressing circumstances. Our army had met with reverses which were not anticipated and a great many hearts in England were sad to-day as a result of the reverses. Our national pride had been touched, and our national spirit had been humiliated to a certain extent, but he had no sympathy at all with a section of the Press which had done everything which it possibly could to expand to the fullest extent, the weaknesses which had prevailed and which had led up to the misfortunes that had occurred. (Hear. hear.) When he saw head lines, half-an-inch deep, "Boiler's defeat" and " Buller super- seded "he felt disgusted and indignant to think that any newspaper should allow itself to play into the hands of the enemy by making a public exhibition of the mis- fortunes which had befallen us and which were mot quite BO serious as that section of the Press made out. But a.; sure as they were in that room, unless some appalling misfortune befel us, all the difficulties would he overcome and the result of the war would be a glorious victory on the part of our army in South Africa. (Loud applause) He should like to say a word or two to their friend who was going out to join the two other young fellows who had left Warrington, and whose going out reflected an honour on the town and brigade. Tony all felt gratified 'that they had had the opportunity—and men who were willing to take advantage of tne opoortunry—ef send- ing three men to do the blessed work of mercy amenest the wounded soldiers in South Africa. Tilers was no room for two opinions as to the character of the work in which their friend was going to take an honourable and worthy part. The work would not be without difficulty, risk and anxiety but he (the Mayor), hoped and trusted,—and he did not think it was an improper thing to say they should pray for their three friends-- that they might return again sound in lvalth and limb, wiser men, and with one of the grandee' satisfactions possible, that of knowing that they had done something in the hour of its danger and trouble, to render loyal service to the country and the dear old Queen who ruled above them all. (Loud applause.) During the evening further toasts were gone through and songs and recitations were creditably rendered by Messrs T Ilealing,Saunders, Freeman, Barnwell,Maszee. Pemberton, and Knowles. The recitation " How 811 Adams won the battle of Waterloo "by Mr Saunders created much amusement. Private Malkin left the Central Station by the 11 53 train amid great enthusiasm.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>LOCAL HELP FOR THE SOUTH LANCASHIRES.</head>
        <p>LOCAL HELP FOR THE SOUTH LANCASHIRES.</p>
        <p>Meeting of Ladies at the Town Hall.</p>
        <p>eitThe appeal which was issued in the" Examiner "last ISaturday for various articles of clothing for the men connected with the South Lancashire Regiment has been most generously responded to. The scheme is to send out woellen garments, more especially good useful socks, to the men who are now on their way to do their duty for Englana'e sake in South Africa. Geed tr.out socks, not too thick, made of undyed wool (for colours may run and hurt blis3ered feet), wonld be the most acceptable things to send. Jerseys, tee, or woallen caps fitting close round the head, coming down to the neck at the back and over ears, to fasten round the throat with a leatner string, are found to be very comfortable by troops sleeping on the grass in the wet. In a day or two now the South Lancashires will be facing the enemy, ready, as we are all sure, to do their duty to their country. Tea rough wear and tear of a campaign will soon make such things as socks of the highest value,and Mrs O'Leary is anxious to send off her consignment, from all sources of collection, about the Isecond week in January, so that all contributions should be forwarded without delay. The ladies connected with ' the regiment who have undertaken to receive t ontribu- Urine in kind, or money to b e turned into kied te the best advantage, are : Mrs. W. MacCarthy O'Leary, 10, Moor Park-avenue, Preston; Mrs. Adam, Orford Barracks, Warringtoo ; Mrs. 03.keley, Brine Baths Hotel, Nantwicb; Mrs. Northern, Bewsey Cottage, Warrington; Mrs. ROOM°, c.o. Major Roonae, Drill Hall, Warring- ton. A meeting was hold to further the scheme on Tuesday afternoon at the Town Hall. the Mayor (Alderman Roberts) presiding. There was a largo attendance of ladies, most of whom subscribed freelyt and undertook, in addition, to impress upon those net in a position to give large amounts the value of smaller contributions. A shilling will buy a pair of suitable socks and single pairs or the equivalent in money will be as welcome as the larger consignments or remittances. Major Adam, Major Roome, Captain Edelsten, and the Town Clerk were amongst those present. The MAYOR said he must apologise for giving such very short notice of the meeting, which was owing to circurnstanees wlich could not be controlled, because the auhject was only brought under his notice the pre- vious day, but the response was an extremely good one. and he was mnch obliged to them for attending. He read letters of apology for non-attendance from Mrs JC Parr, Mrs Willis, Mrs McGregor. Mrs Owen (Latchford), and Mrs Tarbuck. Continuing, ha sail the ladies pre- sent would no dsubt have seen the letter from Mrs Rooms salt to one of the newspapers on Saturday last sneaking of the object for which the meeting was called. Be uaderrood that it was the South Lancashire Regi- ment which was more especially the object of their in- terest that afternoon, and it was considered desirable, if possible, to send out garments for the soldiers on the first week in Januery „at least 1 000 pa'r, of socks, 1,000 bed covers, 1,000 jerseys and waistcoats of that class—all woollen earments. It was practically impossible for these things to be made in that short time,and it would, therefore, be better for the ladies present to make them- selves responsible, if they approved of the object of the meeting, for a certain number of garments or money. They would remember that the old proverb said" He who gives quickly gives twice," and it was especially applicable in the present circumstances. They knew how necessary it was that their men in South Africa, who were doing such magnificent work, in spite of all ,failurc2 up to tiny—fail:l:es which, he hoped, would not</p>
        <p>be reneated—should be assisted in every way. (Hear,</p>
        <p>M-ijor ADAM addvFsed the meeting, and on behalf of the South Lancashire Regiment oxpressed his thanks to the ladies for th-ir kindue3s in attending on that occa- sion to di :cuss the very pressing subject of supplying his own regiment and the reginaent to which Major Rome belonged wirh the necessary clothing for field service. He might be allowed to say with a certain amount of pride, pardonable pride, that it had a record second to none. At one time its recruits were drawn from various parts, but now they came principally from Warrington and Sc. Helens, and they were, therefore, all more or less interested in the composition of the regiment, and the people of Warrington were the proper people to take up the question which was new being discussed. The soldier, as Kipling had told them, was an " absent-minded beggar" not only with regard to his wife and children but with regard to him- self, and on field service he was no less absent-minded ; but they would see froaa what he had done, in spite of fortune's hard knocks, that he was a very tough man when the opportunity came, and he was sure that the ladifs present would like to help him as much as possible. It was proposed to send out socks, cardigan jackets, flannel shirts, Balaclava caps, Stc., and other articles likely to b 3 most acceptable.</p>
        <p>_ Dtain EDLESTEN priduced a small parcel containing a Balaclava cap, which he explained had been made by Mrs. Eilesten,and it was handed round for the inspection of Vie ladies. It was better that the articles should be of th -3 khaki colour. Some information was given as to the price of Socks, caps, &amp;c., and he added that full in- structions for making the Balaclava cap would be found in the second part of Mrs. Weldon's knitting series. On the motion of the MAYOR, seconded by Mr i CARLE- TON, it was resolved " that the ladies present are of opinion that all help pos:ible should be afforded in the direction indicated by Major Adam and the ladies who have convened the meeting." Cards were handed round upon which the ladies were asked to indicate their promises, either money or kind, and the result was most gratifying; no less a sum than £2B se. 61. being promised. It was arranged to hold another meeting to report progress on Friday next. In the rneantime,the Mayor exolained,parcels could be sent to the Town Hall addreisei to the Mayor, and the Town Clerk had kindly promised to forward them to one of the three ladies wh3 had convened the meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>ENGLISH CHURCH UNION.</head>
        <p>ENGLISH CHURCH UNION.</p>
        <p>Mr. John White on the "Crisis."</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the Warrington Branch of the English Church Union was held on Monday evening in St. Paul's Infant School, Kendrick-street, the chair be- ing taken by the Rev. Wilfred Stanton. There was a small attendance. Amongst those present were—the Rector (the Rev Canon Willis), and the Revs. W R Hart- wright, A S Cameron, A F White, and Messrs. H N Eloughton (branch secre' ary), C S Burgess, William Johnson, James Bolton (treasurer),John Hankinson,and the Rev John Wakefi??d, M.A., vicar of St. Margaret's, Liverpool.—Mr. Bolton made the annual statement as to the financial position of the branch. He stated that all subscrip,ions received in Warrington had been sent to the head office in London. With regard to the branch expens-s,he commenced the year with 9l.in hand,and he had now 12s. in hand. There were outstanding accounts amounting to £4. —Mr.John White wrote stating that as the winter evening meetings never seemed to succeed very well, he should like to try a new departure, and invite all the members of the branch, and as many others as possible who in any way sympathized with its work, to Moore Hall, in May or June next. They could put up a large tent, and secure one or two good speakers, and, perhaps, get together a really good gathering.—This suggestion was warmly approved.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>MR._ JOHN WHITE'S VIEWS.</head>
        <p>MR._ JOHN WHITE'S VIEWS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Johnsoa read a letter trom Mr. White, in which he said ,—" Not .more than a year or two ago, S 3 peace- ful was everything around us, we were beginning to think that the age of persecution had really passed away, and that an era was arriving when such an organisation as the union would be no longer necessary. We have had a Somewhat rude awakening, and from the threatening° that are breathed forth there seems to be axother time of im- prisonment and deprivation in store for some of our most zeelous clergy. This is a matter which, however. does not alarm us much, as it always results in the ulti- mate triumph of the Church. (Applause.) But we are face to face with a greater difficulty than this or any that we have yet met with, and that is the recently pro- muleated " opinion " of the two Archbishops, about which you will hear more fully presently. My reason for alluaing to it is only this, that the action of Lord Halifax, our beloved president, has apparently placed a strain upon some of the members of the union that is more than they can bear. Several have left the union— not many, all told—and two or three have left our own branch. As against this, the addition of new members during the past twelve months has reached the unprecedented number of 10.000. I must say, however, I regret the loss ef the few scores that eave turned their hands back from the plough, much mereathan I rejeice over the 10,000 who have joined our ranks. What should we have thought of our soldiers in Natal if they bad turned back at Modder River, or any other of the battle places, and said that they di,Prit know the enemy's guns were so big,and that they could not trust their leaders. Well, I for one have the utmost faith and confidence in Lord Halifax, the noblest layman of the end of the 19th century, who has made unknown sacrifices for the great cause of the Catholic Faith, and under whose banner I hope, please God, to fight to the end of my life. It is very hard for us as a society one of whose main objects is to maintain the discipline of the Ceurch, and whose great desire is to respec:. and honour those whom God bath set over us, to find ourselves so often in cenflict with the bishops, but supposing the union had given in 30 years ago,where woald the Church Revival have been to-day? The bulk of the things that Mr. Green went to prison for have since been admitted to be quite lawful and right. In the present instance we object to the" opinion "not simply because we believe it to be wrong,and that it deprives us of practices common to the whole of Christ's Catholic Church but because it savours too much of surrender to mob pressure ; also because if the rusty old Act of 300 years ago is to be put in force at all, it ought to be put in force all round, and not agairst one section of the Church only. But the principal objection is because the "opinion "is avowedly base." upon an Act of Parlia- ment which is the authority we have been fighting against from the first. All these years we have been struggling for the right of the Church to manage her own affairs, and to submit to an " opinion " now founded on an Act of Parliament is at once to give up all we have been flehting for. No one will accuse our friend the Rev E C E Carleton of being a Ritualist, but be has plainly shown in a recent pamphlet that the Act of Uniformity was practically repealed by the passing of the Toleration Act ot 1689. What I want you to understaed is that we are not fighting simply for the use of incense and processional lights, things in them- selves ot no actual importance, though dear to many whe for years have le?en accustomed to them, but,as has always been the Case, we fight for the greater question that lies behinci,:nd for the freedom of the Church. Now, in the maj aity of churches affected by the Archbishops' opinion the clergy, supported by their people, have, under protest, acceded to the request of their Bishop to keep within the lines laid down in that opinion. But wane few, from conscientious motives, have intimated that they are unable to do so. Lord Halifax says "Stand by your priests." Is the union to stand by these men or to let them be deprived of their benefices, and probably cast into prison ? This is the question that will soon have to be settled, and you will have to make up your minds whether you will stand by the union in this, up to now the most difficult moment of its existence,or 'whether, feeling that your heart fails you and you have not the courage to fight on, you decide to take off your armour and leave the battle to others." (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>ELECTION OF OFFICERS.</head>
        <p>ELECTION OF OFFICERS.</p>
        <p>On the motion ei the CHAIRMAN, seconded by Mr. HouonToN, Mr. John White was re-elected Chairman, and the Rev. W. Stanton vice-chairman, the mover ant seco, der in this case beim; Mr. BURGESS and Mr. JAMES BOLTN rspective!y. Messrs. Houghton and Burgess were app-hnted secretaries, and the Revs. C. Wrav, W. R. Hartvvright, and Messrs John Elankinsm and Herbert Wolds the committee.</p>
        <p>The JOTIN WAKEFORD then delivered an adlress on the Church. Cri,is.</p>
        <p>The RECTOR propcsed a vote of thanks to the Lecturer and rxpr,ssed his immense respect for Lord Halifax. He would not say whether he was always right or not, but he entertained deep re4pect for him, and felt regret that any should have left on account of his action. Ho should als) like to expreis his sense of what they owed to Dr. Ptix.y. Toe CHAIRMAN seconded and the proposition was carried.</p>
        <p>"The Pickpocket."</p>
        <p>The Royal Court Theatre was well filled on Satur- day evening, when the members of the Warrington Dramatic Society gave an extra performance of " The Pickpocket,' the proceeds being in aid of the Mayor's fund for the relief of the wives and chil- dren of the Reservists. During an interval Miss Maggie Pierpoint recited `• The Absent-minded Beggar," and was vociferously applauded. Her elocution was exceedingly goad, and she merited fully the warm enconiums she received. Shortly afterwards a collection was taken, which Mr Browne announced, realized £6 3s.</p>
